President as stated in the main reply the Civil Engineering and Development Department CEDD is now conducting a study on the transport infrastructure network and visitor receiving capability of Lantau . The study will examine from a macro perspective the transport connectivity and needs of Lantau including Mui Wo Tung Chung and Tai O as well as the existing road enhancement proposals which can be further explored . The findings of the study and recommendations will be submitted to the Legislative Council as a joint review of the relevant proposals
President the Civil Engineering and Development Department CEDD completed the technical study on reclamation at Lung Kwu Tan in 2017 and established the technical feasibility of the reclamation project . After fully considering the geographical location transport capacity environment and various restrictions affecting the residents of the district it proposed that the development be based on suitable industrial uses
The technical study also shows that the development of reclamation will not bring unsolvable technical and environmental problems
Although the reclamation area is focused on industrial development the industries concerned are not highly polluting . In the course of development we will conduct detailed environmental impact assessment which is a statutory impact assessment . We will only take forward the scheme when the relevant statutory requirements are met
Therefore the impact on villagers should be acceptable
In fact when it comes to nearby reclamation sites we have already looked at different sites across the territory in the Land Enhancement Strategy 2011 and we have chosen Lung Kwu Tan reclamation as one of the sites
In 2017 we conducted another consultation on the Task Force on Land Supply and considered whether the site was suitable for reclamation . At that time we brought up the site for public consultation . After considering various factors we considered that Lung Kwu Tan reclamation was worth taking forward
We understand their concerns and hope to respond to them in the study on planning and works
President we will conduct various technical assessments in the proposed planning and engineering study . Water supply is of course one of the requirements and sufficient water supply will definitely be required before the scheme can be implemented . Mr SHEK please rest assured
As regards whether there are any water supply problems in the village at present if there are such cases I welcome Mr SHEKs proposal and we can discuss with the Water Supplies Department to see what the actual problems are
President I thank Members for raising this question
The scheme is actually a preparation scheme for the so called ecological coastline . It is to see if the ecological recovery of different facilities on the ecological coastline will be greatly helpful
Given the shortage of land resources apart from the policy support provided by the relevant bureaux the Government generally does not provide the Government or new development sites for redevelopment by affected operators
Nevertheless if an affected operator seizes private land on his own to restart his business the Government will provide consultation services and facilitation measures from the perspective of planning and land administration
As mentioned by Mr HO just now there are currently 4 300 hectares of agricultural land in Hong Kong of which 740 hectares are permanent agricultural land . Therefore there is still a lot of vacant agricultural land which can be used for agricultural purposes including the construction of agricultural farms
As I have said just now if there is a new policy to develop agricultural land which is more advanced in technology the Development Bureau will definitely provide assistance including identifying sites or providing consultation services and facilitation measures from the administrative perspective of planning and land development . But the premise is that the Government must establish whether there is such a policy
Deputy President I thank Mr TSE for his supplementary question
President the consultation and facilitation measures I mentioned earlier on providing a planning and land administration perspective mainly target at the relocation of the affected agricultural lands
If there is such a policy we will try to identify sites or reserve these sites in new development areas so as to make planning and land administration more convenient
President I thank Dr CHENG for his supplementary question and views
The supervision of private buildings in Hong Kong is actually based on the Buildings Ordinance
Under the Ordinance any authorized person registered structural engineer and registered rock and pile engineer appointed to a construction project has his or her respective duties including the supervision of the construction works code enacted by the Buildings Department BD in accordance with the Standards and Guidances for the Construction Works of the Construction Works of the Construction Works of the Construction Works of the Construction Works in accordance with the Standards and Guidances for the Construction Works of the Construction Works issued by the Buildings Department in 2009 2009 and the regular supervision of the construction works of the construction works of the Construction Works of the Construction Works of the Construction Works in accordance with the Standards and Guidances for the Construction Works of the Construction Works issued by the Government of Hong Kong
Registered contractors should also monitor the works in accordance with the requirements of the monitoring scheme
In respect of structural works involving steel bars for concrete construction BD will in the course of approving the design of development projects apply the conditions on the eligibility of a qualified contractor for the works in accordance with the Buildings Ordinance and require the registered structural engineers and registered contractors responsible for the development projects to assign experienced and competent personnel to provide a qualified site contractor for steel bars for concrete works so as to enable the construction works to be carried out in accordance with the relevant requirements
The incident shows that the regulatory mechanism under the Buildings Ordinance BORO which is the requirement for building tests to be imposed by the Buildings Department BD upon approval of plans and the functions and functions of the relevant registered professionals and registered contractors as stipulated in the law can effectively monitor the quality of site works
The Buildings Department is now investigating whether the incident has violated the Buildings Ordinance . After identifying the cause of the incident we will review the mechanism and requirements as required in the light of the findings
I have some new information for the President to consider
President on 16 November 2020 the Government launched the eMPF Mobile App to provide convenient digital tools to help members of the public to record their time travelling to and from different places and taking taxis on their own
If the user is unfortunately diagnosed the travel records stored in the application can also be used to assist the Centre for Health Protection of the Department of Health DH in conducting a survey on epidemiology cases and tracking of other close contacts
After consulting the Food and Health Bureau my reply to the three parts of the question is as follows 1 So far more than 67 000 public and private venues have participated in the Scheme with the address given on the screen which includes about 54 000 private venues
Subsequently the Government also instructed from 10 December that apart from clubhouses hotels and guesthouses all listed premises under Cap . 599F must cease operation and continued to give directions to clubhouses hotels and guesthouse catering businesses to require the aforesaid premises to display the specified size of the QR code at the entrance or at the entrance of the premises within two working days after receiving the relevant QR code
The statutory requirement that the responsible person in charge of a business and the manager of the premises listed on the Form 599F are in breach of which is a criminal offence and is liable to a maximum fine of 50,000 and imprisonment for six months
At present catering businesses and other listed premises in the catering industry are still required to display the QR code of Rehabilitation Cases in their offices
Various law enforcement departments have been deploying more (man power ) to inspect various districts in Hong Kong to ensure that the operators and practitioners of the premises concerned strictly comply with the requirements in Cap . 599F and meet the relevant requirements and restrictions including displaying the eMPF cards properly
During the period from 2 December 2020 to 4 January 2021 the Law Enforcement Department has inspected over 87 000 premises under the regulation of Cap
2 The latest number of downloads in the EFLs has exceeded 430 000
Since the launch of the Scheme the number of private venues participating in the QR code has increased from some 1 000 at the beginning to about 54 000 at present
We have also kept enhancing the software in response to the views of members of the public and the industry including updating the restrictions on the applications required adding new functions such as checking personal travel records leaving the application automatically and simplifying the replacement of locations so as to facilitate the use by members of the public
We will continue to study different technologies and proposals to enhance the user experience and invite different sectors to put QR codes and publicity posters in their premises to encourage the public to use them
In addition we will continue to promote and educate the public through television advertisements and publicity videos on the use of the eMPF SafeTransfer Programme . We will also urge the public to make good use of technology to record their travel experiences and work together to fight the epidemic
3 The Governments design of the mobile application of Anshin Outposts aims to facilitate the public in better and more effectively and accurately recording the dates and hours when they enter and leave different venues under the new normal of fighting the epidemic so as to develop the habit of recording the time when they leave the venue to help reduce the further risk of spread of the virus and enhance their confidence in going out to different venues
Regardless of whether the confirmed case has travelled (with out ) any worries they must provide the Centre for Hygiene and Security with the information of the venues visited during the infection period
The relevant information will be issued in an open data format for download download download download download and notification to the relevant users
We will continue to promote the placing of QR codes at more venues and encourage users to scan their records and the time taken for entering and leaving the venues so as to maximize the effectiveness of the programme enhance peoples awareness of vigilance and self defense as well as their confidence in going out to different venues . This will also help the Centre for Health Protection to conduct epidemiology investigations
Depending on the changes of the epidemic and the views of experts the Government will continue to explore how to make more effective use of the mobile application and other technologies to assist the public in preventing and fighting the epidemic
President I thank Ms YUNG for her views and suggestions
(how ever ) we certainly understand that due to peoples own habits or the arrangements for their travel they may have different tools to achieve the same goal
The Governments application is mainly intended to help members of the public use technology more conveniently and effectively to cope with the epidemic
Mr YUNG has just proposed that we will step up publicity and through cooperation with the industry we may enable more people to use the relevant tools . We have been working on this continuously
President I thank Mr LAU for his reminder
Just now Mr LAU asked whether my team and I have used the Relaxation Walk
I wish to report to Members that since I was born on 16 November 2020 on the first day of the Lunar New Year holiday I have been using the Lunar New Year holiday as one of the beneficiaries
Apart from making such a record I have also made a report with Members . The biggest support to us is that if we have been to certain places at about the same time as the confirmed cases we will take the initiative to receive the notification so that we can decide how to do a good job of early testing or even quarantine in the face of an increase in risk
In fact I have also received an infection risk notice because I have been to a place with confirmed cases . Therefore I have followed the relevant notice to conduct a test as early as possible to confirm that I do not have any infection risk so as to protect my family members and colleagues whom I love
I would also like to make an appeal here . As Mr LAU has said we must make good use of our technology including the ease of use approach so that we can do better in the fight against the epidemic
(how ever ) I would like to emphasize that this ease of travel tool is obviously better than other similar technology tools
Other similar measures may be the use of the same application for both persons to enable the recognition of the risk of infection . (how ever ) the QR code for the word 安心出行 is used for the interaction between persons and the premises
As long as the 430 000 people have the application and have made a record in the relevant premises they will benefit
But of course the 430 000 figure is not satisfactory to us . We hope that all or the vast majority of Hong Kong people will make good use of the relevant tools
President I would like to thank Mr LEUNG for his support . We can now take a step forward in Hong Kong peacefully and in the fight against the epidemic
Therefore in the future when we have electronic stamp cards will we combine the relevant technologies?
We fully understand that various trades and industries including all Hong Kong people have been hit hard by the epidemic
I think my colleagues have made it very clear that each department will decide which technology to use . If there are any special technical or other problems with the technology we will be happy to address them
Therefore I hope Mr MOK can understand the duties of various departments
We do not intend to shirk our responsibilities but rather to provide assistance in the scope of our duties . Of course we have consulted various panels and panels to provide information
President first of all I would like to thank Members for expressing many valuable views on the areas of innovation and technology development in their speeches
I notice that most Members agree that it is necessary for Hong Kong to develop IT and that IT is an important driving force for Hong Kongs promotion of economic diversification and sustainable development
We all support the Governments work in this regard and even hope that we can do more . I am very encouraged by this
Since the establishment of the Innovation and Technology Bureau in November 2015 the Government has allocated an additional sum of more than 18 billion to promote innovation and technology development in various aspects including the promotion of research and development RD the promotion of the Reintroduction of Industrialism the retention of start ups to assist the upgrading and transformation of small and medium enterprises and the encouragement of the use of technology to address social problems
The Budget this year has also earmarked an additional 10 billion to provide us with more resources for the development of innovation and technology
Our work has been on the right track and the communitys awareness and concern for innovation and technology have been growing
Deputy President I would like to give a focus response on several areas
Quite a number of Members have pointed out that the total expenditure on RD in Hong Kong accounts for only 0.76 % of the Gross Domestic Product and the performance is not satisfactory
I understand Members wish to increase RD expenditure
(how ever ) we must understand that Hong Kong is only a city and the economic structure of Hong Kong (cannot ) be compared to that of an advanced country
In addition the lack of national defence and the relevant research and development expenditure have also imposed certain restrictions on the research and development activities
Nevertheless the total expenditure on RD in Hong Kong has increased from 11.9 billion in 2006 to 18.3 billion in 2015 representing an increase of 53 %
The Advisory Committee on Innovation and Technology led by me completed a review of the strategy for innovation and technology development at the end of last month and proposed a series of objectives including raising the ratio of Gross Domestic Expenditure on Gross Domestic Product GDP to 1 % in 2020 from the current 0.76 % which is about 30 billion per annum
In addition we encourage private enterprises to increase their investment in scientific research in a bid to adjust the expenditure ratio between public and private enterprises from 56col44 to 45col55 so that Hong Kongs scientific research investment will be directed by private enterprises instead of public ones
In order to achieve the aforesaid objectives the Government is actively considering the provision of tax and financial concessions as well as other policy concessions to promote the development of innovation and technology in Hong Kong
The Financial Secretary has announced the establishment of the Inland Revenue Department IRD which will proactively study issues such as tax deduction for scientific research expenditure to promote industrial development
The Innovation and Technology Bureau will work with the Inland Revenue Office on the (over all ) promotion of local research and development
REO is a new growth point for Hong Kongs economic development
We hope to reverse the trend of denuclearization and promote the reinvigilization of innovation and technology so as to introduce and develop high end manufacturing industries suitable for Hong Kong so as to introduce the high value added manufacturing part into Hong Kong revitalize the manufacturing industries in Hong Kong again promote economic growth and create quality employment opportunities
The Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation will establish a Data Technology Centre and Advanced Manufacturing Centre in Tseung Kwan O Estate to provide a base for smart production . It is expected that the relevant projects will be completed in 2020 and 2022 respectively
Moreover the setting up of a key scientific and technological base in the Lok Ma Chau Loop the Hong Kong Innovation and Technology Park and the Hong Kong Innovation and Technology Park will help promote the commercialization and industrialization of scientific results and promote the development of Hong Kongs new manufacturing base as well as the advantages of the industry in Shenzhen
In the long run we are exploring the allocation of about 56 hectares of land near the LiantangHeung Yuen Wai Boundary Control Point for the development of the Long Term Housing Development Project to meet the land demand of the new construction site
In addition we have set up the Committee on Innovation and Technology Development and Reindustrialization to coordinate Hong Kongs innovation and technology development as well as the process of reindustrialization with a high level and interdepartmental approach
The Financial Secretary will serve as the Chairman of the Panel and the first meeting will be convened in May
I believe that the Panel will definitely come up with a genuine solution to Hong Kongs development strategy for the long run
Smite is the bottom pillar of the local economy and start up enterprises are the driving force under the new economic order
It is our priority task to encourage existing SMEs to apply technology more effectively improve their production processes and products and assist them in upgrading and transforming
We are also committed to assisting the development of innovation and technology IT and the start ups in providing support in the areas of capital technology and the Internet
I thank Members for their support of this work in their speeches
In order to enhance the competitive edge of small and medium enterprises SMEs in the long run we launched the Technology Voucher Programme last year to provide each SME with a maximum of 200,000 to subsidize their use of technology services and options to enhance productivity or upgrade and transformation
So far more than 1 400 SMEs have registered on the (web site) of the Scheme in April and received over 400 applications
In recent years a wave of innovation has emerged in Hong Kong and through the liaison and assistance of the Government and private sector the innovative environment has gradually taken shape
At present there are about 2 000 global startup enterprises in Hong Kong and 38 operators have set up their businesses in the shared workroom Entrepreneurship Training Centre and Accelerator . The number has increased by 24 % as compared with 2015
Besides the well known International Investment Fund and the Hong Kong Innovation and Technology Fund have also set up different entrepreneurship programmes and funds in Hong Kong showing the confidence of international enterprises and investors in Hong Kongs start up business market
Apart from the training programmes and funding provided by the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation and Cyberport we will soon launch a 2 billion innovation and technology venture capital fund to jointly invest in local innovation and technology startups through a one to two ratio of investment with the selected private venture capital fund
We hope that apart from introducing private capital the Government will also introduce risk investment funds and professional knowledge and the use of the Internet to help Hong Kongs startup enterprises grow
The Hong Kong Productivity Council will provide work space and technical support for young entrepreneurs and start up businesses in the setting up of the Home Office this year
Users can turn their creative ideas into industrial design and then turn them into products through prototype production
Creative space will help nurture Hong Kongs creative culture and support reindustrialization
Technology is closely related to life
Like the Government many Members hope to see a smarter Hong Kong
The consultancy study on Formulating a Smart City Development (blue print ) will be completed in the near future
The major areas of study include smart government services the environment health care and transport
The study will also propose a set of digital infrastructure and technical standards to enable different government departments public organizations and the business sector to broadly and safely apply the latest technology including the Internet of Things big data and cyber security technology
We will work closely with the relevant departments and various sectors of the community to build a smart city
In addition the 2 billion ITF will be launched next month to subsidize some IT projects that will make peoples life more convenient comfortable and safe or cater for the needs of the specialised groups
The fund has a wide scope of applications including health transport education and safety . It is estimated that the first batch of projects will be launched in 2018 to benefit the public
The timely introduction of appropriate technologies can provide better services to the public
Therefore we have earmarked 500 million from the Government to assist different departments in improving technology efficiency and services in the coming five years
The funding earmarked will be used to support departmental planning and technology projects including the Pilot Scheme
With these remarks President I urge Members to pass the Appropriation Bill 2017
Thank you President
The incumbent Government has been in office for more than three years . With the strong support of the country and the assistance of senior government directors and various departments we have been actively promoting the development of innovation and technology in Hong Kong in the eight major directions proposed by the Chief Executive . The various innovation and technology policies and measures have been successfully launched and implemented progressively and the (over all ) environment of innovation and technology ecology in Hong Kong has been significantly improved
For example the expenditure on RD of public organizations has increased substantially from 9.29 billion in 2014 to 14.7 billion in 2019 . The number of investment funds in Hong Kong has increased sevenfold from 12.4 billion in 2014 to 9.9 billion in 2019 . The number of start up enterprises has increased from about 1 100 in 2014 to more than 3 300 in 2020
Hong Kong has also been recognized internationally for its promotion of IT development
In the World Digital Competitiveness Yearbook 2020 announced earlier by the International Institute for Development in Lausanne Switzerland Hong Kongs global ranking has risen from the 11 place in 2018 to the 5 place in the world while Hong Kongs global ranking in respect of IT infrastructure has risen from the 18 place to the 7 place
Besides the Digital Intelligence Index Report jointly published by the University of Manchester and the University of Texas University of Texas in 2020 confirmed that Hong Kong is one of the most advanced digital economies in the world ranking second and third respectively in the Asia Pacific region and around the world
Certainly with the rapid development of innovation and technology we understand that we still need to take forward the work and actively seize the opportunities
Therefore the Financial Secretary has just said in his speech that IT will continue to be one of the key areas of the Policy Address this year
The Policy Address this year has further proposed a number of initiatives related to innovation and technology which fully demonstrate the SAR Governments high regard for and firm determination in promoting the development of innovation and technology in Hong Kong
I will now give a brief response to the views expressed by Members in the first debate session on innovation and technology
Quite a number of Members have expressed concern about how Hong Kong can tie in with the new development pattern with the Mainland as the mainstay of its domestic circulation and actively integrate into the development and construction of the Greater Bay Area
The Secretary for Constitutional and Mainland Affairs has also made a response in his earlier speech
Let me add some more on innovation and technology
Hong Kong and Shenzhen are not only close neighbours but also close partners in cooperation and there is ample room for cooperation between the two places in the IT sector
We are working hard to develop the Hong Kong Innovation and Technology Park in the Lok Ma Chau Loop
Apart from becoming the largest IT platform in Hong Kong HSITP will also join with the Shenzhen Innovation and Technology Zone on the opposite side of the Shenzhen River to form a ShenzhenHong Kong Innovation and Technology Cooperation Zone with synergy so that the two places can complement each others strengths in IT and achieve a win win situation
I am very grateful to Members for their support for the project and for the funding for the first phase major works and fire safety facilities developed in the Lok Ma Chau Loop earlier
I am also grateful to Honourable Members for their valuable views on the development of Hong Kongs innovation and technology HRT in their speeches
We will later apply to the Finance Committee for another funding to implement the early operation expenses for the first batch of development and support for the ITC of Hong Kongs Shenzhen Innovation and Technology Park . We hope to continue to receive Members support
In order to allow local technology enterprises to seize the development opportunities in the Greater Bay Area as soon as possible or to join hands with the Shenzhen Municipal Government to promote the completion of the first batch of buildings in the Hong KongShenzhen Innovation and Technology Park HKITP the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation will lease and manage some of the areas in the Shenzhen Innovation and Technology Zone so as to enable the technology industry in the Greater Bay Area to take root as soon as possible
We are currently jointly formulating a joint policy applicable to the cooperation zone with Shenzhen to provide support measures for the lagging technology
Yesterday Mr Jimmy NG mentioned in his speech that he was concerned about the progress of Hong Kongs reindustrialization
With a promising development prospect in Hong Kong through reindustrialization it can even find new growth points for the Hong Kong economy and create quality employment opportunities to enhance Hong Kongs competitiveness
The Government of this term attaches great importance to and is actively promoting the reindustrialization of Hong Kong and has all along been creating favourable conditions for reindustrialization in the four aspects of infrastructure talent technology and capital
In terms of infrastructure it is expected that the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation will complete the development of a microelectronics centre in the Yuen Long Industrial Estate by 2023 to attract enterprises producing microelectronics products and related industries such as advanced materials . As for the data and technology centre in the Tseung Kwan O Industrial Estate it has already completed its work and the advanced manufacturing centre is expected to be completed by 2022
In respect of talents we launched the Reindustrialisation and Technology Training Programme in August 2018 to provide matching support for local enterprises to receive technology training
As at the end of 2020 the Scheme has approved over 1 760 training subsidy applications which have subsidized a total of 3 440 local enterprises to receive training . The total amount of subsidy is about 256 million
In yesterdays debate Mr LUK Chung hung pointed out the need to prepare well for the coming era of artificial intelligence
REO can precisely subsidize more local enterprises to receive relevant technology training to enhance their skills and knowledge and help them better grasp the convenience and huge opportunities brought by the development of artificial intelligence
As for technology the Productivity Council has been assisting enterprises to switch to high value production and gradually upgrade to Industry 4.0 . It has also assisted traditional industries in upgrading their technologies and organized various types of training courses as seminars to introduce the results of new technologies and latest research
For example the Productivity Council has launched the Upgrading and Recognition Programme under Industry 4.0 to assist enterprises in building smart production lines through the integration of technologies such as artificial intelligence robots and Internet of Things and big data
With the active efforts of the Productivity Council there are already more than 20 industries and more than 50 enterprises using smart manufacturing technology for production
As regards funding I launched the Reindustrialisation Subsidy Scheme in July last year to subsidize manufacturers to set up new smart production lines in Hong Kong in a matching approach
As at the end of 2020 we have received about 170 enquiries and 10 applications
Seven applications have been handled by the Review Board of the Scheme and five of the applications are proposed to be related to the supply of food processing services and construction industries
We understand that RD is a resource of innovation and technology
The Government has all along been committed to promoting the implementation of RD activities in Hong Kong through various funding schemes
Many universities and organizations in Hong Kong have outstanding and outstanding research staff
We are now making full efforts to promote the development of two world class IT platforms in Hong Kongs science parks namely Health at InnoHong Kong which focuses on (health care ) technology and AIR at InnoHong Kong which focuses on artificial intelligence and robot technology
It is expected that the first batch of RD centres will commence in the first quarter of this year
In addition to fully unleash the scientific research potential of Hong Kong and promote the implementation of technological transfer and research and development results we have increased the funding for the 16 National Key Experimental Labs in Hong Kong 6 National Engineering and Technical Research Centres 6 Hong Kong Branch centres and 7 Designated Universities Technical Transfer Scheme Departments in 2019 and will also regularize the funding
In terms of supporting technology startup enterprises the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation HKSTPC has been assisting technology startup enterprises through its nurturing programme to tide over the most critical time of the initial stage of their business
The development plan of the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation HKSTPC provides its development company with systematic support including the provision of work space and shared facilities with rent concessions subsidy technical and management support etc
Besides the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation HKSTPC has also invested in the Startup through the Technology Enterprise Fund TEEF in a matching approach with the Angel Investors or Startup Investment Fund
As for Cyberport another technological flagship of Hong Kong it also supports start ups through its incubation programme to provide a seed fund to assist participants in developing innovative ideas into original products . It also subsidizes its incubation companies to participate in acceleration programmes and provide early financing for start ups in Cyberport
Moreover the eHome Scheme and the (over seas ) and Mainland market promotion schemes launched by Cyberport provide support for tenants and start up enterprises in Cyberport at different stages
Apart from the two major technological flagships mentioned by me just now universities are also a major base for nurturing innovation and innovation
Since 2014 we have been implementing the University Startups Technology Startups Enterprise Subsidy Scheme to encourage university teams to set up start ups so as to unleash the scientific research potential of Hong Kong and promote the implementation of the results of RD
As at the end of 2020 the University of Hong Kong Innovation and Technology Corporation Limited has provided a total of around 200 million in funding to 293 startup enterprises
Besides we have also worked with our joint venture partners through the Innovation and Technology Fund to jointly invest in local IT startups in a matching approach to encourage private investors to invest in Hong Kongs IT startups
Over the past two years the Fund has invested in 19 local fashion companies with an investment of about 100 million attracting more than 500 million in private investment
In order to step up support for innovation and technology IT development and respond to the continuing demand for IT facilities by the industry in addition to the full development of the Hong KongShenzhen Innovation and Technology Park the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation is launching a Phase 2 expansion project . It is proposed to build two subsidiary buildings in the Hong Kong Science Park as well as to build an laboratory and the relevant facilities on some platforms to provide a total floor area of about 28 000 sq m . Our plan will launch a pilot project next month on the development of the Hong Kong Science Park
In addition we also plan to expand the Cyberport 5 to build a new office building . The facilities provided include an office a shared working room a meeting venue and a data service platform
It is expected that the expansion building can accommodate 100 more technology companies and 750 more investment enterprises
We will also strive to seek funding support from the Finance Committee during the current term of the Legislative Council
Many Members have mentioned that technology plays a role in the war of resistance against the epidemic
In the past year the Coronavirus Disease 2019 coronavirus pandemic has highlighted the importance of technology in development and application as well as the potential for technology to assist various trades and industries to turn to the crisis front
In fact innovation and technology plays an important supporting role in a number of prevention and control measures
Some of the technology products and application options developed locally have also been incorporated into the fight against the epidemic . For example there is a system for home care to fight the epidemic and an electronic handbook as well as a local infection situation interactive map
We have fully heard the views of the public on the implementation of mobile applications with peace of mind . We will actively and diligently explore ways to make better use of this technology to help Hong Kong get rid of the epidemic as soon as possible
As many major cities around the world are under lockdown the use of electronic commerce has become more important . At the same time it has created many new development opportunities for the innovation and technology industry and accelerated the digitalization of cities
As early as in November 2016 we launched the Technology Voucher Programme to support local organizations in using technology services and proposals to enhance productivity and upgrade the business process
In order to cope with the current epidemic we have launched the Extending Business Plan in the second round of AEF to support enterprises to continue to operate and provide services under the information technology proposal during the epidemic
The response of enterprises to the Scheme was very enthusiastic and the number of applications was even higher than expected . Within the application period over 38 000 applications were received of which about 95 % were from small and medium enterprises SMEs
We have also further increased the funding to 1.9 billion to provide subsidies for eligible applicants
I hope that all enterprises will receive the application results in the next one or two weeks
I also hope that enterprises can seize the opportunity to speed up digital transformation in order to face the challenges and new normal brought about by the epidemic . In the long run this can also help enterprises enhance their competitiveness and develop more new business opportunities
President the Policy Address this year has put forward a number of measures to promote innovation and technology development
On nurturing and attracting technology talents as well as promoting the development of smart city many Members have put forward valuable views
I will listen carefully to the speeches of Members in the next debate session
The Government and the relevant departments will follow up the proposal
Chairman the public hospitals are filled with grievances when health care workers and patients enter public hospitals
In order to improve the situation I think we must change the way we have been handling public health care services . We should focus on the prevention rather than cure . We should do a good job in community prevention diversion and health management
As I have mentioned earlier I hope that the authorities will expand the public community health centre service model to cover 18 districts
The Chief Executive has said earlier that the development of primary health care services in Hong Kong has lagged behind the development of primary health care services in the past 30 years . Can the Chief Executive give us some determination and specific plans in respect of the promotion of primary health care services?
As the community health centre will only be set up in all 18 districts in Hong Kong it will take time . Chief Executive you might as well expedite the process by directly increasing the amount of the Elderly Health Care Vouchers from 2,000 to 3,000 so that elderly persons will not have to wait in the public hospitals and can go to private hospitals for assistance right away
(how ever ) this is actually out of public interest and public sentiment
If schools need help in cleaning measuring and measuring cups and even drinking water can they help to make an assessment so that the water in our wells can be cleaned?
President I would like to focus my speech on Juning Village in particular
(how ever ) the situation has been alleviated to this day and some measures to curb and combat the epidemic have been gradually relaxed
The reason is that many residents of Chun Yeung Estate including those living in Kowloon West and other districts have waited for more than 10 years for the provision of housing in Chun Yeung Estate . In my case I have been waiting for 11 years for the provision of housing in Chun Yeung Estate
The Government has a number of sites including 24 subsidized site for nongovernmental organizations and 19 sites for nongovernmental organizations
Please make good use of these sites which are off the Mainland or in remote areas and expeditiously hand them over to the Promotion Council
I move that the resolution as printed on the Agenda be passed
The Ordinance was enacted in 1986 to allow the death recipient to make an appeal to the deceased in respect of the mistake or negligence of the death of the deceased
According to the legislation the number of claims under the liabilities under clause 4.3 of the Bill includes the amount of damages incurred for the cause of death of a relative
Section 45 of the Ordinance provides that the Legislative Council may by resolution amend the relevant sum
Since the enactment of the Ordinance the amount of compensation for the loss of a relative due to the pain of death was increased three times in 1991 1997 and 2018 respectively
After the review conducted last year in 2018 the amount of funding has been adjusted to 220,000 at present
In 1997 the then Secretary for Justice undertook to review once every two years the amount of compensation for the bereavement sum to reflect inflation
In a recent review conducted in 2018 the Government reiterated its commitment to review the amount of bereavement sum once every two years with reference to the inflation under the Consumer Price Index A
This joint review takes into account the cumulative inflation between March 2018 and March 2020 and makes reference to the Consumer Price Index C
In comparison the cumulative change rate of the Consumer Price Index A during the period was 5 %
The resolution proposed by the Government today seeks to increase the amount of compensation for the bereavement sum from 220,000 to 230,000 and 1,000 reflecting the cumulative inflation in reference to the period of the Consumer Price Index C
If the resolution is passed it will come into effect from the date on which it is published in the Gazette
We have notified the Legislative Council Panel on Administration of Justice and Legal Services the Law Society of Hong Kong the Hong Kong Bar Association and the Hong Kong Insurance Association of Hong Kong the result of the joint review and the motion proposed by the Government today
Moreover before and during the last review in 2018 it was proposed that apart from reviewing the amount of compensation for each year of reference to the Consumer Price Index A and determining the amount of compensation for the loss of a relative the other factors other than inflation should also be reviewed comprehensively
The Government is still open to detailed studies on the views of stakeholders and will maintain communication with them on the future direction
(how ever ) this is an independent and (on going ) task and should not affect the cumulative inflation in the annual review of the Consumer Price Index C which is based on the Consumer Price Index C
