President I thank Dr Priscilla LEUNG for proposing this motion today which gives us an opportunity to discuss the issue of fire safety in old buildings together
First of all I would like to talk about the background of the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance
Hong Kong is a small and densely populated city with many old buildings and many of them are over 30 years old
In recent years the community has become increasingly concerned about the safety of these old buildings especially fire safety
Members should remember that in the 1990s there were a few big fires in Hong Kong involving old commercial and residential buildings which caused many casualties
At that time the general public strongly hoped that the authorities would expeditiously take measures to raise the fire safety standards of old buildings
The Bill was born under this background
In the late 1990s the Fire Services Department FSD conducted a survey on buildings in Hong Kong and found that many fire safety facilities in old buildings had failed to meet the modern fire protection requirements despite being compliant with the fire safety standards in place at the time of completion
The authorities then introduced the Bill into the Legislative Council in 2001 to enhance the fire safety standards of integrated and residential buildings
The Bill was passed in 2002 with the support of the Legislative Council across political parties
The Building Security Ordinance came into force in July 2007 and is enforced by the Fire Services Department FSD and the Buildings Department BD
In respect of fire safety measures in respect of building planning design and construction the Executive Authorities under the Ordinance are the Director of Buildings and the Executive Authorities in respect of fire equipment and equipment are the Directors of Fire Services
The department will send its staff to the target buildings for inspection and then give fire safety instructions to the owners and occupants of the buildings indicating the need for fire safety improvement works in the buildings
Such works generally include the installation of fire safety mountains and rear wheel systems in the residential parts of the buildings as well as the use of the first hand fire alarm system and emergency lighting in public places as well as the provision of escape routes and internal fire protection structures in the buildings
I believe Members will agree that the Bill is mainly the owners responsibility to enhance fire safety in private buildings . All the discussions on the Bill start from this point
(how ever ) since the implementation of the Ordinance FSDC and BD have been putting in a lot of (man power ) and resources to assist owners in meeting the requirements of the Ordinance and following the instructions given by the enforcement authorities
The motion moved by Dr Priscilla LEUNG seeks to address the difficulties encountered by owners of old buildings in completing the requirements of the Ordinance . I hope the Government will step up intervention
(how ever ) I do not agree with the motion that the support provided by the Government to the owners of the old buildings as directed by the fire safety officers is very limited
I will try to give an introduction on the technical support and financial support provided by SDUs and BDs to owners
First of all in respect of technical support the department has appointed a case manager dedicated to following up on individual buildings and providing technical advice and assistance
I agree with the motion that when the Government is implementing the Bill it should take into account the structural conditions of the buildings and handle the problems in a flexible manner when necessary due to land constraints
This has all along been the work direction of FSDC and BD
The departments will adopt alternative proposals based on the circumstances of individual buildings and the information provided by the authorized person for the implementation of the guidelines in accordance with their flexibility or acceptance of the owners proposal
Our work has always been targeted . If we can identify a feasible and technically feasible way to improve the safety of fire safety under the Ordinance the department will definitely consider and provide active assistance
As regards technical support the Fire Services Department FSD has just mentioned some solutions to the problem
I would like to highlight that FSD has conducted a special study on and launched a pilot scheme for the use of the rear wheel system in connection with the problems encountered in the improvement works in respect of the improvement works of old buildings on three floors above ground floor
First of all the Fire Services Department FSD has noticed that the building of old buildings with three floors or below is often older and may encounter greater room and structural constraints when adding fire service tanks and water pumps
The Fire Services Department and the Water Supplies Department reviewed the pilot scheme in July this year and considered its effectiveness
The Fire Services Department FSD is now promoting the connection system to different stakeholders including other suitable third or lower floors owners and the fire works sector with a view to assisting the owners concerned to comply with the requirements of the展 plan as soon as possible
We expect that more than 300 integrated building uses will be benefited
As for financial support the Government HKHS and the Urban Renewal Authority URA have their own permanent plans to help the needy
SFW works related to the Ordinance are already included in the scope of these subsidies or loan schemes
For example a one stop integrated building repair and maintenance support scheme jointly managed by the Hong Kong Housing Society and the Urban Renewal Authority can provide owners of old buildings with a repair allowance for their public premises . Under the general repair allowance the ceiling is 1.2 million per unit or 3,000 per unit
In addition the Government will provide funding and the Housing Authority will manage the Old Age Ownership Grant Scheme to provide the most affordable grant of 40,000 to eligible elderly self (-...) owners
Owners may also apply to the Buildings Department for loan under the Building Safety Loan Scheme for a loan amount of 1 million . Eligible persons with financial difficulties may enjoy a loan (with out ) interest and the repayment period may be extended to 72 days
Past experience has shown that quite a number of property owners have successfully obtained financial support through these schemes and have successfully completed the building management and maintenance works as well as the fire safety works
President in a city with a densely populated city the danger of fire and disasters in buildings should not be overlooked
Over the years the small and big fires that destroyed old commercial and residential buildings have taken away many precious lives . We should not forget these lessons
In particular the fire safety standards of old buildings are the best protection for residents and visitors and should be a common goal of the Government the Legislative Council and the general public . This is also the original intent of the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance
We understand that in order to achieve this goal landlords need to be vigilant and invest resources and time
It is undeniable that many buildings are still waiting for the completion of the Bill after its commencement and it is inevitable that some owners will encounter various difficulties in the process of licensing and licensing . (how ever ) this does not mean that we have to question the effectiveness of the legislation or change the implementation mode drastically right away
The Government will review and continue to study different options from time to time on the premise of not jeopardizing basic fire safety such as reducing the work load of old buildings for fire safety improvement works and lowering the costs incurred by owners so that they can comply with fire safety guidelines as early as possible
