As an international financial centre Hong Kong has been actively participating in the global economy transformation to a low carbon and sustainable economy
The Outline of the 14th Five-Year Plan proposes to promote economic and social development through a comprehensive plan on green transformation and to strive for carbon emission to meet the target by 2030 and achieve carbon neutrality by 2060
In Hong Kong the Chief Executive announced in the 2020 Policy Address that the HKSAR Government would strive to implement carbon neutrality by 2050
The SAR Government the Financial Reporting Council and the industry have been working closely and collaboratively to promote the development of green and sustainable finance so as to increase the investment which is conducive to reducing carbon emissions and building a low carbon economy and contribute to the aforesaid objectives . At the same time the status of Hong Kong as a green and sustainable financial hub in the region will also be enhanced
We have witnessed the flourishing development of the green and sustainable financial markets and the diversification of participants and products
In 2020 the total amount of green bonds issued in Hong Kong reached US1.2 billion and the cumulative amount of green bonds issued by the end of 2020 exceeded US38 billion
In 2020 one third of green bond issuers issued their first bonds in Hong Kong
The Government the financial regulatory authorities and the industry have adopted a multidisciplinary approach to step up market infrastructure and development regulation capacity enhancement and promotion international and regional cooperation and integration into the (over all ) development of the country . We are stepping up our efforts in promoting market development so as to align Hong Kongs regulatory standards with the best international practices and encourage more institutions to make use of Hong Kongs capital market and financial and professional services as the basis for their development
Specific measures include the issuance of government green bonds subsidizing bond issuance and external assessment services continuous enhancement of the responsibility of investing in the Exchange Fund promoting the development of the green and sustainable banking industry encouraging fund managers to take into account the relevant climate risks and making appropriate disclosure studies on the adoption of a common green divestment strategy strengthening the influence of listed companies on Hong Kong and the global economy and the development of the Hong Kong economy
To encourage more fundraisers to use Hong Kongs capital market to finance green projects and provide important new criteria for potential issuers the Hong Kong SAR Government established the Government Green Bond Programme in 2018
In May 2019 and February 2021 we successfully issued two batches of government green bonds totalling US3.5 billion respectively attracting investors to pay fourfold and fivefold issuance of green bonds which was well received by investors
The Government has funded 14 public works projects through the issuance of green bonds covering four eligible categories under the Green Bond Framework
The Government will announce the distribution of funds raised and the expected environmental benefits of the financing project through the Green Bond Report every year
For example the Government announced in the 2021 to 2022 Budget that it would issue green bonds regularly and plan to issue another green bond of equivalent to HK175.5 billion within five years of the 2021 to 2022 financial year in the light of the market situation
In this way we will have more room to try to expand the types of green bonds the mode and channels of issuance of green bonds and the types of projects
The Legislative Council has passed the Resolution in July 2021 to raise the ceiling of borrowings under the Green Bond Programme to HK200 billion which will cover more green projects of different types and will not be limited to public works projects
We are also preparing to issue green retail bonds within this financial year so that the general public can participate in them and thus deepen their understanding of green finance
To attract more green and sustainable bond issuers and borrowers to use Hong Kongs financing platform and professional services the Government launched the Green and Sustainable Finance Subsidy Scheme in May this year to subsidize eligible bond issuers and borrowers to issue bonds and conduct external assessment services
As at 24 September 2017 applications had been approved or are expected to be approved
The subsidy scheme can encourage more providers of financial and professional services and external assessment bodies to set up their bases in Hong Kong
The Central Government has published the Outline Development Plan for the Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao Greater Bay Area in 2019 and 2020 respectively as well as the Opinion on Financial Support for the Development of the Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao Greater Bay Area to support Hong Kongs development into a green financial centre in the Greater Bay Area
Hong Kong as an international financial centre and a bridge connecting the Mainland and (over seas ) can fully leverage the advantages of connecting green and sustainable capital flows in and out of the Mainland under the new economic development pattern of dual circulation thereby promoting green investment and ecological civilization in the Mainland
Looking ahead we will continue to work closely with the relevant departments and industries in the Greater Bay Area to provide the financial services required of the enterprises in the Greater Bay Area in respect of green financing with a view to enhancing Hong Kongs attractiveness as a one stop platform
In addition in respect of the promotion of the Belt and Road Initiative we will continue to work with stakeholders to promote sustainable infrastructure and green development
To further coordinate and enhance the work in the financial sector the Green and Sustainable Finance Intergovernmental was established in May last year by the Financial Services and the Treasury Bureau the Environment Bureau and the relevant financial regulators . The goal is to coordinate measures in the financial sector to address climate and environmental risks expedite the development of Hong Kongs green and sustainable finance and support the Governments climate strategy
In December last year after consulting major stakeholders and members of the sector the Steering Group released its Strategic Plans which proposed six key areas and five short action orders
In July this year the Steering Group further announced its next stage of work focus namely the disclosure and sustainable return on climate research exploration of market opportunities and the development of a green and sustainable financial centre
Just now many Members have mentioned the opportunities of a carbon market
As a matter of fact with the Mainland and other major (over seas ) markets striving to transform into a green low carbon and climate adaptable economy the international carbon market can expect significant growth
Hong Kong as an international financial centre has a unique status in that it can play the strategic role of a gateway to the Mainland to connect with the capital flows in the Mainland and support the countrys goal of achieving carbon neutrality
The Steering Group on Green and Sustainable Finance has set up a dedicated team to assess the feasibility of developing Hong Kong into a regional carbon trading centre with a view to deepening cooperation between Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao and exploring the opportunities arising from the carbon emission matching market and voluntary carbon markets both at home and abroad
We plan to complete the report in December this year
In addition the Hong Kong Exchange has also signed the Two Quarters Memorandum with the Guangzhou Stock Exchange in August this year to foster strategic cooperation among the two places to jointly support and promote sustainable development and facilitate the (over all ) development of the Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao Greater Bay Area GBA
The two sides will study the feasibility of conducting product cooperation in the domestic and (over seas ) markets promote exchange and cooperation in areas such as transaction litigation and technology and share resources in the market for publicity and investor education
Looking ahead the Government will continue to work closely with the financial sector and relevant stakeholders to promote green and sustainable financial development and promote carbon neutrality projects in the region with a view to promoting capital
President I so submit
President I thank the Member for her supplementary question . I will give my reply in two parts
Regarding the form of payment I would like to point out that as pointed out in the main reply various purposes and nature of online payment may be subject to the relevant legislation . I will not repeat the details
I would like to highlight two points
Firstly in respect of the Governments and the activities of other districts in respect of the existing platform for fundraising the relevant legislation is generally based on the nature of the activities and the use of funds for the purposes of fundraising rather than the method of fundraising
Regarding the use of funds as mentioned by the Secretary just now the use of the funds raised must be lawful and law enforcement agencies will monitor pay attention and enforce the law in accordance with the actual circumstances
Under the Organized and Serious Crimes Ordinance anyone who knows or suspects that any property belongs to the offender will be required to submit a possible transaction report to the Police as soon as possible within reasonable limits
Unless the Government agrees to this it will not continue to deal with the relevant matters
President as I have mentioned just now we have some relevant legislation to regulate the nature and purpose of activities relating to various remunerations
As regards future development we will continue to keep an eye on the situation in Hong Kong and other places
Now he only says that some people may have already confirmed the letters but this is only a verbal confirmation
If the Secretariat and the President consider themselves responsible for the entire election or if they consider that the entire Legislative Council should be trusted by the people and the public should be trusted again they should be more emotional . They should not act arrogantly like the Secretariat saying that they will not conduct any inquiry if there is no need to do so
The spirit of the Rules of Procedure is to ensure that every Member is able to complete his or her oath under sufficient circumstances and opportunities . The Secretary for Justice (cannot ) use his or her own judgment to tell other Members that he or she is incapable of presiding over the oath
I think this is totally against the spirit of the Rules of Procedure which is to allow all Members to complete the proceedings successfully before entering the President Election
Therefore the decision or judgment of the Secretary for Justice Andy CHAN is actually an arbitrary use of power (with out ) any basis
If we look at the previous video clip we can see that the three Members had read out the oath completely . The Rules of Procedure only require that Members read out the oath completely . They are not allowed to judge that they do not have the right to take the oath on the basis of their tone or other reasons
Mr LEUNG the fire safety of old buildings in Hong Kong has once again been caught on camera after the fire in the Mini Store in To Tai Industrial Estate was set ablaze in June this year
Under the existing Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance all domestic and integrated buildings completed before 1 March 1987 1987 1987 were subject to regulation
The legislation requires the installation of a series of fire safety facilities in buildings to ensure that the fire safety of old buildings is in line with the standards in the minimum degree of fire safety equipment and equipment units published in 1987 in 1987 1987 in 1987
Although the legislative intent is to ensure the safety of the public the legislation has neglected the individual circumstances of the buildings . The Government has willingly copied all the facilities one by one to each and every building so as to resolve the safety problem
From 2014 to the end of September 2016 the Fire Services Department FSD and the Buildings Department BD have issued a total of 58 000 fire safety directions under section 5 of FSDO and about 24 000 of them have been approved or revoked . Combined with the figures in Dr Priscilla LEUNGs original motion we can see that the number of buildings which can meet the standard is still small and the effect of the legislation has not been confirmed since 2007
In fact it is indeed very difficult to fully comply with the requirements of the Ordinance for the purchase of old residential units
First of all due to the old structure of the building the building may not be able to accommodate the water tanks required for the installation of fire hoses or the automatic sprinkler system . Forcing the installation of water tanks may jeopardize the structural safety of the building . There were also cases in the past where the building was designated as an unauthorized building after the installation of water tanks by the Buildings Department
Moreover although section 59 of the Ordinance provides that a building may be exempted from the penalty for the destruction caused by a structural hazard the situation in the past was that the Fire Services Department FSD did not accept the assessment made by the outsourced professional of a building and insisted on installing the facilities in the building
Lastly the number of contractors willing to undertake improvement works in fire safety and safety is also inadequate . They can easily be given a chance to do so and thus the prices have been pushed up
These projects often cost hundreds of thousands of dollars or even hundreds of thousands of dollars . How can the residents of old buildings especially the elderly and the elderly residents who have been living in these buildings for years afford the cost of carrying out these projects?
Regarding the original motion of Dr Priscilla LEUNG I generally support the need to amend the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance to relax the current requirement of overly stringent requirements on old buildings . But I have reservations about empowering the Government to carry out improvement works for buildings in an emergency
I believe Mr LEUNG has made reference to the proposal made under section 263 of the Buildings Ordinance
It is pointed out in the provision that if the Buildings Authority believes that any building may or may become dangerous but is unable to seize the owner of the building or the owner does not comply with the requirements under the order given in the Bill the Buildings Authority may or may arrange for the order to carry out the specified works or other works that it considers necessary and the cost of the works may be claimed by the owner of the building
I am worried that when the Government exercises its power it will only disregard the owners wallets and employ expensive contractors to carry out works for the sake of speed and safety
Recently there have been a lot of scandals of bid rigging in building maintenance works and many bid rigging cases have been uploaded onto the media screen . The public are gravely concerned about this
Therefore I hope that if the Government really adopts Mr LEUNGs rationale and introduces this amendment in his motion it will ensure that the Government will consider the price issue when exercising its power and even introduce measures to restrict the price
As for the existing loan and subsidy schemes their funding scope covers the improvement of fire safety facilities
The Administration also considers that the existing scheme is sufficient . Despite this I think that the launch of the Fire Safety Update is not a lie in wait
On the contrary the introduction of this scheme can raise public concern about fire safety and the Government can also relax the application threshold and raise the subsidy level
As for the amendment proposed by Han Pan I oppose it mainly for two reasons
First the amendment proposes to expand the Frontier Scheme of the Jetson Loop to cover buildings with a floor area of four to six storey buildings . I have reservations about this proposal
According to the reply given by the Security Bureau to Mr WU Chihui on 2 November the Government has responded that it is conducting a study and has not directly pointed out the feasibility of the proposal put forward by Mr CHAN (Han-) pan
In my opinion the Government must complete the assessment as soon as possible and ensure that there will be no problem of insufficient water supply before the installation can be implemented
In addition I do not support the proposal in the amendment to set up a building maintenance board to attract more contractors to participate in tender . I am worried that this will lower the threshold for participation in works projects and will give rise to greater doubts about the quality of works projects . There is also the suspicion that the Government is compromising the quality of works projects for the sake of speed
Lastly I agree with Dr YIU Chung yim that the Government should continue to develop and promote new technologies and new concepts to improve fire safety facilities so that they will not become an obstacle to social development
In fact many industrial buildings have a great demand for rezoning so as to provide such facilities to residents of cultural recreational and sports organizations or other noncommercial uses . I hope the Government can make improvement in the future
The first debate today will be about the 26 heads with no amendment . This is the most numerous item among the six debates but the debate will only last for seven hours which is very unreasonable
In fact the Budget is different from other bills in that we do not propose any amendments which does not mean that the motion is not important or there is no controversy . It is just that we (cannot ) increase the expenditure of certain projects under the Rules of Procedure so we are left with no choice
We all know very well the existing restrictions imposed by RoP . Hence I hope that if we are given another chance to speak in the future we should not think that Members do not have any views in this regard as there are actually many things to discuss
Next I would like to talk about different projects
The first project is the Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department Fire Services Department
The estimated expenditure of FSD this year is 6.449 billion and the establishment of a job base will increase the number of employees by 331 to 10 709
If we make a comparison with the Hong Kong Police Force HKPF is worth 18.497 billion . It has created an additional 608 posts with a total number of 34 660 posts . All these figures are several times that of the Fire Services Department FSD
Next I hope the Administration will face the matter squarely
In my view the remuneration package for firemen should be on par with that of the Police and there should even be higher requirements for them because they have to face a lot of work and a lot of risks
The Fire Services Department FSD is regarded as an ordinary disciplined force rather than an emergency force and its commissioning fee is only 19,780
On the contrary the Police Force as the best group of the disciplined services received 22,410 as a pay rise which is 13.3 % higher than that of the Emergency Dispatch Force
The decision was made in response to the Report of the British Hong Kong Government in 1988 on zero profit management . As we can see there is a gap between the two
Next I would like to talk about why I hope the Government will increase the budget for FSD so that the remuneration package can be improved
Over the past period of time we have heard a lot of news about firemen being unfortunately killed or injured . We are very sorry about this and hope that more resources can be provided to prevent such incidents from happening be it in terms of salaries or equipment
At present firemen have to deal with very complicated chemical products and they have to inspect many buildings . The situation is very different from the situation in the case of the report published 30 years ago
Therefore the pressure and danger expected by the existing firemen are different from the pressure and danger faced by potential (fire fighters ) 30 years ago
Therefore I think it is very inappropriate for the Government to fail to conduct a comprehensive review in the past 30 years or to consider the room for improvement in the pay structure
The original Fire Services Organisation has also put forward the same proposal in the hope that the Fire Services Department can be classified as an emergency disciplined force with an independent pay scale so that the pay scale can be aligned with the danger and responsibilities faced by the Police Force
Moreover according to all the opinion polls the popularity rating of firemen is also the highest among the disciplined forces
Otherwise we can see that the number of applicants for the Fire Services Department FSD has been low in recent years . If we draw reference from other disciplined services the average number of applications from the Customs and Excise Department CED is 11 000 per year while that from the Fire Services Department FSD is only 4 000
We can see that there is a very high demand and expectation in society
In the past the Urban Council had the power to deal with most of the duties and powers of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department the Leisure and Cultural Services Department and the Liquor Licensing Board in the Urban Council era . The fiscal resources that could be used independently amounted to as much as 8 billion per year which was an important pillar of district administration
Therefore I hope that the Government can increase the funding for the District Leading Action Programme in my following speech so as to provide more supportive measures to improve the municipal affairs in the districts
Districting activities schemes receive a funding of 63 million per annum for each district . Most of the schemes are short term schemes . For example the Central and Western Districts Scheme only provides three rounds of cleaning services . It is thus difficult to follow up district issues
Therefore I suggest that the Government should allocate the 1,000,000 earmarked to the District Leading Action Programme so that DCs will not be led by the scourge but can truly resolve the district issues and truly give play to the important function of DCs to absorb public opinions and then formulate policies
Even under the current circumstances it is difficult for the Government to make contributions to DCs
I find this rather ridiculous because it shows that district administration has been left in a vacuum after the Government has killed the district authorities
DCs are actually quite powerless . They do not have any real power and they can only raise their views to the Government for amendment only when the Government is willing to consult it
(how ever ) the bureaucratic system also has its major problems
Therefore even if we hope that more funding will be allocated to the District Leading Programme in the long run this may only be a means to cure a disease or a means to cure a disease but not the root cause
Apart from the funding proposal for the District Leading Programme and the Community Involvement Programme there is another funding proposal which is of great concern to us . The Government has earmarked 640 million for organizing and organizing celebration activities for the 20 anniversary
I wonder if the Government has the intention to produce such a sensitive figure . If the Government files a complaint to the Central Authorities they may refuse to approve it
All in all the preparation of this celebration is actually a waste of time and effort
We have to think about whether we should spend so much money on organizing these celebrations in the 20 years after the handover of sovereignty or should we just let a group of government officials and Members sit quietly and reflect on why there have been so many problems in Hong Kongs development so far?
The political system is unfair and the conflicts in the deep city have yet to be resolved . It is indeed quite absurd to spend so much money on the celebration
The Home Affairs Department has earmarked 29 million for organizing activities such as the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum the Hong Kong Palace Museum and the Hong Kong Palace Museum
I think it is rather ridiculous to celebrate the 20 anniversary of the handover of sovereignty
Some Members who love the country and Hong Kong have pointed out in the Panel that hanging sheep heads to buy dog meat is not a genuine way to celebrate the 20 anniversary of the surrender of sovereignty
This is exactly what the public need
I believe many people are different
Just now a large number of community activities were held in the city but I do not know how many members of the public participated in them
In fact the 29 million can be spent on more necessary areas
In fact the reputation of IPCC is already very low and people think that IPCC is a toothless tiger
Such a sweep of dust will even destroy the remaining credibility of IPCC turning IPCC into a club for LEUNGs fans and the anti Occupy Central camp
In fact IPCC has been investigating police officers on its own . This is a major public query raised by IPCC and the Complaints Against Police Office . It is indeed difficult to give public confidence to the existing system
Therefore I propose to increase the resources of IPCC in respect of the scale of IPCC so that IPCC can make reference to the independent investigation power of the United Kingdom IPCC and further increase the relevant resources so that IPCC can practically investigate the disciplinary or abuse of power issues of the Police
Let us look at the comparison between Hong Kong and foreign countries . In fact the number of people working in IPCC in Hong Kong is relatively small
Just now I spoke on the head of IPCC but I would also like to add a few words on the head of FSD because many Members have also spoken on this subject
As I said just now we do not propose any amendment does not mean that we have no objection to the funding because our duty is not to increase the Governments expenditure but I am speaking on the hope that the Government can increase the expenditure of FSD
In fact many accidents in society are caused by the increase in the workload of the Fire Services Department FSD . To put it simply it is like eating a dead cat . For example after a fire broke out at the shop in Mai Lai Chong in Ngau Tau Kok earlier FSD had to put out a fire immediately . In addition to the subsequent work such as inspection of industrial buildings a lot of workload has been added to the (front line ) of FSD
Hence many firemen have told me that their workload is too heavy
In fact I do not know whether I should declare my interest or not but my relatives work in the Fire Services Department . From their personal experience I understand that this is a highly risky industry
The Fire Services Department FSD needs to improve its remuneration package . It should at least be on a par with the Police Force . It should have an independent remuneration structure or follow the Police Force as an urgent disciplined force so that it can make a proper comparison between its contribution to society and the crisis faced by the Police Force . This will also enable the Police Force to achieve better performance
My suggestion is that the increase of resources is based on the premise of opening up the structure and providing more powers so as to perform the function of monitoring the Police Force
We can see that many police cases in the past have caused great concern to the public . By causing concern I mean whether IPCC can perform its functions to immediately deal with the abuse of power or disciplinary issues of the police force
The Independent Police Complaints Council IPCC has raised 793 proposals in respect of a number of police questions including the revision of the classification of investigation findings improvement of the常規 and procedural recommendations of the Police Force exercise of police powers and reasons clarification of information in investigation reports etc but only 381 of these proposals were accepted by the Police and only 59.2 % of the questions were accepted by the Police on average
Hence at this level the Police Force has great power or simply put it is able to listen to what it likes and ignore what it dislikes . This is a common situation
In my first speech just now I said that I proposed that the Hong Kong Police Force should make reference to the proposal of the British Independent Police Complaints Council IPCC and give IPCC independent investigation power . Only in this way can the situation of police officers taking care of each other be prevented and IPCCs monitoring of the Governments functions (cannot ) be effectively implemented
On the other hand there are many major cases or cases of social concern . The Police have responded very slowly and IPCC does not see any pressure to monitor the Police in a timely manner
There were two serious cases involving police officers in the past . Even though IPCC considered the complaints to be true the Police refused to accept them and the proceedings lasted for several years
Even if the case is true the Police may refuse to follow up the case . For example the Police Officer CHU King Wai had not formally initiated prosecution after more than 800 days
Therefore if the Independent Police Complaints Council IPCC wants to give the public confidence and the public thinks that the Police have the appropriate powers and the IPCC can monitor the public and monitor whether the Government has abused its powers there must be some amendments
As I said just now an open structure will enable members of the Executive Council to be more broadly composed including pan – democratic Members or other representatives so as to achieve a more balanced consensus
Besides the authorities should also have the independent power of inquiry to allow IPCC to refer cases directly to the prosecution authority for decision on whether or not to institute prosecution
This involves the issue of resources . I hope that after these two cases are reformed IPCC will increase its resources so that it can effectively carry out investigations
At present there are 51 staff members of IPCC in Hong Kong and 16 of them are directly responsible for handling cases
Let us compare the situation with the situation in other (over seas ) countries . For example in the United Kingdom there are 804 police officers and 482 of them are responsible for investigating and following up cases . In the Hong Kong Police Force there are 51 police officers and 30 of them are responsible for investigating and following up cases
In fact the survey rate in the United Kingdom the United Kingdom and the Independent Police Complaints Council IPCC is the lowest especially for the surveyors
Therefore if IPCC wants to give full play to its function of investigation and monitoring it is imperative to increase the number of investigation officers
Therefore the existing structure is unable to provide sufficient support for the independent investigation into complaints lodged by the Police from a neutral and impartial perspective by the Independent Police Complaints Council IPCC
Therefore I hope the Government will conduct a review on Head 121 and consider increasing resources in the future
The next head that I would like to speak on is Head 169―Exempting the Commissioner of Communications and Supervisors from expenditure . The estimated expenditure for this year is 21.2 million which is 23.9 billion in total
In June 2016 we passed the Interception of Communications and Monitoring Amendment Bill 2019 which empowers the Commissioner of Interception of Communications and Monitoring to require the law enforcement agencies to provide the results of the intercepting and monitoring operations for investigation . This is a major reform which gives the Commissioner the power to monitor the law enforcement agencies
(how ever ) there are still many standards which have not met the publics requirements and the application of technology in the communication industry has become increasingly distant . This may not necessarily be the case under the Bill and all the relevant ordinances in the past
For instance the monitoring of hacker software such as the breach of mobile phones or the intercepting of digital communications and the request for providing user information to online service providers are not subject to the limitations under the Ordinance
Earlier on Secretary for Security John LEE also admitted that the Police had used technology to decrypt mobile phone numbers . He also refused to deny the Police use of hacking software on the ground of law enforcement . This has aroused reasonable suspicion that the Police had intercepted the personal data of the public through different technologies
This is a serious issue that has been exposed to the world and has caused scandals all over the world . Monitoring the Governments efforts to protect the privacy of the people is also a very serious issue in the civil society of various places
After the occurrence of many similar cases in the United States the United Kingdom and Europe the community has been greatly followed up
Therefore apart from obtaining information to dispense with the offences respect for and protection of peoples privacy are also very important factors for us to consider when considering the Bill
As pointed out in paragraph 6.31 of the Anniversary Report 2015 it is not the first time that the person concerned has not fulfilled the duties and responsibilities stipulated in the Ordinance
In other words the officers involved are law enforcement officers . In the past many law enforcement officers obtained private resources through the use of technology not covered by the Ordinance but they were not subject to regulation
I believe this has also confirmed that the relevant legislation needs timely update and review
Originally I proposed to reduce the amount of the amount of 690,000 which is equivalent to the remuneration payable to four police officers who were convicted of causing serious bodily harm to others and the amount of 12,000 which is equivalent to the amount of the amount of the remuneration payable to four police officers who were convicted of causing serious bodily harm to others
(how ever ) since it is ruled that the headland is not in compliance with the rules it does not matter . I still wish to express my views here
In fact some Members concerned have pointed out at previous meetings that the controversies involving the Seven Cops and the prosecution of several police officers have actually existed
Certainly many Members of the pro – establishment camp will say that they have already gone through the established procedures to determine whether they should be entitled to long service or retirement benefits
(how ever ) if we take a step back how will the public or other stakeholders view this matter?
We all know very well that we have to face up to the harm done by a single group because if we are not careful enough the image of the whole disciplined services or the whole group will be seriously damaged
It can thus be seen that the image of the Police Force in society has already been undermined greatly including the indiscriminate arrest for the Umbrella Movement the subsequent indiscriminate prosecution of the Police and political prosecutions coupled with various other cases . I am not the only one who has mentioned these cases and the opinion polls have clearly shown that
Therefore the protestors do not need to accuse me of smearing them . This is absolutely not the case
During the Umbrella Movement these police officers were handcuffed and convicted by the court for targeting a man with barbed wire in private . They were sentenced to imprisonment for this act
In the face of such court judgments the disciplined services should have greater restraint and set up a good shield
Police officers are the symbols that can monopolize violence . They have guns and guns . They have all the conditions to protect themselves from causing harm to others
Therefore in the face of this situation I earnestly urge all sectors of the community to seriously examine whether a black sheep should continue to be brought to the police station in accordance with the benefits originally granted to them . If so the image of the police force will only continue to be damaged and (cannot ) be repaired
Please stop speaking
This policy has been criticized and criticized by quite a number of people for various faults and shortcomings
In this session I would like to give a detailed account of my views on the 2030 Towards a Planning Vision and Strategy Transcending 2030 and draw a conclusion from this that is I think the assessment on sustainable development should be suspended
Since the introduction of the 2030 Towards a Planning Vision and Strategy Transcending 2030 the consultation has been poorly conducted . The consultation period started with only three to four public announcements of the 15 thematic reports of the 2030 Towards a Planning Vision and Strategy Cending 2021 . It was difficult for members of the public to start studying the proposals
The development plan to be implemented in the next 10 years will only have a consultation period of only six months which is half less than the consultation period of the one year planning for the elderly care service under the smaller scheme
Does the Government think that the concepts and views put forward by the public are never enough to be adopted or that the publics views are being used as an enemy to the present situation?
In order to study the development of Hong Kong in the next 10 years or so we must first ask ourselves what problems Hong Kong is facing so that we can think about the future direction of Hong Kong
The problems in Hong Kong are like a river that flows into a mountain . There is a disparity between the rich and the poor and the Government and business and the rural are in dire straits . The property prices are totally unbearable and the property prices are heavily dependent on the external economy . The property prices of some industries are monotonous and young people (cannot ) move upward . The elderly care and education problems have been discussed countless times in the past six months since I took office as a Member of the Legislative Council
(how ever ) can the 2030 Project address the problems faced by Hong Kong?
I think Prof LUI (Dai-) lok has quoted a very appropriate article in which he says that the main element of Hong Kong 2030 is to plan for a highly densely populated city to meet new economic challenges and create capacity for sustainable development . The main themes of these three elements are to provide land and space and to create capacity for development
In response to the proposal of the 2030 project he concluded that he could not read out any new strategy in the economic development phase
After 2030 what new advantages will Hong Kongs economy and related areas such as talent training create?
How can it support or make use of the objective conditions and trends of the world or the region to promote development?
Which opportunities will the Government seize?
The Government has neither put forward any specific proposal nor given any detailed explanation
Such a vision and planning is even more like my own wish in a primary student novel
More importantly what he has not said is the most important and urgent problem that Hong Kong needs to solve
According to the Development (blue print ) for Hong Kong 2030 for the Next 30 Years the population will increase by 800 000 . Will the Lok Ma Chau Loop import a large number of Mainland enterprises and export Hong Kong technology talents?
As we can see the industrial and infrastructure policies of Hong Kong are also in line with the development (blue print ) of the country which imagines Hong Kong to dovetail with regional development . We (cannot ) help but ask Is this really what Hong Kong people want?
For example the housing supply that local residents can afford the employment opportunities in the districts and community elderly care are not only out of reach but also in the way of development
For example in the 2030 to build a liveable city the Government did not mention the appropriate living space standard . This means that the Government can actually make random calculations and build more housing units in the same area
Furthermore with the continuous expansion of the infrastructure group the infrastructure will definitely lead to a rise in rents and cost of living and tenants of subdivided units will only be exploited more severely
As Hong Kong and the Mainland are not able to integrate into each others employment opportunities or regional employment imagination more Hong Kong people may have to return to the Mainland for employment or spend their twilight years in China
The price of infrastructure development is that the residents have to pay for additional transport time cost and maintenance as well as rentals and inflation
As for elderly people aged 18 or above in China many problems are now facing them and this is also against the original intent of the Governments community elderly care policy
In the end when more dense railway and public systems are connected to the communities and commercial centres of the people local residents and working class people will definitely experience more inconvenience . The conflicts arising from the integration of Hong Kong and the Mainland will also emerge
If a cultural policy is not up to the mark and there is no communication between the Government and the community how can the Government put forward more important policies for the development of Hong Kong in the next few decades?
If the Government has the intention to develop such places why can it not do so in Hong Kong?
I would like to point out that the (blue print ) for 2030 has not been consulted by the public apart from the problems with its preliminary design . It is also obviously not developed for the needs of Hong Kong people
Eight years ago the Government kept saying that it would ban Hong Kong people (with out ) further deliberation but now it has completely failed to provide policy support and has merely talked about killing people . What kind of government is this?
First of all the issue under discussion this time around is very serious
The Budget is about the Governments future expenditure
We all know very well why people are human beings . According to the traditional Chinese philosophy of the spirit of everything there is a difference between man and bird meaning that is there are people who govern by the law of nature
I also wish to vindicate proXs colleagues
We have a hundred or so amendments and a hundred or so amendments is a huge number
It is impossible for a normal person to remember all the contents of a hundred articles . It is also very difficult for him to read all the articles carefully and make a list of all the amendments in advance
On this premise I believe Members need more time to think about the meaning and logic of each amendment
From the perspective of thinking about a bill (every one ) can be very different very simple and very complicated
For example the first amendment proposed by Mr CHAN (Chit-) chuen seeks to reduce the annual operating expenses of the Chief Executives Office to 777 . What does 777 represent?
This may be one of the reasons
In fact there are many interpretations
Therefore I think if we have to think about the contents of a motion we may have to look at it from different angles . Given that the motion is binding―the first one already has a reduction of more than 100 million which is a huge amount―I think Members should have more time to carefully examine the contents of the motion carefully and then try to understand it from different angles before deciding their voting stance
The second reason is that I think if the term reduction has become a culture over time people will mistakenly think that Members of the Legislative Council are all voting machines and this is very serious
Hence when we study culture we will understand culture as a human being and the behaviour in our daily life
Otherwise it is reasonable to speculate whether a certain Member or a certain group of Members has put all the amendments first in their mind and they think that they will vote for certain amendments such as those supporting opposing or abstaining from voting
This will give the public the impression that the opposition camp is a group of people who only know how to press a button upon their first visit to the Legislative Council instead of making a decision after careful consideration
What I am saying is that this motion or the amendment will give the public an impression that we are already voting machines with established positions . We do not intend to conduct a comprehensive and in depth debate on every issue and then come up with a thoughtful and thoughtful reply
If we still adopt the approach of being mother and father or almost like a husband to deal with this important motion before us the public may not see it as a good impression
I do not wish to see the tradition of five minutes turning into one minute being regarded as a pile of voting machines in the Legislative Council where Members will vote (with out ) thinking and thus the culture of society will be formed or people will think that we have certain characteristics . For this reason I oppose the reduction of the time limit
When we make a choice the greatest value of giving up is the opportunity to succeed
This is also a very big question
This will not take up more time and will instead make the legislature more transparent and clear
I will still focus my speech on the youth policy of Hong Kong
The amendment I am going to speak on is about the total estimated expenditure on Head 53 which is reduced by about 2.9 million from 2017 to 2018 for the International Youth Exchange Scheme
Just now Mr LAU has given his views on these projects . Mr LAU also raised a question earlier on about the reasons for increasing the expenditure of the Mainland Development Plan under the Policy Address . He said that the additional funding was mainly for the increase in demand
(how ever ) I would like to point out that the Governments practice of only increasing the fees of exchange tours in Mainland China but neglecting the international exchange programme is actually not because of demand but because of bigger political reasons and the international interests behind it
I believe that if the demand for young people in Hong Kong increases the demand for international exchange and foreign exchange will be even higher
According to the paper of the Youth Council the International Youth Exchange Programme started in 1979 and was originally known as the Union of Britain Youth Exchange Programme . But later it was changed to the current name not only because it was held in the United Kingdom but also because it was held in other countries
These exchange programmes are very different from the free tours offered by the New Territories and they are mainly for sightseeing . For example the Mainland Youth Exchange Programme approved by the Government just now is a kind of exchange programme for Mainland young people
First the authorities will directly contact the governments of (over seas ) countries to arrange youth exchange activities
In the process the Government has failed to identify any community organizations or youth organizations in Hong Kong . In the process the Government has failed to provide people with a lot of political objectives or a lot of political contents
The authorities should directly contact the local authorities to ensure that they can discharge their duties properly
First of all the estimated expenditure for this financial year has been substantially increased by 62 % to 2.92 million
We asked why and the Bureau replied that this was because the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region would be part of the 20 Anniversary Celebrations and more young people would participate in the exchange activities in Hong Kong and they would consider spending more money in countries in the distant future
I believe this is not the real reason
The Secretary then replied that it was for the sake of celebrating the reunification and preparing to go to a faraway place . I believe these two are not the real reasons for the increase in the estimated expenditure
It is a very poor response to say that the Government is celebrating the reunification after strengthening road cleaning the launch of the Exchange Scheme in foreign countries and the holding of a music concert
On the other hand these public funds have increased and have cost nearly 3 million . This will also arouse other queries . For example when young people go on (over seas ) exchange programmes they are often concerned about safety and insurance
All these are the basic directions for the review and review of the Exchange Programme . It is all because the Home Affairs Bureau has all along been unwilling to openly explain to the public how the money spent on youth development work is meaningful . It has also failed to explain clearly the meaning of the Exchange Programme especially the Mainland Exchange Programme
In fact there are some specific examples of youth exchange programmes in the Mainland
For example last year a group was approved to set up a cross province cultural survey team led by a limited company under the Employment Development Fund . Six months later some Mainland young people returned to Hong Kong
A total of 150 people participated in the event costing 470,000
During the same year an organization called the New Territories Youth Association organized a two weeks exchange tour in Beijing . 50 people participated in the tour and the cost of the tour was the same as that of the exchange tour . The exchange tour costed 450,000
What is the ratio of exchange tours sightseeing and visiting places?
All the documents are not made public nor have they been published in the regulations . Given such a low transparency the Home Affairs Bureau does not have any intention to conduct any review or improvement and only approve similar standards to community organizations every year
As for these community organizations as mentioned by Mr LAU Siu – lai just now they are very suspicious . Will the funding for these politically affiliated organizations which have connections in the feeding of fat people achieve the effect of political division?
I believe all of us are well aware of this
For example the Youth Internship Scheme for the Youth in the Oolong Nature Reserve in Sichuan and the Youth Internship Scheme for the Youth in the Palace Museum in Beijing are all organized to celebrate the reunification
Therefore I support the amendment of Mr LAU Siuwet which seeks to reduce the estimated annual expenditure on the Youth Training and Training Funding Scheme under the Home Affairs Bureau and the reduction of the amount of money for the IYEP which I have just mentioned
The Government replied that it intended to spend 3 million in this year but it was over then
He said that the money would be used in a partial manner in the next six years . How much money will be spent each year?
This is exactly the reason why we asked the question
Therefore I have proposed an amendment to reduce this sum of 100 million . (how ever ) as pointed out by Mr LEUNG just now my amendment does not target at the estimated expenditure for this year and hence I (cannot ) propose it
The amendments proposed this time around mainly target at the Governments criticism of the (blue print ) or direction of the development of the entire tourism industry such as large scale tourism infrastructure and land development
According to the paper of the Panel on Economic Development the Government claims that it has to enhance the receiving capacity and attractiveness of visitors in Hong Kong as a whole and has therefore developed a number of tourism infrastructures . The projects to be further developed in the future include the expansion of the Lantau Development Project at the Hong Kong Disneyland and the Fai Yiu Kai Tat Provincial Shopping Centre
Tourism is a pillar industry of Hong Kong . I believe no one will deny its importance and no one will totally oppose the development of tourism
Of course the development area is indisputable but I oppose the Government to use the development of tourism as the basis to make the urban planning urbanized and aristocratic and to suppress the existing trees environment and natural environment . This will make the Government lose public money and harm the environment and peoples livelihood
As a matter of fact as a very small community with a population of over 10 000 Tiananmen Square has been a popular destination for inbound hotel guests . But now the only shopping place in Tiananmen Square which can cover the daily expenses has been turned into a shopping centre for inbound tourists . This will have a great impact on the local residents
In fact the development of tourism is very important to the community of Hong Kong . This is a very big problem and one of the many problems pointed out by the Government in the past . This is one of the problems that will affect the development of the tourism industry in Hong Kong
Another major tourism infrastructure project is the development of a shopping centre in the community . This project is in a different direction from the development of commercial buildings in the community . Let us take a look at the recent project under discussion namely the expansion project of the Hong Kong Disneyland
Dixieland expansion has recently become one of the focus of the city because there are actually a lot of problems with the Dixieland expansion project
As I have said before although the Government is the major shareholder of the Hong Kong Disneyland which is an international theme park it is bound by the shareholders agreement signed by the United States Disney Company Limited and the Government refuses to hand over the agreement on the grounds of commercial secrets and it is against the constitutional requirements of the United States Disney Company Limited and is unable to amend the Convention signed in 1999
On the other hand as we all know clearly from the current discussion we have to accept the very poor agreement made by Dr KWOK Ka Ki just now that is the land is precious but the pig is cheap
Besides the Government has failed to recover the 60 hectares of Site 2 site for conversion after it has been left vacant for 20 years
If Members have paid attention to the Wang Chau Town Plan they will know that the area of Wang Chau will be overcrowded with people and the area of Wang Chau will only cover 20 hectares of land
In the past 10 years or so in the next 20 or even 25 years the land for the second phase acquisition of 60 hectares of land will actually be exposed to the sun
On the one hand the Government says that many activities have been organized in the past but on the other hand according to the information there were only four or five activities organized in the past 10 years or so . This is absolutely a waste of land and is not cost effective
The Governments signing of the unequal treatment back then (cannot ) be attributed to this . Now the Government wants to expand the treatment by injecting more than 5 billion into the scheme . This will indeed make the public coffers of the Hong Kong Government rise and fall
The Legislative Council or the people of Hong Kong are forced to step down even though they know that they are being pampered . This is really unacceptable
Now the Government continues to spend money on business with no decision making power . In the past 10 years only the Disneyland (business men ) have made more than 2 billion to 3 billion . Even Mr Michael TIEN from the establishment camp has said in his speech that the Government is giving away its power to Hong Kong
The management of Disneyland is not operating well and we have to provide subsidies to protect its profits . This is certainly very ridiculous . What is even more ridiculous is that the Governments attitude is making people envy the Government
(how ever ) in fact the Government has failed to dovetail with the Legislative Council in fighting for this right which is rather ridiculous
This is a major problem brought about by the tourism infrastructure of Disneyland . In the development of other cities such as Lantau Island or Kai Tak the Government has repeatedly deployed transport infrastructure from the 2030 Towards a Vision and ambition to activate the Kowloon East Cluster
For example the East West Line the Shatin to Central Link Railway the Kai Tak Cruise Terminal the Hong Kong – Zhuhai – Macao Bridge and the construction of the Lantau Extension and the Kai Tak Sports Park the West Kowloon Palace Museum are all projects proposed by the Hong Kong 2030 family yesterday to integrate the transport infrastructure of Hong Kong and the Mainland as the main line development areas
On the one hand the development that Hong Kong people need is basically a backward approach which is in line with the planning of the country of China . On the other hand can these local projects really bring healthy development to the tourism industry of Hong Kong?
Apart from failing to solve the problems faced by Hong Kong how much room has been left for the survival of local minorities in these large infrastructure projects?
Back then when the Hong Kong Disneyland was under construction many of its ancient monuments had not been carefully examined and the Governments reclamation project led to a sea of stones and caused a great deal of controversy
Lantau Island is actually a place with a long history in Hong Kong . It was inhabited by people long before the Stone Age and it has a lot of historical relics and monuments
In order to have a tourism policy on conservation as a prerequisite the Government should first conserve the ancient relics and sensitive ecological areas of Lantau Island
Some Honourable colleagues have also proposed the establishment of a long term bazaar policy to develop bazaars into food bazaars and cultural and arts bazaars
As I said just now these issues can not only promote the development of the tourism industry but also bring the natural environment and peoples livelihood into focus . I believe this is the right direction for the development of the tourism industry
Despite my proposal to reduce the expenditure of the large tourism infrastructure project to only 337,000 which is only a drop in the bucket compared with any other infrastructure project I still wish to express my stance on promoting the large tourism infrastructure project
The tourism industry aims at giving tourists a rich range of historical and cultural experiences . If we give up the natural environment heritage and cultural spaces as Hong Kong should have for the sake of the tourism industry our tourism industry will only be left with a shabby image and Hong Kong will become a deserted city with no history and no soul
What is truly spiritual has been suppressed in recent days and our culture and roots have gradually been stripped of
His motion is very similar to Mr Michael TIENs motion and he should oppose the agreement now signed between the Government and Disneyland
(how ever ) he said just now that we have to be a person of integrity and take care of the (over all ) situation . He does not want Disneyland to be unable to expand so he has to take the responsibility
This is indeed a very sloppy remark
Just now he has used a lot of words and tricks to cover up the problems arising from the unequal treaty between Disneyland and Hong Kong
We all know very well
Members have criticized the development perspective of Hong Kong saying that all the works are just white elephant projects (with out ) any land . But we all know very well that there is no land in Hong Kong
We can use brownfield sites government government land which has been abandoned or the golf course next to Wan Chau but the Government refuses to do so
The reason is that these amendments will affect the interests of some powerful people in society
Members of the public do not oppose the Governments land search nor do they oppose the Governments housing construction
What he opposed was the identification of sites which were mainly government and commercial sites with vested interests at the (fore front )
I would like to conclude my speech by saying that the reduction of head 184 by head 898 is equivalent to a reduction of the amount of the Capital Works Reserve Fund CWRF . What is the purpose of CWRF?
At the beginning of the current term of the Legislative Council there was a great controversy when we first discussed the Public Works (sub committee )
We notice that the existing mechanism is very problematic . Let us start with the background of the fund
The Fund was set up by the Legislative Council on 20 January 1982 to provide funding for minor works land occupation and system equipment
In 2012 the Legislative Council endorsed the increase of the cap on the amount of grant from 21 million to 30 million
(how ever ) today the SAR Government has taken away the power it obtained back then and has continuously increased the amount of funding for projects which do not require independent scrutiny and voting to 30 million
As early as 2007 when the Legislative Council discussed the proposal to raise the ceiling of the minor works project of the Capital Works Reserve Fund from 15 million to 30 million the Legislative Council Secretariat issued a paper to explain the role of the Legislative Council in scrutinizing and approving public works projects
At that time some Members already queried whether the Government should include in the Public Works Programme the criteria for the use of the projects provided by the Assessment Policy Bureau such as the lack of transparency and mechanism so as to enable the Legislative Council and the public to monitor the expenditure and criteria for the use of approved public works projects
The Government has also proposed to split large projects into smaller ones for listing
Land leases should not be subject to any ceiling because the land leases approved by the Government do not have any ceiling
This will enable land resumption projects in Yuen Long and Wan Chau to be approved in the remaining projects in the evening
The original intent of proposing the minor works and non recurrent expenditure items as motions to the Legislative Council was to enhance the efficiency of the funding
For example we do not need to conduct an independent review on a small construction project such as the Tianqiu Link . The Government can allocate funding for the construction project in one go so that the project can be approved expeditiously
Back then a ceiling was set for the expenditure of each project to avoid the Governments inclusion of excessively large projects such as the XRL and HZMB in the fund
On the one hand we will ensure that many motions on major projects can be carefully scrutinized in the Legislative Council and on the other hand we will avoid the collusion of non controversial projects on peoples livelihood with the controversial projects concerned
As we can see the premise of the project is that first it is a minor project and second there should be no dispute
The most absurd part of this mechanism is that even though we agree that 99 % of the 9 400 projects are proposed we are still forced to express our position and express our support or opposition for these projects in an organized manner . As a result we are forced to support the projects we are not willing to support and we are forced to oppose the projects we are willing to support
In 2014 the Government introduced the 2,000 million application for extension of the preliminary funding for landfilling into the Capital Works Reserve Fund
Not only were the democratic camp criticized but even the establishment camp criticized the inclusion of this project in the Budget as a reason for the failure of effective discussion on the project reserves
Finally Secretary for the Environment WANG Ganseng took up the project for the sake of preserving the (over all ) situation . Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury Kenneth CHEN also indicated that he would review the mechanism
After the SFO negotiations the SFO Declaration has established the role and contents of the Fund
After deducting the land cost from the land premium income of the Land Trading Agency the Hong Kong Government and the SAR Government will share the benefits
The land revenue earmarked by the Hong Kong Government will be allocated to the Capital Works Reserve Fund for public works and land development in Hong Kong
Why do I have to propose a mechanism for earning income here?
In other words if the Government adds projects which are highly controversial and favourable to the white elephant projects of big property developers into the Fund it will first sell the precious land to developers for development and then develop more white elephant projects with the money earned by developers and the land earned by developers and the contractors may be these people
In fact the money and contributions made by these relationships can be regarded as indirect transfer of benefits
This is because we think that the entire project should not include any controversial items and there should be the issue of transfer of benefits
As I said just now the mechanism (cannot ) effectively monitor Members . For this reason we have proposed a new mechanism and this is also the reason why I propose this amendment to cut part of the Budget
The mechanism has yet to be established and in the absence of such a mechanism I consider it necessary to raise a strong protest
(how ever ) if 20 Members are already a large number
After all the Legislative Council should have the power to include any project in the Capital Works Reserve Fund
In the past I believe very few people would talk about the problems with the Secretary for Justice structure . Secretary for Justice Rimsky YUEN who is a man with a clear mind may not have been hit by the bullet on this subject
(how ever ) this topic is also very important because especially after the Umbrella Movement LEUNG Chun – ying kept saying that he wanted to turn the tide and bring the tertiary institution to its knees
He has arranged for a large number of people to join the University Council and even tried to control the management of the university to make a lot of decisions that are unfavourable to academic freedom and the autonomy of the university
The Government should focus on the organizations that can be controlled and not the tertiary institutions
In 1965 UGC was established as an unconstitutional advisory body which has been responsible for advising the Government on funding applications to various universities and handling funding applications for public money research
The University Grants Committee has the Research Assistance Authority which is responsible for vetting and approving applications for various types of research funding and new grant . The Quality Assurance Agency is also responsible for conducting quality approval work at universities to affect the academic quality of universities and the character of tertiary institutions
As the funding for tertiary institutions it is decided that first their ideology will definitely affect the direction of university development because the university will adjust its development direction according to the requirements of the University Board
As a society universities play a very important role in nurturing talents . The direction of study of how universities should be educated will definitely affect the development of society as a whole
For example universities respect scientific research and attach great importance to nurturing scientific and technological talents and the scientific research in Hong Kong society will also enjoy sufficient growth
Universities and society are inseparable and they can nurture the motivation of the bottom half of society
Meanwhile the relationship between UGC and the Government is also a factor in determining how UGC will be affected by the Government and thus how the tertiary institutions will be affected
According to the second report of the Audit Commission issued at the end of October last year No . 67 the Funding Committee for University Education and the Funding Committee for Academic Study of the Research Grant Programme the Audit Commission has criticized the University Grants Committee for its black box operation and its failure to respond to public money
The current system needs improvement because it lacks transparency and accountability . As an unlawful advisory framework in charge of a large number of public funds and a large number of public funds it should have greater transparency and accountability
The report pointed out that the Secretariat of the Hong Kong Institute of Teachers had never promulgated the Rules of Procedure of the Council and students and the public had never been informed of the quorum and voting requirements of the Council and the operation of the Council had even been overlooked
The report also further pointed out that the status of the 26 interest declaration forms of the University Grants Committee members was unknown . The relevant report also revealed that four principal researchers who had received the funding were also members of the Financial Services and the Treasury Bureau but they had not declared any conflict of interest or any personal injury or loss of property
It is a huge amount of public money for 360 university students to pay for one years tuition fees
From the above we can see that the administrative chaos in the University Grants Committee has resulted in serious problems in terms of the details of meetings the expenditure criteria and the declaration of interests . This is also related to the lack of transparency and accountability
Apart from these administrative issues the specific work of UGC ie . the approval of funding is still worth criticizing
In respect of the funding proposal of UGC there were a few major initiatives in the beginning . The first one is internationalization . This approach is also applicable to the scoring games played by Hong Kong and the Mainland
Every year when a ranking of the Hong Kong Economic and Trade Commission ETCC is published all newspapers will report the news
Hong Kongs concept of ranking is embedded in university education and one of the factors is internationalization
In fact there is a special category for internationalization of universities in different scores . The higher the internationalization the higher the scores and the higher the rankings may be
Therefore over the years universities have been blindly pursuing the idea of internationalization
For example there have been a lot of news reports recently saying that the University of Hong Kong has (canceled ;) the Tiananmen School Festival . In fact this is the same kind of logic
The more competitive a school is the more places it will be given and the so called competitiveness is determined by the University Grants Committee
This is very saddening as it will lead to the diversification of schools and the elimination of many subjects that have contributed a lot to society . This is very saddening
Therefore there is a serious problem with the funding proposed by the University Grants Committee
Apart from the study funding the structure of UGC itself is also very backward
The Education Council EDB is actually a subordinate of the United Kingdom EDB which is a semi official body with the Secretariat being the Governments institution
The Government is responsible to the public opinion agencies rather than the current undemocratic government
We all know very well that the funding for the Chief Executives Office is a tobacco of the university . We must monitor the institutions under the Chief Executives Office through the means of public opinion monitoring so as to ensure that the funding for the university will not become one of the tricks of the regime to threaten the university
The Education Council EDB has wasted no time in studying the funding proposal . It needs to make a drastic reform
Therefore I have proposed two amendments in this session in the hope that Members will seriously consider the current recommendations made by the Education Council
Next I will speak on the project of activating Kowloon East
Kowloon East was proposed in 2011 with a view to assisting and monitoring the development of Kowloon East and transforming Kowloon East from an old industrial district into the second core business district of Hong Kong
The objective is very clear . The Government wants to transform many industrial buildings in Kowloon East into core commercial districts through the planning of old industrial estates . This means commercialization of the entire district
(how ever ) the Government is well aware that many of the sites in those districts are not only industrial buildings but also residential buildings that (cannot ) afford the rents of commercial buildings
For this reason the Government has adopted many other methods such as arts or local creation in order to send a message to the outside world that we are not talking about the group of people who used to live in the same community in Kowloon East
The ecological environment in Hong Kong is very rich and many people from the community and other industries come to live here
As mentioned by Mr LEUNG Chun – ying the policy on revitalizing industrial buildings is very successful . From the perspective of property speculation it is certainly successful as more than 80 % of the revitalized industrial buildings have been turned into offices and some of them have even been turned into Grade A office buildings . In the eyes of the Government the Government can successfully transform the industrial buildings into landmines and turn them from dark industrial districts into a colourful commercial district where people can live and work in peace and contentment
(how ever ) from another perspective the policy of revitalizing industrial buildings is a total failure to accommodate industries that (cannot ) survive in the commercial sector
Let us take a look at industrial buildings in Kowloon East or other districts . The people who used to live in industrial buildings have many different industries and uses
If we look at the situation in more detail people who (cannot ) rent a flat in the commercial district in the area are not only in need of music but also of many other industries which are exactly what Hong Kong needs such as the local community and the people of Hong Kong . Many of them are living in those places
As we all know there are not many places in the commercial buildings or shopping arcades and those places which are large enough are mostly used for the sports of the nobles such as sword fighting because the users are very rich and they do not mind paying high rents or tuition fees
The Government has now started to promote the use of industrial buildings in land leases for the purpose of incorporating them into art studios . But such work is only for home use . It is still not acceptable to let people use a studio of the Government because it has to follow the present stringent and complicated procedures
Is it not necessary for Hong Kong to rent large venues with sufficient resources or even rent performance units rented by the Government?
In Hong Kong the reason why people say that the industry is monotonous is that there is simply no room for people who (cannot ) afford the business to develop their careers and professionalism . The Government will not think of other ways to assist these people . Instead it only knows to turn things around and suppress them . A good example is the hidden agenda
As I said yesterday and I will say it again today Hong Kong needs a cultural policy to enable those who (cannot ) afford the rents of the rural areas to live in the same district
In order to make a living they have to set up a shopping mall and build a unit in a shopping mall in the middle of (no where ) . This is not the way to do it . They have to set up a marriage circle and an ecological circle
For example why is the sound system of a music hall in Kwun Tong so special?
There are places to sell musical instruments and to make musical instruments as well as performing venues . Many people of the same social class have a cultural background
If we take a look at the cultural and creative circles in Taiwan and Beijing we will see that many cultural and creative sites developed by the industrial and manufacturing sectors have created an ecological circle . Hong Kong should learn from these places . At present the most talked topic of revitalization of industrial buildings in Hong Kong is the demolition of buildings
If the Government really wants to develop the area just as Carrie LAM said eight years ago that it hoped that people joining the band would not have to secretly touch the area the Government should draw up a park instead of having it be used for property speculation . It should allow artists and musicians to live in the area at a price they can afford and move in with a whole ecological chain and the whole atmosphere to promote the development and creativity of people . Only in this way can the area be developed
If we look at the whole project of activating Kowloon East we will see that it is nothing but a mudslide
The Government has adopted a number of different approaches to revitalize industrial buildings . For example a new commercial building will be replaced by a core commercial area but the remaining industrial buildings will be driven away because the rents are too high to afford and the people who have been driven away by the industrial buildings are actually in dire straits
This is a very big problem . This is not simply a problem of one or two live houses and one or two musicians failing to apply for a licence but a mistake in the (over all ) government policy
In order to develop their business they have neglected the people they should take care of
In the whole society what is impossible to be free from training classes on ping pong? . What is impossible to be free from the formation of musical groups? . What is impossible to be free from the formation of small and micro musical units?
If they only know how to organize big events and grand concerts please do not blame them for saying that Hong Kong is a cultural desert . This is the first step of the Governments policy
In my view activating Kowloon East is not only a matter of rent . The policy of regional development can also highlight the nature of the land withdrawal
The Government has made such a beautiful back door and turned the bridge (up side ) down . The cultural and arts units in Kowloon East are actually very opposed to it because the Government has spent hundreds of thousands of dollars on the construction of such a building . The Government only thinks that a prosperous business district should be built in a way that will enable it to do better in some areas but it has actually neglected how the users there treat the whole place
All in all the implementation of Kowloon East will only lead to a dead end and the people living in Kowloon East will not be well treated
A more important issue is the Governments (over all ) cultural policy
In the case of the Heater Agenda many of the problems mentioned by the Government including fire safety and licence issues have been addressed by all means to the best of our ability
Therefore I propose this amendment to cut the annual expenditure for the commissioning of Kowloon East . I am doing so to condemn the commissioning of Kowloon East . Kowloon East is a place where people who only know how to make money and drive away workers from industrial buildings can turn them into commercial buildings and offices and financial institutions can be built . It is a place where narrow industries can afford to live and the industrial buildings in Hong Kong have been ignored
This applies not only to music units . Perhaps you do not like listening to music but I believe you also think that Hong Kong people need the support of other different facilities
Therefore I move this motion . Second I wish to speak on head 60 on the Highways Department―the expenditure on the reduction of the expenditure of the Hong Kong – Zhuhai – Macao Bridge Works Management Department
Many friends of HZMB have also mentioned that since its commissioning in 2011 the number of casualties has been devastating with 14 234 cases
Certainly LD will say that this seems to be lower than the average figure but do Members understand that there are actually many cases of concealment?
As long as the Government can achieve a figure lower than the average the Government can tell the public that it does not need to deduct points or money and it can continue with its works in a reasonable manner
Over the past six years the Police have investigated 1 400 cases . Over the past six years the Police have investigated 1 400 cases and investigated several hundred cases every year . It seems that the Police have done a very good job
Is it not ridiculous for the Government to conduct a thorough investigation and then tell the public that it has done its job?
If a inspection area does not have any force and only sees the operators acting in a drama or acting in a comedy or setting up a scene for the public to watch it will be useless even if the inspection is conducted 140 000 times let alone 1 400 times
Therefore the 14 234 cases are absolutely a cold mountain . The Government and the contractors have been playing the deaf ear and made the employees suffer . The employees who are suffering are those who are truly working in high risk places
The Governments regulatory power is inadequate and the whole industry is in a bad mood . At present 10 people have died not a small number . This is not caused by the cost of construction works
How can the authorities not step up their monitoring efforts by conducting more special inspections improving the atmosphere and imposing more mandatory monitoring measures?
Therefore I agree that the management fees should be reduced
Over the years in the Legislative Council of various terms of office the pressure of the establishment camp has been on the lower side and ridiculous remarks have been coming out one after another
Recently we have seen that Ding Ding who is very smart and does not know how to use a computer has issued a document in his own hard drive claiming that he has made an open report
Today we can see that Mr LUK Chung – hung has taken the lead of the pro – establishment camp to beat up the democrats and ruin the Budget
Will the Government really put forward its views on the existing social problems and put forward a solution?
Just now Mr LUK really said something quite terrifying . He said Dr Fernando CHEUNG had a conflict of conscience . The Government has increased its expenditure but it (cannot ) solve social problems so Dr LUK said Dr Fernando CHEUNG had a conflict of consciousness
I believe Mr LUK is the one who has a sense of contradiction . He should know what is going on in society
The Government has been scolding the Government in every possible way but it has not even touched on the crux of the problem
The Governments Budget has adopted public money to solve problems and public money is also involved in the allocation of financial resources
In the past the Gini Coefficient in Hong Kong has always been at its highest but not its lowest and it has always been at the (fore front ) in advanced regions . The protection of human rights and freedoms in Hong Kong has also been declining year after year
We can see that the ranking of freedom of the press has risen from the 12 or 18 ranking 15 years ago in 2002 to the 73 ranking now
We can see that the electoral procedures and diversities of Hong Kong are very low among many other places in the world and the ranking of Hong Kong is even lower
It is precisely because of our electoral system that when we discuss the Budget now some of the views which truly represent the majority opinion of the public (cannot ) be reflected in the Budget
This is what we have to consider
This is exactly the great predicament that Hong Kong is facing in respect of wealth and social justice that we (cannot ) see or manifest in this Council
Therefore when there is no comprehensive universal suffrage in the Legislative Council before a comprehensive constitutional reform takes place the views of the opposition camp will definitely continue to exist and the amendments will definitely continue to exist
Members oppose the Budget and the unfair parliamentary system in which so many people can enter the legislature by zero vote thus leading to the distortion of certain views in the legislature and unreasonably representing the views of certain people
Before the adjournment of the debate on this motion the Budget is destined to be passed on to someone else and this person is definitely not the most needed person in the community or the largest number of people
Regarding the amendments to the Budget just now many Members from the pro – establishment camp have expressed their views . For example they have expressed their views on the anti – reunification waste charging scheme which was proposed by Mr Martin LIAO and Mr LUK Chung hung
Mr LUK said that the amount of money spent was very limited at 640 million . He then suggested that the money should be shared with the country
I would like to clarify one point . First the 640 million is not a small amount . Mr LUK Chung hung said that he worked for the peoples livelihood and the workers but he actually spent the money on the so called celebration activities . He does not care at all
(how ever ) after 20 years of reunification no one has ever felt any sense of reunification because the previous Communist regime had oppressed the people of Hong Kong . The values we pursue in Hong Kong and the things we have learnt from education such as human rights and freedom have all been violated
In the past 20 years what is worth being happy about when Hong Kong has been going backwards step by step?
This is precisely why we say that the return of sovereignty may not necessarily require a celebration but a review
The upward mobility of young people is inadequate . The monthly salary of a university graduate is no different from that of 20 years ago
All these are the indicators that will bring the society to a standstill and make people who are about to become the bottom of the middle class lose their motivation and confidence
Therefore based on the above I oppose this years Budget
Just now a few Members including the class monitor Mr Martin LIAO and Dr Junius HO have expressed their views on the discussion of the Budget on 1 January . They said that filibustering was not a bad thing and that it was a waste of (tax payers ) money and time
So far the biggest problem is that the Government lacks a sense of accountability
When we discuss the efficiency of a local government or whether its governance is smooth in the world we have a few very important principles including the accountability of the government and whether it can adequately respond to the aspirations of the public
In many studies the Hong Kong Government scored very low marks in this regard
The reason why so many Members are still against the Governments policy or the reason why we oppose to include these 50 items in the Schedule is that the Government has not reflected appropriate accountability and listened to the aspirations of the public in the past so as to balance the views of various parties and formulate a suitable policy
In the past we have focused on the pros and cons of different policies of the Hong Kong Federation of Trade Unions
The root cause of the problems with so many policies is that the entire Government is not operating smoothly . The entire Government is unable to attract different people different Members and different public views from the structure . As a result the structural problem with the formulation of policies is that the Government will deviate its interests from those of consortiums real estate developers and the Mainland
This is exactly the predicament we are facing
Even if a motion is not widely welcomed by the public and not supported by the majority of the democratic camp what policies and strategies can the Legislative Council resist?
This is precisely the opportunity arising from filibustering in recent years and it is also full of social factors
If Dr Junius HO or Mr Martin LIAO think that we should not speak in the light of policies and that we should not voice our views in the Council debate this is a civilized reverse action
Mr LEUNG I would like to express my views on the remarks made by Dr Junius HO and Mr Martin LIAO just now
Next I will talk about the reasons for opposing the inclusion of the Schedule in the Bill
In fact this Council has not only changed its course through the views of some Members but also through the entire Council including the discussion on the Budget and the ruling made by Mr LEUNG
During the discussion on the Budget this year we only had about 60 hours to discuss 185 amendments . But each debate only lasted about five to seven hours and we simply did not have enough time for Members who had proposed amendments to clearly state their reasons and justifications
I have proposed 17 amendments in this Budget but I have only spoken on 11 items of business at the Committee stage . Many members of the public are very concerned about the urgent need for social reform including issues relating to the Working Family and Student Financial Assistance Office and the Radio Television Hong Kong . I (cannot ) speak on these issues in this speech
The third motion is about poverty alleviation welfare and health care elderly care public health and population . I (cannot ) have sufficient time to queue up to speak on this motion
As I have said just now we have to surrender our sovereignty after 20 years of reunification . But now when we discuss government policies we find that the Legislative Councils ability to monitor the executive especially its financial monitoring is still very poor
In the face of such executive hegemony and the total refusal to accept the voices of the Legislative Council I am really disappointed with the Governments previous ability to monitor the financial situation and its attitude in handling the matter
In the meantime the Government has incurred the biggest expenditure in the history of infrastructure development and the expenditure on sovereignty surrender has increased by 29.1 % over the past 20 years
Even though we know that many amendments have been rejected in the past we still have to seize the time to speak up and oppose to include these subheads in the Budget
The Government has never seriously looked at the questions raised by the Council on the Budget . In fact the Government has never given a serious response to the questions in the Bills Committee . It has not seriously evaded the questions on the written submission and it has been delayed and delayed again at the meetings
In the past many public officers were derelict of their duties and responsibilities . As a result the Council is now facing many problems and difficulties which (cannot ) be addressed by the Panel at its regular meetings
In our view the Schedule should not be included in the Budget because the whole process and the whole Council (cannot ) operate effectively
For example the Democratic Alliance for the Betterment and Progress of Hong Kong spends more than 100 million a year to encourage electors to come to Hong Kong . This will bring great harm to the (over all ) development of democracy and the legislature
Over the past few years we have been filibustering and we have been treating this Council in a strategic manner . We have adopted different directions to speak out things that are reasonable and based on facts and comply with the principles of democracy but we have all along been subjected to the criticism of the proletarian camp
(how ever ) on behalf of the public I would like to ask what the establishment camp has done in the entire Budget (with out ) making any speech or asking for any payback?
Have they ever cried out for the people loudly?
Or is it just like Dr Junius HO who came to the Legislative Council to ask questions about his family members in an ugly manner and then taught his children a lesson by taking selfies in court rooms?
As elected Members who have to face such an overbearing system and the many policies that harm peoples livelihood how can we still support the Budget?
The executive authorities are poisonous and opaque . They are also a kind of fraud in the legislature which has rendered it impossible to monitor the Council . The only thing we can do is to use our opposition votes to make it clear to the public that we do not only oppose the Budget but also oppose this kind of government and legislature structure that harms the public
Deputy President in this debate session I will first comment on the (over all ) performance of the Department of Justice DoJ
The rule of law is a very important value of Hong Kong and I believe all of us will agree with this
The rule of law is an important element of Hong Kongs electoral system and it is also an important factor for maintaining Hong Kongs international status and competitiveness
The Mainland has long been a hot spot for innovation and technology . But the Cyberport also told me that if Mainland products wanted to go international they had to come back to Hong Kong first
When Mainland companies want to go international they have to come to Hong Kong first because Hong Kongs brand name is the best in the world and its reputation and system can give people confidence
Why is the rule of law in Hong Kong so important?
Over the past few years the Department of Justice has indeed failed to do a good job in defending the rule of law . It has even allowed some unreasonable moves made by Beijing to interfere and undermine the rule of law in Hong Kong but the Department of Justice has failed to do a good job in defending the rule of law or playing the gatekeeper
The second incident was about the recent interpretation of the Basic Law and 3 000 people took to the streets
Let me cite the interpretation of the Basic Law as an example this time . We all know very well that the Beijing Government has interpreted the Basic Law in response to the many oath disputes and Hong Kong independence scandals in the past and has then taken a drastic step to implement the interpretation of the Basic Law
(how ever ) let us look at the interpretation of the Basic Law . It reads The Court of Final Appeal of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region will seek an explanation from the Standing Committee of the National Peoples Congress NPCSC on the relevant provisions
Why are the legal sector and the community so opposed to the Bill?
Neither the court nor the Court of Final Appeal nor the Government has said that NPCSC has exercised its power arbitrarily to bypass the procedure to interpret the Basic Law
More importantly DoJ has made no comment whatsoever in this regard
The Secretary for Justice is absent and so are her subordinates and even the Chief Secretary for Administration is absent . This will indeed make people question whether the Secretary for Justice is really a neutral defender of the rule of law in Hong Kong
Members of the public have raised this very important question
In fact we all know very well that the last interpretation simply did not comply with the existing provisions of the Basic Law
More importantly it seems that the community the Department of Justice and the establishment camp all hold a sword in their hands to cut off people with different stances
This is a great warning to society not only on the rule of law but also on our core values and civic values
I believe we all know very well that in a society a system should not attack or harm any individual for the sake of its stance
If this is something that (every one ) believes in and that (every one ) considers to be a basic human right we will not give up on our approach because of their stance
Even if you see a person on the street who has a different political view from yours and no matter how ridiculous he is you will still save him when you see that he may be in danger
This is our core understanding of systems and values
(how ever ) the entire interpretation of the Basic Law scandal is not the case . He basically used this tactic to attack the Secretary for Justice when he said something that was in conflict with the basic ideology of the country . This tactic was very rare in the past
If the Secretary for Justice trusts the rule of law and the system and believes that the system is meant to protect the rights of (every one ) and not to allow people with power in the regime to arbitrarily abuse the system she should have spoken up
He should say that although his idea is different from that of the Beijing Government and XI Jinping he should be allowed to participate in the election because the right to be elected is a core human right and a fundamental right
The harm done to our system this time around is that the State can completely put its ideology in the interest of the people
This is no longer a matter of distribution of interests . It is no longer a matter of distribution of interests . This is a blatant threat to the police force . This is a blatant use of ideology to advocate internal struggle and suppress dissidents
As we all know if the interpretation is not easy it will distort the rules of the game and make it difficult for the decision makers to make
Many people in the community say that we should abide by the law . They do not understand whether the law is in line with social morality . They do not understand what procedures are involved in the enactment of the law and whether it is very cruel to allow a group of people of the Standing Committee of the National Peoples Congress NPCSC to interfere and meddle in politics
DoJ has not explained this to the public at all
What is more important is to achieve justice by law
Why does it not mean that the law can bring justice? . It is because the law may cause harm to society in the light of its achievements
Second the law may also be amended in the light of the suppression of dissidents and things they dislike by those in power
We usually call this a draconian law including section 2 of the Public Order Ordinance
Therefore lawful treatment or the practice of acting in accordance with the law may not necessarily reflect social justice
Why does the Department of Justice not take the initiative to make public statements so that the public can have a clearer understanding of the rule of law and the core values?
This has aroused a great deal of doubt . Is the Department of Justice simply a tool of the regime? . Is it no longer independent of the regime to monitor whether a person has acted in accordance with the law?
He has become a knife in the hand of the regime . We really want to see DoJs position and role in society
The Police Force is the biggest disciplined force of the Hong Kong Government and it is also the hope of the public that they can protect their own safety
After ICAC came into operation there was a large scale of opposition but the situation has gradually improved
How come we have seen a straight line drop in the popularity of the public opinion charts of the University of Hong Kong in recent years?
The reason is that the political regime is condoning the Police Force to do such things and they are its henchmen and the senior staff of the Police Force have not reflected on themselves at all
Therefore please do not criticize him for his poor performance because many things are objective . His score has dropped and he has received more complaints . He is now gradually gaining a higher chance of winning the News Award
Before he went to jail the Correctional Services Department CSD sent him a letter to tell him that he had no evidence and he should not say that he had really changed his mind although the director said that he had a very gentle tone
(how ever ) we all know very well that the use of gentle words is a great threat
The Correctional Services Department CSD has refused to admit that the incident is true
Secretary thank you very much for visiting the construction industry in the past two weeks . Ir Dr LO (Wai-) kwok and I are here to thank you for your concern about the industry especially the construction industry
I understand that these long working hours include 15 days of work in March and the Government will consider granting them six months of allowance in the past few months . We hope to receive the information as soon as possible
The Secretarys main reply only says that the Government is making improvements to the road situation and that there is a road for them to travel on but they do not have the chance to survive because of the smell of air
On behalf of the real estate and construction sectors I strongly support this proposal . At the same time I also strongly support the National Security Law because it can bring social stability to Hong Kong
Through social stability and political stability our country leaders have asked us to resolve our own deep social conflicts
I would like to ask the Chief Executive how she can achieve stability in the third aspect so that Hong Kong can return to the normal path of prosperity
The question raised by Mrs Regina IP is very clear . It is about how the MTR Corporation Limited MTRCL would monitor the situation before the incident because it is the person in charge . The Secretary has used many words to explain but he has failed to answer Mrs Regina IPs question
President my supplementary question is about the responsibilities of the MTR Corporation Limited MTRCL before the occurrence of the incident because this involves a liability issue
The Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority was established in 1977 which was the time of the British Hong Kong Government
Nowadays we have to take care of our own people and the education system of Hong Kong should provide quality education to local students . We should not apply the old concept of the year 1977 to the operation of Hong Kong in 2017
In respect of the government structure the Government has listened to the views of various sectors on setting up a Culture Sports and Tourism Bureau
As recently mentioned by the Chief Executive it has become an issue that (cannot ) be avoided to rethink the composition of the SAR Government into the most favourable for the future development of Hong Kong
The incumbent Government will listen to the views of the public extensively and make every effort to do a good job in the relevant preparation work so as to enable the new Government to consider and take forward the project
I look forward to hearing the valuable views of Honourable Members
In respect of cultural exchange the SAR Government has been strongly supporting Hong Kong arts groups and Hong Kong artists to perform and organize exhibitions outside Hong Kong . Financial support has been gradually increased since 18 and 19 and the recurrent funding has been increased to 50 million per year
The funding is mainly used in three major areas 1 organizing Hong Kong Weeks in major Mainland and (over seas ) cities . 2 providing funding and subsidies to the Hong Kong Economic and Trade Offices in Mainland and (over seas ) cities for organizing cultural and arts events supported by Hong Kong arts groups and artists . And 3 providing funding to the Hong Kong Arts Council and the Hong Kong Arts Council for organizing cultural and arts events
In addition since July 2016 the SAR Government has added a Culture Exchange Unit to the Beijing Office . The Home Affairs Bureau has also provided funding for other Economic and Trade Offices ETOs to recruit local staff to work in culture exchange including assisting in the organization of cultural exchange activities and connecting with different places in Hong Kong
Mr MA mentioned in his motion the proposal of setting up an arts exchange fund
In the past the Home Affairs Bureau has also set up the Arts Development Fund for promoting cultural exchanges between Hong Kong and other places
Since April 2020 the administration of the Art Development Fund has been entrusted to the Hong Kong Arts Development Council HKADC to provide one stop services directly to arts groups and artists so as to facilitate their application for funding
As regards the proposal of providing tax concessions if arts groups or sports organizations are registered as charitable organizations under section 88 of the Inland Revenue Ordinance they are eligible to apply for tax concessions for donations to such organizations
If there are commercial entities sponsoring cultural and sports activities to promote their own businesses the funding can be regarded as market promotion expenditure and tax deduction can be applied for
President under the National 14th Five Year Plan Hong Kongs new positioning is a cultural arts exchange centre for China and the rest of the world
Hong Kongs cultural integration with China and the rest of the world has built up a wide international network over the years and brought together outstanding talents from both the Mainland and (over seas ) as well as the local community . Hong Kong has the necessary conditions to become an influential platform for promoting Chinese culture in the international community
The HKSAR Government will consolidate and give play to Hong Kongs unique advantages in various aspects and make use of the platform of Hong Kong to bring Chinese culture to the world and promote Hong Kongs development into an arts exchange centre for cultural exchanges between China and the rest of the world
Hong Kong possesses world class cultural and arts facilities and cities
The West Kowloon Cultural District WKCD will be the new landmark of Hong Kongs culture . The Hong Kong Palace Museum and the Hong Kong Palace Museum a museum showcasing the contemporary visual arts and the real Chinese arts and culture will be opened in November this year and mid next year respectively
These two museums together with the Hong Kong Museum of Art which reopened in 2019 will be established on the Victoria Harbour coast and will form a organic art cultural museum cluster with the Hong Kong Cultural Centre and the West Kowloon Arts Centre
In addition the Kowloon East Cultural Centre which is expected to be commissioned in phases in 2023 will become an important venue for arts and technology . In addition the Performing Arts Complex in the West Kowloon Cultural District is expected to be completed in 2024 and will inject new impetus to performing arts
As for cultural events Hong Kongs arts sector has long been well known internationally
Since the first visit to Hong Kong in 2013 the Barcelona Art Exhibition Hong Kong Exhibition has become an international city of visual arts in less than 10 years and attracts more than 200 top artists from many countries and regions around the world to participate in the exhibition every year
More than 100 contemporary art lovers around the world exhibit new artists at Art Central every year
The SAR Government has been holding the Hong Kong Week since 2019 in the Mainland . The first two Hong Kong Weeks were held in Shanghai and Guangzhou respectively in 2019 and 2021 and the response from the public was very enthusiastic
We have also planned to hold the next Hong Kong Week in Wuhan in 2022
In addition the Greater Bay Area is a new market that we attach great importance to . Since 2018 LCSD has arranged a total of 40 local productions of over 110 production runs in seven cities in the Greater Bay Area for online and offline performances
The Government will continue to establish closer cooperation with (over seas ) cultural and arts organizations and strengthen cooperation in cultural exchanges with the Mainland with a view to consolidating and strengthening the status of cultural and arts exchange centres
President Hong Kongs art market is very vast and is one of the three largest art auction markets in the world
In 2020 Hong Kongs share of global art auction markets accounted for 23.2 % surpassing London for the first time
In 2020 the total value of Hong Kongs art collection and antiques reached HK33.6 billion which doubled that in 2017
We will continue to make good use of Hong Kongs advantages in low tax regime and high efficiency services to promote the development of the relevant industries
The integration of arts and creativity into innovation and technology is the trend of the world
The SAR Government has set up an interdepartmental task force on arts technology to formulate strategies and measures for the development and promotion of arts technology . A total of 100 million has also been earmarked in different funds and schemes to assist the industry in making applications in one go
Apart from funding the SAR Government will also enhance the existing venue facilities and the East Kowloon Cultural Centre which will be commissioned soon will be designated as a venue for promoting the development of arts and technology . It will be used as a base for nurturing arts and technology development through the most advanced IT facilities and trial venues
The SAR Government will continue to allocate resources to train talents encourage more young people to participate in culture and the arts and make use of innovative technology to explore new room for creative work to bring new faces to the arts and creative industries
The Government has been working closely with a number of cooperation partners including the Hong Kong Academy of Arts the universities of Hong Kong the Hong Kong Arts Development Council the major arts groups and the West Kowloon Cultural District Authority to nurture outstanding arts talents and arts administrators
With the gradual implementation of a number of major cultural facilities in the coming few years I believe that more opportunities will be created for the arts and cultural sectors and related professionals in Hong Kong
All in all the SAR Government will endeavour to strengthen and give play to Hong Kongs unique advantages including building world class cultural facilities creating diversified cultural spaces strengthening our relationship with (over seas ) arts and cultural institutions strengthening our cooperation in cultural exchanges with the Mainland and endeavouring to develop culture arts technology and talents so as to provide more opportunities for the development of Hong Kong
I will respond to Members speeches
The National 14th 5 May Plan proposes to enhance external cultural exchanges actively develop external cultural trade and develop (over seas ) cultural markets enhance the influence of Chinese culture and strengthen the modern cultural industry system
We thank the Central Authorities for accepting the Chief Executives proposal and supporting the development of HKSAR into a centre for cultural arts and exchange in the Mainland and (over seas )
The SAR Government is very grateful to the country for its support for Hong Kong . This is not only a recognition of Hong Kong but also a target for Hong Kong and will bring huge development opportunities to the industry
In my opening remarks I mentioned the unique advantages of Hong Kongs development into a centre for cultural and arts exchanges between China and the rest of the world . In the future our key work is to make good use of the support of the country keep upgrading the software and hardware of Hong Kong and give full play to the strengths of Hong Kong so as to tie in with the contribution of the country to the Motherland
In terms of policy we will adopt a number of major areas including hardware development the construction of more cultural facilities and the creation of more diversified cultural spaces . In terms of software we will strengthen exchanges and cooperation with Mainland and (over seas ) cultures enhance relations and use technology to nurture talents
Regarding the Governments reform structure Members have fully expressed their views and provided their views just now . The SAR Government will actively and thoroughly consider them
In respect of financial assistance as I said in my opening remarks the SAR Government has already provided a series of dedicated resources for cultural exchange
In July this year the National Arts Foundation announced that it would commence in Hong Kong its work on the declaration of funds on projects in 2022 . This is the first time that the funding will be open to local arts organizations and artists so as to reflect the concern of the Motherland for them and provide them with more room for broadening their scope of development
In terms of industrial policies many famous international auction firms have been out of touch with Hong Kong in recent years . With the vigorous development of the art exhibition industry Hong Kongs market for art auction is on par with that of London and New York
Art as an investment product is also a new trend in asset wealth management . We believe that the development of ARC and Hong Kong as an asset management centre will be complementary to each other
In recent years a large number of new arts and cultural venues have been built in the Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao Greater Bay Area
The Leisure and Cultural Services Department LCSD has conducted more than 100 local performances in seven cities in the Greater Bay Area in a topside and downstream manner since 2018
We will continue and step up our efforts in this regard in the future
Hong Kong and other cities in the Greater Bay Area share similar geographical location and the same family of lies and pain . Interdistrictal performances can give play to the advantages of proximity to the people and create more interdistrict cultural and tourism activities . They can also connect with the Belt and Road Initiative especially the Chinese communities in Southeast Asia and promote the connection of peoples hearts thereby creating a new cultural pattern for the Greater Bay Area city groups
We will also enhance cooperation and exchanges with arts and cultural institutions in various places through organizing various international conferences
In 2022 Hong Kong will host the International Arts Council Intermediate Exhibition which is the first Asian city to successfully host two international exhibitions
LCSD also plans to host the third Hong Kong Museum of Culture Hong Kong Museum of Culture and the 22 Asian Museum of Culture in Hong Kong at the end of next year to promote cultural cooperation between Hong Kong and the neighbouring regions of Asia
Regarding the immigration policy the Leisure and Cultural Services Department LCSD has supported many local arts groups and artists to perform in the Greater Bay Area since 2018
To sum up the experience gained over the past two years and the views of stakeholders from both places we expect that the implementation will be further enhanced to promote exchanges within the Greater Bay Area and promote Hong Kong arts groups to enter the huge market in the Greater Bay Area
The Government will continue to maintain close communication with the stakeholders and explore ways to facilitate the performance of the Hong Kong arts community in performing in the Mainland such as streamlining the administrative vetting procedures for performing or performing in other cities in the Greater Bay Area by Hong Kong arts groups and relaying views to the Mainland authorities in a timely manner
All along ImmD will provide immigration convenience in individual cases when processing work visas or entry application for short service activities in Hong Kong including expediting the application procedures and giving priority to emergency applications so as to cater for the actual needs of the participants and the organizers
Deputy President cultural work is also a youth work . In education the SAR Government has all along supported the training of cultural and creative talents and actively promoted Chinese culture the history of the country and the national affairs in primary and secondary schools
Since assuming office in 2017 the current term Government has been committed to promoting the development and exchange of arts and culture and providing substantial resources
Cultural and arts policies should keep abreast of the times and adopt a timely approach
Today we are very glad to hear the valuable views of Honourable Members which is very desirable
The SAR Government expects to work together with all of you to achieve the goal of Hong Kong becoming a cultural arts exchange hub in the Mainland
Thank you President
President first of all I would like to thank Mr Frank CHAN for moving the motion and the 12 Members who have spoken for providing valuable views on sports policy and innovative proposals
The frequent promotion of physical activities has a great impact on the health of people of different ages
DAB has been striving to promote sports revitalization for people of different ages . In particular regular sports for the elderly can help to enhance the physical functions of elderly people maintain a cheerful mind and enable them to live a happy life and benefit from the benefits over the years
Next I will briefly introduce various policy initiatives and measures of the Home Affairs Bureau to encourage the elderly to participate in sports activities
We organize over 700 activities every year and the number of beneficiaries is over 21 000
To enhance the interest of the elderly in participating in sports activities LCSD has organized in particular sports training classes in the community which are popular with the elderly and which they are interested in participating in such as swimming Tai Chi eight sets yoga goalball and golf etc
To encourage people to exercise more during the epidemic LCSD has launched the One Stop Online Resource Centre for Entertainment in particular to enable people to watch demonstration videos of various sports at home and participate in online sports training courses through interactive practice
Apart from organizing various types of sports events in the community and online the Government also offers different types of recreational facilities on a concessionary basis to meet the interests and needs of the elderly
For example people aged 60 or above can enjoy concessionary fees for using sports grounds sports grounds sports grounds and swimming pools etc . at designated times under the Leisure and Cultural Services Department LCSD
Currently over 1 600 parks and playgrounds in Hong Kong are equipped with fitness facilities suitable for elderly people to exercise . Over 500 recreational venues are equipped with elderly fitness spaces providing over 2 700 sets of fitness facilities for the elderly
President healthy living can effectively enhance the physical fitness of the public and good physical fitness can help the public to improve their quality of life
In order to keep abreast of the latest physical fitness of the public and their sports habits we have launched a new round of Community Physical Fitness Survey for the whole territory in 2005 and 2011
In order to better fit in with the present trend of ageing of the (over all ) population we have specifically increased the age of participation in the survey from the last survey at 69 to 79 on a large scale
Through regular and standardized physical fitness tests the Government can establish a systematic database on physical fitness data to better understand the physical fitness of the public including the elderly so as to formulate the most appropriate and effective sports promotion policy
The survey was divided into several parts including basic personal and physical information their living habits sports mode and physical fitness tests
The inquiry has already commenced and is expected to be completed early next year
President the Home Affairs Bureau is very concerned about the health of the public and encourages them to exercise more . The Government allocates over 6.5 billion annually for sports development and 5.4 billion is used for promoting sports in the community
In addition the Government has been fully committed to implementing the Five Year Plan for Sports and Recreation Facilities and earmarked 20 billion to launch 26 key projects to increase and improve sports facilities
We will continue to organize more and more diversified sports activities on the community and the Internet for the participation of the elderly and provide them with more and more appropriate recreational facilities
Through the Community Physical Fitness Survey for the whole community we will establish and roll out corresponding measures as soon as possible in accordance with the physical conditions and conditions of the elderly
We have to consider the (over all ) situation
President the public have the freedom to express their views make comments and assemble but they must do so in a peaceful and lawful manner
The Police have all along been committed to assisting members of the public in conducting peaceful order and lawful public activities . They also have the statutory responsibility to safeguard public safety and public order . They will assess the situation at the scene make professional judgments and adopt appropriate measures to safeguard law and order and ensure peace in society
Since early June there have been over 700 public demonstrations and processions in Hong Kong and many of them have ended with violence
Among them were acts of illegal acts committed by rioters including arbitrary blocking of roads arson attacks on police lines throwing of bricks and petrol bombs and violent assaults on police officers serious vandalism of MTR and Light Rail facilities and traffic lights vandalism of shops and crazy assaults on people of different opinions as well as arson attacks
The Police must take action to bring them to justice or disperse the rioters and control the scene so as to restore public safety and public order as soon as possible
The purpose of using tear gas is to stop violent people from continuing to charge at the Police lines or to stop their violent acts thereby creating a safe distance and reducing physical contact so as to avoid serious injuries . Those who have been attacked should leave the scene immediately
In general smoking tear gas will make the skin and eyes feel hot and the nose and throat stimulated but the impact is only brief
As long as the area affected by tear gas is removed it can be restored in a short time
Like some foreign modernized police forces the Police will deploy police dogs to assist in the execution of missions as and when necessary
Police dogs can effectively assist police officers in handling violent acts unlawful assemblies and situations in which public order is being violated
Under such circumstances the specific duties of police officers usually include providing support to (front line ) police officers in handling violent and (sub standard ) acts reinforcing the police blockade lines and guarding designated buildings or sites etc
After the Police have disbanded the unlawful assembly the Police can effectively stop the people on the scene from committing unlawful acts that undermine social order
My reply to the various parts of Mr KONGs question is as follows 1 In the past three years there was no case of unnatural death of a police officer in a detention camp after his return to Hong Kong
Second and third as mentioned above after conducting risk assessments in respect of public activities and the scene the Police will as one of the action plans appropriately deploy police dogs to the scene to assist (front line ) officers in enforcing the law
Police dogs undergo rigorous training and are regularly tested to ensure adequate nutrition and rest and that their physical condition is in order to carry out the duties entrusted to them by the Police
Generally speaking the units assigned to police officers are the charge force and the Police Region
In the operation the Police will work closely with various (front line ) police officers to maintain public order and public order as well as cope with emergencies
Since June this year police officers have been using police dogs to assist in the handling of the numbers of public demonstrations processions assemblies and other activities
In the recent months the Police have not received any report of any concern about police dogs being used for hunting purposes by Link REIT officers
The Police have attached great importance to the health of police dogs and their working environment
All police dogs are led by dedicated police officers in the form of one person one dog . Upon issuing an order the dedicated police officers will cater for all the needs of the police dogs they are responsible for and establish a close relationship with the police dogs they are responsible for
Tainters will always be concerned about and pay attention to the physical condition and needs of the police dog . If they find that the police dog is not behaving well they will immediately arrange for a vet to carry out examination and treatment for the police dog
As a matter of fact every police dog keeper is very concerned about the physical condition of the dog . They will immediately pay attention to the dogs physical reactions during and after the operation to provide the dog with appropriate care and take care of its various physiological needs
In addition to following up any ill health of police dogs and hiring a vet they will maintain close liaison with the Agriculture Fisheries and Conservation Department AFCD and other relevant departments to ensure the health of the chosen dogs
President the Police certainly attach great importance to the health of police dogs and every police dog keeper has a strong relationship with his or her own police dog as if it is a partner of the police dog . The Police are very concerned about the health of the police dog
Certainly we will draw reference from (over seas ) practices when deploying police officers and some Western countries have also deployed police officers to participate in the handling of mass incidents by police officers . The approach adopted is similar to that adopted in Hong Kong
The first reality is that if there is any report or reaction from the Police we will immediately seek the guidance and follow up . This is the first point
Secondly as I said in the main reply just now AFCD and I have communicated with and held seminars in this regard
Foreign countries use police officers to deal with these incidents and their equipment is the same as that in Hong Kong
As I have said many times every Link REIT member is very concerned about their dogs
President I will relay these views to the Police in respect of the use of police dogs in large scale public events
Secondly I understand that Members care very much about dogs and I also deeply believe that the Police also care about their dogs . In particular the Police have established a very close and close partnership with the dog keepers
If a dog keeper takes care of a dog after it has retired I believe that the dog keeper will love the dog very much and take good care of the dog . I also believe that the dog owner will take good care of the dog in terms of health and health care as far as possible
Of course we understand how to help dog lovers so that they can take better care of retired dogs
(how ever ) in respect of the use of public money I believe that the Government has to carefully study the use of public money as a subsidy
Any dog owners who have come into contact with dogs adopting retired dogs are very caring and most of them think that they can take care of the dogs themselves including their abilities and financial means . (how ever ) if individual people really need to consider providing support in this regard the Animal and Dog Association can provide some assistance so that the health care services for dogs can also be provided
I thank Members for their questions just now
I understand that the problem lies with the care for dogs but we all care for animals
In this regard I have not grasped the details
(how ever ) I will submit my views on this to the Police Force so that they can do more to seek justice
(how ever ) I must stress that in our daily lives when we consider whether police officers are required to perform certain duties we will indeed consider a number of factors . Apart from their ability to assist in the handling of certain duties we will also consider the welfare of police officers
Therefore regarding the consideration factors mentioned just now for dispatching police officers to handle major incidents I will relay to the Police that different factors will be taken into consideration when dispatching police officers in the future
On the welfare of police dogs the Agriculture Fisheries and Conservation Department AFCD and the Police often provide professional technical advice and often send vets to inspect police dogs
It is right to communicate more with animal lovers and there may be other suggestions to better deal with the problem of police dogs
I will relay these views to the Police
President I wish to make it clear in my reply that I am not referring to the number of police dogs engaged in public activities . I am not referring to the number of police dogs engaged in public activities in general . This is the first point
Secondly we must understand that when the Police Force dispatches police officers to carry out reconnaissance and patrol operations such as demonstrations and protests it is not necessary for the Police Force to carry out reconnaissance and patrol operations every time
Therefore when the Police considered the allocation of police officers they had full consideration
If something happens at the scene the Police will definitely take into account the situation and make appropriate arrangements to deal with the scene . For example if the scene is targeted at by police officers the Police will definitely take into account the situation
Therefore we must make it clear that we will not use tear gas every time there is a transfer of power
Another point is that when we study the use of police dogs by foreign police forces what factors should we consider?
Their deployment is very similar to our deployment and their consideration of the situation is also very similar . As for handling the situation of sudden changes in the (work place ) their corresponding deployment is also very similar to that of the Hong Kong Police Force in respect of police dogs
President in 2011 the Hong Kong Police Force launched the Sexual Offences Record Checking Mechanism to allow employees employed by employers of organizations or enterprises engaging children or mentally incapacitated persons in their work to check whether the sexual offences listed on the Record of Sexual Offences specified in the Record of Sexual Offences are criminal
The employers concerned may request eligible applicants to apply to the Hong Kong Police Force for inspection
The inspection mechanism provides a reliable channel for employers to ascertain whether the applicant has a record of sexual offences when employing employees to perform the relevant duties . The purpose is to reduce the risk of sexual offences being committed by children or mentally incapacitated persons and enhance protection for them
Since the introduction of the inspection mechanism the scope of the mechanism has been expanding
At present the applicants include employees who are engaged in related work with children or mentally incapacitated persons contract renewal employees and employees who are outsourced to other organizations for the provision of relevant services including private entertainment organizations such as swimming events sports events and music centres
After consulting the Education Bureau and the Social Welfare Department the various parts of the question are as follows 1 During the period from 2016 to 2020 the Police received about 43 000 new applications of 51 000 50 000 and 45 000 respectively
The validity period of the inspection result is 18 months . During this period the applicant or the authorised employer may make an automatic telephone inquiry into the result
Applicants may perform the extension procedure for 18 months within the last three months of the validity period
During the period from 2016 to 2020 the Police received about 7 000 8 000 7 000 11 000 and 13 000 applications for renewal of the legal terms respectively
As the decision on the employment of an employer is affected by different factors the Police are not required to inform the Police and hence the Police are not aware of the employment of the applicant
From 2016 to 2020 the Police Force received 41 38 51 38 and 19 cases of illegal conduct at kindergartens or primary and secondary schools
President as Mr CHOW said just now we agree and attach importance to the need to protect children and mentally incapacitated persons from sexual abuse . Therefore we have tried every means to help them
First of all the purpose of conducting the inspection is to prevent volunteers from participating in voluntary work because most of the volunteers are participating in the service of society and they may not need to provide information under the mechanism to reduce their enthusiasm in volunteering . This is one of the arguments
Secondly there are activities arranged by voluntary workers who are often monitored by other people . It is thus understandable that voluntary workers may pose a risk of sexual violence in this respect and the other factors are not so low . But I agree that we should minimize all possibilities
I think that we should consider the direction of expanding the scope of the scheme together . (how ever ) in different areas as mentioned in the question just now such as volunteers and some existing employees my position is that I have to balance the risks and my ability and take risks as the first consideration
Therefore I think that as compared with the measures mentioned just now those who seek help may not be at the highest risk because other measures can ensure that they will be subject to regulation
President I absolutely agree that we should do our best to protect children who are mentally incapacitated from sexual violence
(how ever ) we also have to consider how large the number of cases we have to deal with in the entire investigation mechanism because we have to deal with the most risky cases precisely
Therefore our policy should be gradually expanded and progress should be made in a orderly manner
Under this principle we must understand that when a large number of people have to pass through the inspection mechanism first other problems will be brought about and consequently the entire system will be out of order
Certainly technology can always be expanded but this is just one of the steps
Besides even after the use of technology we still need (man power ) to confirm that the person who was taught right at the first stage of investigation is the same person and we still need (man power ) to teach him right again . This is another situation
In this connection we have to resolve the bottleneck problems in various aspects
Therefore we have to expand the capacity on the whole . Apart from ensuring that the capacity (cannot ) be extended to cover a very long time which will lead to a total delay we have indeed considered the technology aspect
If this approach is successfully adopted I believe we can at least increase the capacity by 70 % . I also think that due to the convenience of this approach more people will not mind making checks
As I said just now if volunteers consider it a very convenient method and do not have to do it in person it is easy for them to submit the survey results (with out ) affecting their ability as volunteers
President according to Chapter 604 of the Law of Hong Kong that the Independent Police Complaints Council IPCC shall be responsible for the monitoring and review of complaints against the Police for the purpose of processing and investigating complaints that are necessary and for the purpose of reporting complaints IPCC shall be responsible for the monitoring and review of complaints against the Police for the purpose of processing and investigating complaints that are necessary and for the purpose of reporting complaints IPCC shall be responsible for the monitoring and review of complaints against the Police for the purpose of processing and investigating complaints against the Police for the purpose of reporting complaints IPCC shall be responsible for the monitoring and review of complaints against the Police IPCC shall be responsible for the monitoring and review of complaints against the Police IPCC shall be responsible for the monitoring and review of complaints against the Police IPCC shall be responsible for the monitoring and review of complaints against the Police IPCC shall be responsible for the monitoring and review of complaints against the Police
IPCC may also identify the deficiencies or inadequacies of a complaint that may lead to a report in the常態 or procedural adopted by the Police and make recommendations to the Police or the Chief Executive
In respect of the system of the Complaints Against Police Complaints Council the system is an effective statutory structure with two levels . The first level is responsible for receiving and investigating complaints from the Police Complaints Office and the second level is the statutory Police Authority
Under the effective operation of IPCC and a clear legal basis the two tier complaint system ensures that every complaint against the Police is handled in a fair and just manner
Under the two tier system the Complaints Against Police Unit which operates independently from other police departments is required to submit a detailed investigation report to IPCC for examination upon completion of each investigation
If IPCC considers that the handling and investigation of complaints against the Police Force are inadequate it may request the Police Force to clarify or conduct investigation afresh
Besides IPCC may provide views and suggestions to the Commissioner and the Chief Executive on any complaint
IPCC has all along adopted a fair just and impartial approach to comprehensively monitor and examine the handling and investigation of complaints by the Complaints Council
IPCC is made up of people from outside Hong Kong and members come from all walks of life including Members of the Legislative Council . They have extensive experience in society and public office and have been involved in the investigation and complaint work of the Police in an objective and direct manner . IPCC is also supported by the Independent Commission against Corruption the Independent Commission Against Corruption and the Independent Commission Against Corruption
There are doubts about the independence of IPCC and there is a lack of impartiality among IPCC members and observers who have all along been strictly following the Police investigation and complaint work
Since 9 June this year there have been over 800 demonstrations processions and public assemblies in Hong Kong
In response to public concerns about the law enforcement actions taken by the Police in major public events IPCC has established an expert panel and an international expert panel under the powers conferred by the Implementation Ordinance to take the initiative to examine major public events and actions taken by the Police in Hong Kong since 9 June and to announce the progress to the public in phases
The Government will closely monitor IPCCs report and recommendations and carefully study them to decide how to follow up
On the other hand the Chief Executive launched four actions on 4 September this year in a bid to break the deadlock for Hong Kong . One of the actions is to invite social leaders experts and scholars to conduct independent studies and reviews on the issues of the deep center of society and to make recommendations to the Government
The Independent Review Team will review the causes of the social incidents that have occurred over the past few months and study the various deeper contradictions in Hong Kong society and formulate solutions to resolve them
The Government is making preparations and will commence the work of the independent review team as soon as the situation becomes slightly stable
2 and 3 IPCC exercises its statutory functions under the independent operation of IPCC Ordinance
President I think after the Police Force and IPCC have made their final evaluations the (over all ) performance of the Police Force will be reflected
We should not only look at the figures in the first year . Perhaps I can let Members compare the figures in the third year
In fact the main reply just now only mentioned the figures of three years
In the past the Police Force maintained law and order and was well received by the public for their performance in this respect . (how ever ) in the past three years the number of complaints filed by the Police Force reflected the (over all ) performance of the Police Force for a period of time
Hence the (over all ) performance of the Hong Kong Police Force over the past three years shows that it is a professional and quality police force
Given the different public activities after June the number of complaints has certainly increased . According to the report issued by the Independent Police Complaints Council IPCC in the past the number and proportion of complaints submitted each year were almost the same . This precisely reflects the professionalism and performance of the Police in this respect
Therefore the figures of IPCC show that Hong Kong has a professional and outstanding police officer
Moreover I have already made it clear in the main reply that different views have been expressed on how to deal with the problems in this respect in the future . In my reply to the question just now I have already stated the Governments position on this and how it intends to follow up the issue
Therefore in the 2020 financial year the Government has provided HKPF with a subsidy amount of 96 million to meet its expenses on salaries allowances and other operational expenses
This funding is 17 million more than the previous financial year ie . more than 20 % of the total amount of funding and the main purpose is to recruit additional (man power )
We will also take into account the needs of IPCC and will be happy to consider any further requests from the Government for further support
Moreover the Complaints Against Police Office CAPO has been actively supporting the investigation work of IPCC and set up a special team dedicated to handling complaints in this regard
President in the scrutiny work of IPCC the Secretary has made it clear that he will definitely deal with the matter in accordance with the principle of fairness and impartiality
As IPCC operates independently I must respect their work autonomy
(how ever ) IPCC has already pointed out that the report will be a phased report
I believe IPCC will be very prudent in determining how comprehensive or how much of the (over all ) matter will be covered in this stage report . When making the report IPCC will clearly state the extent at which the review has reached
President as I have clearly stated in the main reply different views have requested the Government to do different work on the whole . The Governments position has also been clearly stated in the four actions proposed by the Chief Executive to Members
Apart from withdrawing the proposed amendments to the Bill the Four Actions Ordinance also provides support for the work of IPCC so that we can find out the inadequacies of the system or procedures currently under the handling of the Police
The third point is to open up dialogue with the public and we will set up an independent study and review panel which I have already mentioned in the main reply
The suggestions made just now are one of many examples
As I have mentioned in the main reply the Government has also proposed to set up another (sub committee ) to study the practice of the United Kingdom in handling disturbances and victims
Other matters are also addressed such as investigations into criminal liability and riot involvement
The questions raised just now are also covered by the Police investigation
President people have the freedom to express their views speak and assemble but when exercising such freedom they must abide by the law in a peaceful manner
In the event of any unlawful acts the Police have the statutory responsibility to deal with them in order to maintain public safety and public order
Since early June this year there have been over 700 demonstrations processions and public assemblies in Hong Kong and many of them have turned into violent illegal activities
In the past five months or so some rioters have committed a number of serious illegal acts including arbitrary blocking of roads paralysing traffic throwing of petrol bombs and setting fire to various places throwing bricks deliberately vandalizing and burning shops and the facilities of the MTR Light Rail etc . They have seriously endangered peoples safety public order and public safety
When a person is arrested by the Police the Police will inform him or her of the reasons and the reasons for arrest as soon as possible
The arrested person will be brought to the front of the Immigration Tower as soon as possible to ascertain the legitimacy of his arrest and detention
Subsequently the arrested persons would be referred to the investigation team for investigation
Upon completion of the preliminary investigation the Police will decide whether the arrested person should be detained
In the light of individual cases the Police will consider allowing the arrested person to sign a self guarantee for going out to keep him or her safe or to release him or her (with out ) any conditions
Generally speaking people in detention will not be detained for more than 48 hours
My reply to the various parts of the question raised by Members is as follows 1 and 2 Since 9 June the protests and violent clashes in Hong Kong have persisted
The offences involved by the arrested persons include rioting unlawful assembly assault of a (business man ) to cause physical injury common assault arson criminal damage assault of police obstructing police officers in performing their duties possession of offensive weapons use of mask when in an unlawful assembly etc
Regarding the age of the arrested in the question how many of them were released (with out ) conditions how many of them are still under investigation and how many of them have been included in the statistics of the judicial process we can see from Schedule 1
The Police did not keep the statistics on the time of detention of the arrested
3 The Police have clear guidelines on the handling of arrested children or young people and will ensure the rights and benefits of the children or young people concerned
When the Police arrest children or young people they must follow all reasonable procedures stipulated in the Police General Orders to inform their parents or caretakers
Under reasonable circumstances the Police will request suitable adults to accompany the victim to the Police Station before inquiring into the child or young person arrested
A suitable adult refers to the following persons 1 a relative a caretaker or other person responsible for taking care of or looking after the person 2 a person with experience in dealing with a person with special needs but who (cannot ) be a police officer or be under the care of the Police or 3 a person who is not able to arrange the persons mentioned above to be present but who needs other assistance
The Police will arrange suitable adults for the arrested persons in accordance with the established procedures and guidelines
The Police did not provide any information as requested
Each suitable adult will be issued a suitable adult notice for persons under the age of 16 who are incapable of behaving or of ageing psychologically with the help of the Police in an investigation or detention of the Police
The Notice explains the roles and responsibilities of suitable adults to help them understand how to provide appropriate support for the arrested
For persons under 16 years of age who have been detained before conducting the detention and search the Department of Justice will ensure that a suitable adult is present and he must be 18 years of age or above and his gender is the same as that of the detainee
The Police will ensure that both the arrested persons will not be detained in the same temporary detention place and that both the adults and the children will be separated from the adults
The Police have all along been committed to protecting the privacy and rights of persons being arrested
There is no difference between the basic rights enjoyed by children or young people in detention and those enjoyed by other people in detention including seeking legal assistance reaching out to family members obtaining written records made in the police force food and drinks supplied seeking the right to receive medical treatment and so on
The rights of detainees are clearly set out in the notice issued by police officers to each detainee or any police investigator
Regarding the relevant statistics mentioned in the question the Police have not denied it
President the Police must take action on the basis of facts and evidence when they arrest anyone . If someone breaks the law and commits an illegal act in front of the Police the Police must take action on the basis of facts and evidence
We do not want to see young people taking part in any illegal activities but we must face certain facts . As I mentioned just now we have clearly stated in the main reply that members of the public do have the freedom of expression and assembly but they must abide by the law in a peaceful manner
For example we have seen people digging up bricks from the ground and throwing them at other people . When we see people throwing bricks and objects at a highway on Tin Kiu the Police must take action
All government departments have followed this policy and target including the Police
There is no need for the Police to make their own decisions
The Police have all along been handling any public activities in accordance with the laws of Hong Kong in a fair impartial and impartial manner
The policy of the Police is to strike a balance by facilitating lawful and peaceful public assemblies and processions as far as possible so that public activities can be conducted smoothly and on the other hand to minimize the impact of such activities on the public and road users while maintaining public safety and public order
During the protests in the past few months the Police have been performing their duties and handling the provocations of the protesters with tolerance and restraint
(how ever ) some demonstrations did not require the Police to be informed in advance in accordance with the law . Some demonstrations even unlawfully assembled in various places and some of them even turned into violent acts
The escalating violence of the rioters has created numerous phenomena and posed a great threat to the peoples livelihood and property
The Police have the statutory duty to maintain public order and public safety
When there is a serious threat to public order and public safety the Police must take decisive actions to curb violence and restore peace in society
My consolidated reply to the three parts of the Members question is as follows
Since June last year there have been more than 1 400 demonstrations processions and public assemblies in Hong Kong and many of them have turned into serious illegal acts and violent incidents
Over the past period of time there have been rioters from time to time advocating the besiegement blockade and vandalism of the Legislative Council Complex the police stations of various districts the quarters of disciplined forces and other buildings
Such behaviour has seriously affected the work and life of people travelling to the above mentioned places including the emergency services provided by the Police to the public
Furthermore some rioters threw petrol bombs set fire to police cars and police stations at the Police Headquarters and government buildings and attempted to damage government facilities . This has posed a serious threat to public safety and public order
After assessment of the risks the Police considered it necessary to enhance the security of these buildings in order to protect them
Apart from handing in the hostages different measures have to be implemented including the prevention of water horse and attack and vandalism
In taking the relevant security measures the Police will communicate with the property managers and the persons in charge of the premises on the one hand to ensure that the security measures can be implemented effectively and on the other hand to minimize the impact on the people and road users including the people on both sides of the road who enter the premises
During the implementation of the security measures the Police will maintain liaison with the managers of the relevant properties and review the security measures and arrangements from time to time
The Police have contacted different stakeholders including nongovernmental support groups for persons with disabilities to explain to them the reasons for the Police to implement security measures
Meanwhile the Police have also explained to them the Governments policy of no barrier
If necessary persons with disabilities may directly seek assistance from the Director of Security of the premises under the Governments charge of no obstruction so as to enter and leave the premises and use the facilities
I would like to point out that the implementation of additional security measures for the storage of government buildings and the implementation of the water barrier is a necessary response to the frequent occurrence of violent incidents and attacks in government properties and police stations which have been provided for public services for months
If members of the public conduct public activities in a peaceful lawful and orderly manner (with out ) committing any serious acts of violence vandalism or assault as shown on television the Police need not implement the aforesaid security measures
I hope that the general public will face squarely the violent acts of the rioters which have caused serious damage and adverse impacts to society
Since June last year a large number of shops and even courts including the Court of Final Appeal the High Court and the Magistrates Courts have been vandalized
There are about 740 traffic lights in Hong Kong which have been vandalized about 1 600 times including being cut off roads vandalized and even set on fire
Moreover as at 11 March 2020 MTRCLs barrier free facilities had been vandalized on a number of occasions including the use of the escalators about 120 times and the lift more than 80 times
The violent acts of these rioters have seriously affected the lives of various social strata including those of persons with disabilities . They have brought inconvenience and even brought danger to all people travelling
If Members are concerned about the ease of access for persons with disabilities they should also be concerned about the brutality of the facilities for persons with disabilities . They should shout loudly condemn the rioters for vandalizing the facilities for persons with disabilities
The general public should resist the violent acts of the rioters . Members should not even be allowed to glorify the violent acts of the rioters and should not be the accomplices who undermine the peace in society
(how ever ) if we are really concerned about the provision of a barrier free access and allow persons with disabilities to use these facilities we should at the end of the day examine why these facilities are not available and why we have to protect the (over all ) safety of society
It is precisely because of such violence that the lives of the persons with disabilities have been ruined
Therefore I think we must set things right and find out the root of the problem . The root of the problem is violence
Therefore we should consider why the facilities of persons with disabilities are damaged and why we need to do security work
If there is no violent destruction by rioters and no rioters have turned Hong Kong into a place where people are always worried about being attacked we do not need any special security measures at all
When we look back at our past years or our past life we were able to live in peace and contentment and live our daily lives . (with out ) violence we would have been able to get back to the way things were back then
President we must look at the cause and effect of the incident and the cause is violence
The example I cited just now is that the various problems caused by violence have affected persons with disabilities
Certainly if there is no need for security no one can live a normal life and this is what I wish to see
President first of all I do not agree with his use of this term to describe the Police and I have not used this term to describe Members
Therefore if Members can be more cautious I believe they should not focus on attacking others when they speak . Thank you President
I will provide Members with the information later on . I agree that many sound equipment for helping persons with disabilities to use the facilities has been vandalized and the use of these facilities has been greatly affected
Regarding these vandalisms we will definitely pursue those responsible for the vandalism and hold them accountable for their criminal and civil acts
We will also provide the public information of the departments concerned including the contact information of all staff members hidden in the (web site) to them and will also provide the links to the relevant associations
In this regard the Police will continue to maintain communication with the public in the hope of ensuring the smooth entry and exit of people from both sides and understanding why the Police will do so in this regard so as to enhance the communication between the public and the public
President section 10 of the Police Force Ordinance Cap . 232 stipulates that the duties of the Police Force include taking lawful measures to maintain public safety to prevent and inquire into crimes and illegal activities and to prevent the damage of life and property
Any place in Hong Kong is regulated by law and there is no place that is out of law
Therefore if anyone commits an offence in Hong Kong the Police are duty bound to take action
I do not agree to the proposal to stop police officers from violently treating Members or journalists and I even disagree with the so called arbitrary prosecution and indiscriminate arrest
The actions of the Police are taken in accordance with the actual circumstances and the use of force is also taken in accordance with the actual needs
The decision on prosecution is based on evidence and facts as well as relevant legal views
The Police have all along attached great importance to the conduct and behaviour of police officers
Police officers must comply with the requirements in terms of conduct and discipline and uphold the values of the Police in respect of justice and professionalism
The Police Force has a comprehensive and orderly integrity management system which is committed to promoting the integrity and integrity of its officers as well as the discipline and conduct of its monitoring officers
In order to further enhance the efficiency of integrity management the Police Force set up the Integrity Assessment Task Force in May this year to make integrity management work more targeted
My reply to the various parts of the question is as follows 1 In the trial the judge of the case made a comment on the performance of the witness and determined that the evidence in question was used as evidence in the case and thus the comment made on the witness of the case was only applicable to that case
As a matter of fact in different cases of trial some Judges have claimed that they are honest and reliable witnesses in accepting the evidence of police officers
I must point out that as at 15 October 2020 over 80 % of the cases involving violent illegal acts since June last year have already been concluded in court proceedings and more than 80 % of the people involved have to bear the legal consequences including conviction of the offence or signing of a petition in court
2 If the court comments on a case the Police will examine and examine whether there is a need to follow up
If the court considers it necessary it may refer the case to the Department of Justice for follow up and the Police will conduct an investigation in accordance with the actual circumstances
(how ever ) I must point out that during a criminal trial the court may not accept the evidence of a certain witness on the basis of different factors such as the memory of the witness is unclear or there is a bit of blank in the description and the conclusion (cannot ) be made reasonably or the evidence is more subjective than objective to the witness . Hence the evidence (cannot ) be arbitrarily classified as unlawful
President just now I mentioned in the main reply that there were 28 persons under the disciplinary sanctions . I have provided the figures and the number of such cases in the past few years . Hence I do not agree with the remark made just now that I have not provided any information in this regard
We know very well that the Police Force is disciplined and will punish those who have done wrong
Regarding the Voluntary Poisoning Programme we all know that we must act in accordance with the law
At present there is no law in Hong Kong to force anyone to take drugs . I believe all of us know that
In order to uphold the culture of integrity management we hope to build a positive culture . In the course of building a culture we must act in accordance with the law and comply with the legal restrictions
Therefore in respect of integrity management we have been talking about building up a healthy culture over the years . In addition we will deal with any irregularities and irregularities seriously and we will not be biased in handling them
This is the (over all ) health culture of the Police Force
Any witness in court or even the defendant should be treated fairly . I believe this is the rule of law in Hong Kong
In the case of a trial if there is unfair treatment on one side the lawyer on both sides will make a request and protest . This is the legal procedure
Therefore if it is found that a certain witness has really injured his or her rights and interests the Prosecution Officer should take the initiative to protest or raise arguments for consideration by the Court
Moreover I know that the Judiciary has already set up a mechanism for handling complaints relating to judges behaviour and the relevant matters can be handled in accordance with the mechanism set up by the Judiciary
As regards police officers the Police attach great importance to the performance of each and every police officer in these trials . In terms of training the Police should better understand the legal requirements and procedural limitations and how to strengthen their preparation
Thank you Deputy President . I will give a brief response
The law enforcement work of the Police Force is complicated especially in the case of large groups of people . In many violent incidents different people often appear on the scene and the work of the Police Force is affected in certain aspects
As regards media reporting the Police have been increasing the number of their liaison team in recent years . For example the number of liaison team members has now increased to 300 . It is hoped that the Police will communicate more with new staff members in large group activities so that they will not affect the work of both sides when they are doing their respective jobs
Moreover we understand the pressure faced by (front line ) police officers on these complicated occasions . In particular some officers have really obstructed their actions and provoked them
In this regard we certainly have to strengthen their training in respect of professionalism and psychology so that they will understand that when they are faced with complicated situations such as provocation by the Police or obstruction by the Police they will encounter such situations from time to time
Therefore the Police must be prepared to deal with such occasions including professional training tactical preparation and communication between the police officers on site and how the commander can better understand the actual situation as a whole so as to make proper preparations . This is the duty of the Police
Moreover to enable police officers to deal with the pressure faced by these complicated situations the Police Psychological Services Department will from time to time carry out training and (counseling ;) work in this respect . The Department will also conduct workshops to help police officers understand how to ensure that they will not be subjected to these complicated situations at the scene and how to ensure that they will not be subjected to these difficult situations
Regarding the recent incidents I have already made it very clear that if there is a comment on a case by the court the existing system is that the Police Authority will conduct a review of the comments made on the case
President since early June last year there have been more than 1 400 demonstrations processions and public assemblies in Hong Kong and many of them have turned into illegal acts
Under section 10 of the Police Force Ordinance Cap . 232 the Police Force has the statutory duty to maintain public safety and public order
When unlawful acts are committed the Police must take action to maintain public order and protect the lives and property of the public
The Police have attached great importance to the conduct and conduct of police officers
Police officers must comply with the behaviour and disciplinary requirements of the Police and uphold the values of the Police in all aspects such as fairness and professionalism
My reply to the various parts of the question is as follows 1 and 2 The discipline of police officers is regulated by the Police Regulations
Spitting is an administrative measure and the starting point of the punishment mechanism
Regarding the misbehaviour of individual police officers the Commissioner of Police has already given an account of the 22 cases involving police officers
These cases mainly involve misuse of force misuse of language and misbehaviour
The Chief Secretary for Police has made an analysis that the purpose of the analysis is to intervene immediately and to stop the offences of officers and to rectify the offences and to let other officers know the wrongdoings of the relevant acts
Spitting was the starting point of the punishment mechanism
If the Police further investigate finds that it is necessary to take other actions disciplinary or criminal investigations and procedures will be conducted
The Police will handle the matter in a fair and impartial manner in accordance with the established mechanism
Among the 20 or so cases of being defamatory mentioned just now the Police have already initiated disciplinary review on four of them . Among them 19 cases of complaint against the Police Force have also received complaints
Given that the Complaints Against Police Office and the disciplinary review procedures of the Police are in progress it is inappropriate for me to further comment on the details of the case
The Police Force did not keep any statistical data on the division of duties in the question
In respect of promotion the Police will consider the (over all ) performance of its staff and examine their work performance talent and discipline etc
President Members are the ones who betray their conscience
The first point he made just now is a fragmentary point . He can lodge a complaint and the case will be handled in a fair and just manner
I remember that I have seen a video clip in which the Police used a shield to cover a family when rioters threw things in a shopping centre . The Police did not allow the rioters to throw things out of the shopping centre . They used a camera to describe the scene as the Police drove the family away
Therefore we (cannot ) judge the facts with one single judgment . We should deal with the matter fairly and impartially and then lodge a complaint . When lodging a complaint we should tell the story of the incident and the person responsible should be the one to lodge the complaint
President I must make it very clear that all the remarks I made just now are based on personal experience
Moreover in September last year at the Lantau Claims Court the Police intercepted a man in a reflective garment and a press card but he was not issued with a press card
A man who was arrested for the crime of hiding a bomb was also wearing reflective clothes when he was arrested
I understand that journalists and the Police need to do their job
Therefore I very much agree that the Police should meet with the media representatives and discuss with them . It is good for (every one ) to seek a consensus under the circumstances that they can do each others jobs and make (every one ) do the same
If an individual police officer commits an offence in breach of the law he or she will be held responsible for the offence when he or she lodges a complaint with the Police . This is the spirit of fairness and impartiality in Hong Kong
President we welcome anyone who wants to report to the Police and we will definitely conduct an investigation when the Police report to the Police
(how ever ) the most important thing is to provide information and make a report in a genuine capacity . It is because many people do not provide any information after the allegations have been made through air waves . I therefore welcome Members to make a report
In the past when the Police handled large scale protests especially rioting acts they followed the guidelines and procedures and acted in accordance with the laws of Hong Kong
If any action of individual police officers has caused dissatisfaction to another member of the public we should conduct a thorough investigation and make a judgment accordingly
As for disciplinary measures to deal with police officers the punishment is an administrative measure . As I have already explained clearly just now the Commissioner of Police considers that this approach can be intervened as early as possible so that police officers will know what actions should be corrected
Therefore I strongly support the immediate intervention of the Commissioner of Police by way of defamation and defamation is only a starting point which will be followed up by the Police in accordance with the actual situation
If they are found to have committed any genuine offence in the course of investigation they will be prosecuted by disciplinary hearings in accordance with their disciplinary proceedings
If there are other needs they will be dealt with in accordance with the law
Therefore in respect of disciplinary operations the Police have been handling the cases in accordance with the Police Regulations under the Ordinance over the years
As stated in the Regulation the (over all ) handling of disciplinary matters including minor and serious acts of discipline will be eliminated . In this regard the Commissioner of Police has intervened early with administrative measures to improve the entire disciplinary system
Deputy President some of the remarks made just now have precisely made some people believe that what they said is the truth
The Police have all along attached great importance to the Police Force . It seems that the Police will not take any action to deal with those who have violated the law . This is certainly not true
I have already made it clear in the main reply that the Police are police officers convicted of offences in contravention of the law . The figures tell us that they are convicted of offences so we (cannot ) say that they are not . This is wrong and misleading to the public . Some people do not know and really think that what I said just now is true
First they will take the initiative to investigate the serious breaches of the covenants . Second they will examine whether there are any procedures which are particularly high in risk and need to be improved or even restricted . Third they will conduct thorough monitoring and supervision
The Police Force is a disciplined and strict team and I hope that members of the public will not be misled by biased remarks
The 12 fugitive offenders mentioned in the question are suspected of committing various serious crimes in Hong Kong . They are related to the seven cases . Among them 10 people are suspected of committing serious crimes such as creating or possessing explosives arson incitement by (business men ) riot assault on police officers possession of offensive weapons and possession of tools which may be used for illegal purposes . They were prosecuted by the Police and sentenced to imprisonment
Another case was the suspected manufacture or storage of explosives which was brought to justice by the Police . These 12 fugitive offenders left Hong Kong through an unlawful means and were arrested by the Mainland law enforcement agencies in the Mainland waters in accordance with the law
They should stay in Hong Kong and face legal liability but they abandoned their protection and fled to Hong Kong to escape court trial
President I strongly oppose the proposal put forward by Mr KWONG Chun Yuen just now to encourage people to give up and protect their future
I am only talking about his behaviour . He is encouraging others to give up and protect their future . This is not a speculation at all
Our answer is to stay calm
Secondly we have made it very clear that we must pursue each persons legal liability . It is only natural that he has violated Mainland laws on the Mainland and the Mainland will deal with him in accordance with the law
I can tell them that they are dead . The SAR Government will not play along with them when they write the script and we will not let them take the script away
According to the provisions of the law the court will give due consideration to whether the arrested person will give up his or her life or commit another offence and the seriousness of the case will be the basis of its decision and whether a guarantee will be given
Under the existing system I believe that we can propose more such provisions as different compensation restrictions such as the banning of arrest warrants and the number and duration of reporting to the Hong Kong Police Force . We can also include in the compensation conditions measures that will enable the Police Force to report to the Hong Kong Police Force if there is a chance of escape
(how ever ) I must make it clear to Members what problems we are dealing with
Secondly these technology measures may let us know earlier that a person may have left a place restricted to him by the court but it is another matter of action to immediately stop him
President these false information have really caused great disturbances to Hong Kong especially in the past 10 months or so when the black violence broke out and Hong Kong was seriously harmed
Such false information rumours and false information are very disturbing to Hong Kong
Under the existing legislation in Hong Kong anyone who makes any provocative remarks may violate section 9 of the Criminal Criminal Ordinance
Section 10 of the Ordinance stipulates that any person who conveys any incitement to another person including online incitement such as using violence or inciting a person of a different class from having a sense of evil or hostility or inciting another person not to abide by the law and not to obey an order is an offence
The Police will handle the arrest of all lawbreakers seriously
President I move the Second Reading of the Immigration Amendment Bill 2020 the Bill
The Bill aims to deal with different problems of non – refoulement claims more effectively including enhancing the efficiency of the Immigration Department ImmD in vetting and approving claims and preventing delays improving the procedures and functions of the Appeal Board and stepping up the efforts in detention and (crack down ) on illegal employment
The Government has all along attached importance to and is determined to address the problem of no appeal
Over the years the Security Bureau and the Immigration Department have implemented a number of measures targeting the handling of claim issues including combating theft and expected stay at source expediting the handling of appeals by the Appeal Board on claims and appeals against false claims expediting the resumption of claims after the completion of the vetting and approval process and stepping up enforcement actions to combat illegal employment
Moreover the screening work for the claim has also been affected by the outbreak of the Coronavirus Disease 2019 epidemic
The feedback from the relevant figures precisely confirms that the Government must take decisive actions to further improve the investigation procedures through strengthening and rationalizing the existing legal framework so as to plug any possible loopholes and strengthen the measures to block enforce and repudiate claimants at source
The Government has to ensure that the (over all ) policy on non – refoulement claims is to ensure the high efficiency of vetting and appealing of claims and to meet the highly fair and standard legal requirements set down by the court
In addition we should expeditiously send the noncommunicative claimants to Hong Kong
On the other hand the application for leave for judicial review handled by the Court of First Instance of the Court of First Instance of the Chief Executive is in a serious backlog . For this reason the Judiciary has made a number of amendments to the High Court Ordinance Cap . 4 including extending the use of the Court of Appeal composed of two judges and cutting the power of the Judge of the Court of First Instance or the Court of Appeal in writing to handle cases (with out ) having to open a court in person
We will keep a close watch on the situation and the effectiveness
President after the passage of the Bill it will help to prevent delaying and expedite the (over all ) vetting work of the Executive Authorities by way of resumption of proceedings . It will also provide a more solid legal basis for improving and strengthening the implementation of various provisions such as extradition to prison blockade and enforcement
I hope Members will support the Bill
I would like to take this opportunity to give a brief response to the views expressed by Members just now
Every Member who speaks today is very concerned about the fire safety of old buildings
This is in line with the Governments position and the purpose of the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance
Dr Priscilla LEUNG proposes to amend the legislation to empower the authorities to carry out fire safety improvement works in buildings which fail to comply with fire safety guidelines
In the past Mr LEUNG also put forward a similar idea and suggested that the authorities should draw reference from the Buildings Ordinance to carry out fire safety improvement works on behalf of the owners when necessary and then collect the works fees from the owners
As I mentioned in my opening remarks I hope to do my best to help appreciate and understand Mr LEUNGs concern about the difficulties encountered by owners of old buildings
(how ever ) in respect of how to better assist property owners in meeting the requirements under the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance my view and that of Mr LEUNG may not be entirely consistent
My colleagues from the Fire Services Department have explained on various occasions that there is a difference between building works under the Buildings Ordinance and fire safety improvement works under the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance
When owners of certain buildings are unable to comply with the repair and demolition orders issued by the Buildings Department BD in accordance with the Buildings Ordinance the BD may exercise the powers conferred by the Buildings Ordinance to carry out orders for these owners to carry out the maintenance works and recover the works costs from these owners after completion
It should be noted that the current repairs and maintenance works of buildings for owners by the Buildings Department or the demolition of UBWs generally involve immediate risks and there is no alternative option
(how ever ) under the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance fire safety improvement works are mainly required to enhance the fire safety of old buildings
Unlike the repairs and demolition works under the Buildings Ordinance these fire service improvement works often involve different feasible options and works arrangements and the costs involved are also more diverse
For example fire service tanks can be installed at different locations and may involve different owners and the costs will also vary
Under the principle of private property rights these matters should be discussed and reached a consensus by property owners on their own and should not and should not be decided and implemented by the law enforcement authorities on their own initiative . Otherwise unreasonable interference in private property rights may even lead to disputes and (law suits ) which will bring more consequences to the improvement of fire safety in buildings
It is undeniable that in order to carry out fire safety improvement works in accordance with the Ordinance the first step is to enable the owners to understand their own responsibilities take the initiative to participate in the works and coordinate with other owners
(how ever ) this does not mean that we have to amend the Convention and allow the Government to take over
Besides the Government may not be able to get everything done in one go but the problem mentioned just now is just an example
We always think that the more appropriate approach is for the Government to play the role of an assistant to follow up on the problems faced by different buildings on its own in the light of the land needs
In fact the problems faced by different buildings vary from one another . Some are technical problems some are financial problems and some are management problems of corporations
In this regard government departments have put in place targeted assistance measures and gained some experience
In addition FSD and BD will also take into account the difficulties in coordinating the works of the previous owners such as the need for time to raise funds and the need to recruit engineering consultants or to set up OCs
Departments will take into account the actual circumstances of each case and consider extending the period for direction
According to past experience after reasonable justification is given by the owner the extension of the period will be allowed under most circumstances
Some cases have even been delayed for years allowing owners more time to follow up on their cases and the departments will not lightly prosecute them
The Chief Executive Officer of FSDC and BD is also frequently invited to attend owners meetings to answer phone calls and written enquiries and to give detailed answers on issues related to the Ordinance
Dr Priscilla LEUNG and other Members have also mentioned how to handle the installation of fire safety facilities in old buildings in a flexible manner especially in the case of water tanks
As I mentioned in my opening remarks the Fire Services Department and the Water Supplies Department have introduced a flush type flush system for old integrated use buildings with a floor area of three floors or lower to avoid owners installation of fire service tanks and water pumps
I would like to take this opportunity to report on the new development of the four to six levels of integrated use building owners
After making reference to the previous experience of the enforcement of the Ordinance and having considered the time required for the dispatch of fire trucks to handle fire service calls the department has decided to lower the original requirement for the fire service to use the water tank at most four to six floors from 2 000 litres to 500 litres
In terms of the volume of the reservoirs the volume of 500 litres is about 1 x 1.5 m . This volume has substantially reduced the weight and volume of the reservoirs and the roof of most old buildings is already heavy enough in terms of structure
In some districts with relatively remote areas and more scattered buildings fire trucks may have to spend more time to reach the scene of the fire . As a result the capacity of fire trucks is relatively higher at about 750 to 1 500 litres
The Fire Services Department FSD expects that more than 3 000 multi purpose buildings with a floor area of four to six floors will be able to cope with the technical difficulties and expeditiously comply with the fire safety guidelines at cost of a larger sum through this measure
The beneficiaries will include many old property owners in old districts such as Sham Shui Po Yau Tsim Mong and Kowloon City
The Fire Services Department FSD informed registered fire service contractors and other members of the industry in late October and will maintain full communication with the owners
Mr LEUNG proposes to follow the model of building updating a big action by setting up similar schemes to help owners raise the fire safety level of their old buildings and substantially increase financial support for them
I would like to point out that the Building Update in 2009 was a special employment relief measure launched by the Government under the financial tsunami with the aim of creating more employment opportunities for the construction sector and achieving the dual objectives of promoting building safety and beautification of use
The old buildings approved for participating in the update include buildings which are required to comply with the fire safety guidelines . The funding for the update can also be used for fire safety improvement works
According to the estimation all 3 000 building maintenance works under the Upgrading Action are expected to be completed by the end of 2017
As I mentioned in my opening remarks in respect of financial support the Buildings Department the Housing Society and the Urban Renewal Authority have already put in place a number of permanent schemes including the Integrated Building Maintenance Support Scheme the Elderly Building Maintenance Subsidy Scheme and the Building Insurance Scheme to provide different forms of financial support to owners of old buildings so as to help them complete their construction works
Just now Members have put forward some suggestions and views on these existing schemes . We will consider and follow up with the relevant departments as appropriate
Some Members have raised the question of whether the fire extinguishers can be used to replace the fire hose reel system to reduce the difficulty in installing fire hydrants
I have to emphasize that the requirements for fire safety equipment and equipment under the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance are actually basic fire safety improvement measures . They are very important in reducing the risk of fire and safeguarding lives and properties and should not be exempted or replaced arbitrarily
For example the installation of fire hills and wheel arches is to effectively control the spread of fire in the early stages of fire which is very important to the fire safety of buildings
Therefore the Department of Justice considers it inappropriate to exempt the owner of an old building from the requirement for installing a wheel system in the building
As the fire extinguishers are subject to physical constraints and fire extinguishers have limited fire safety protection they (cannot ) be completely regarded as a substitute for wheelchairs
Deputy President I understand Members concern about the Sha Mau Building
It is indeed difficult for owners to commence fire safety improvement works in unauthorized buildings
I know that the Home Affairs Department has been assisting property owners in setting up OCs and applying for maintenance subsidies through the Building Management Professional Advisory Scheme
Since its introduction in 2011 the Scheme has successfully assisted owners to set up or redevelop 328 OCs and assisted 204 OCs to apply for building maintenance subsidies or loans and 169 OCs to commence building maintenance works
(how ever ) I understand that in order to set up a corporation owners have to take the initiative to work and devote time and energy
The efforts made by many District Council members in this regard are worthy of recognition
Mr CHAN (Han-) pans concern is that there may not be sufficient contractors and technical personnel in the market to undertake fire safety works
In order to smoothly complete the project of enhancing fire safety apart from the determination of the owners sufficient professional staff are also an indispensable part
The work of FSDC and BD in this regard is to ensure that the information available in the market is open and transparent
The Fire Services Department FSD and the Buildings Department BD have been uploading the list of all registered fire services contractors professional building workers and contractors including those who are interested in taking up fire services improvement works related to the Ordinance on their websites for public and related property owners reference
To better grasp the requirements of the Ordinance and enhance the interest of registered fire service contractors in carrying out works FSD has organized 13 technical themed seminars over the past two years to explain the flexible and pragmatic proposals adopted by FSD
More than 1 200 members of the industry have joined the event
FEHD will continue its promotion efforts in this regard
The amendment of Mr CHAN (Han-) pan proposes the establishment of a Building Maintenance Authority to enhance tendering transparency
The relevant Policy Bureaux have responded to the proposals on various occasions
In short the relevant departments and organizations have established a mechanism to deal with the possible misconduct in building maintenance and tendering
The authorities will continue to take action in areas such as law enforcement publicity and education as well as providing support to property owners in order to relax the restrictions and combat bid rigging
In support of property owners URA launched in May this year the Building Maintenance Promotion Service Pilot Scheme under the tendering approach to enable independent professionals to provide property owners with technical advice and project estimates as well as provide contractors with an electronic platform for recruitment so that property owners can make decisions on building maintenance works when they have full information and can make decisions on building maintenance works in a timely manner
The Urban Renewal Authority has announced that it will extend the service of the tendering platform to all private residential and commercial buildings in Hong Kong and will not impose an annual application limit to provide assistance to more owners
Regarding the fire safety situation of schools I know that the Education Bureau and the Development Bureau have received the letter from Mr IP and the relevant departments are following up the matter
Deputy President Hong Kong is one of the safest cities in the world and fire safety is one of the essential and essential pillars
The Governments and the Legislative Councils policy on enhancing the fire safety of old buildings is of great importance
To provide better protection for the households of these buildings and to prevent tragedies from happening the Government passed the Fire Safety Buildings Ordinance on that day precisely for this purpose
According to the spirit of the Ordinance it is mainly the responsibility of the owners to carry out fire protection works for private buildings as well as other improvement works for the buildings
(how ever ) I also understand that it is not easy for many owners to carry out these projects under multiple buildings because they need to coordinate with the owners and spend time and resources . It is also inevitable that they will encounter many difficulties in the process
(how ever ) I hope that Members will not jump to the conclusion that the Bill is a nuisance to the law and forget the original purpose of the Bill
As a matter of fact the law is not meant to create trouble for owners but to ensure the safety of fire services which is a major issue in society and should not be taken lightly
I also hope that Members will not think that the Government can go on with its work for eternity just because of some operational difficulties and disregard the consequences of this practice and the responsibilities that private property owners should bear for their properties
That being the case we agree that it is important to provide appropriate support to owners
Since the implementation of the Ordinance various government departments and organizations have been assisting owners to complete the fire safety improvement works as soon as possible through various measures including various technical and financial support schemes and flexible and pragmatic proposals
We will continue to explore and explore further to enhance the room for these measures on the premise of not jeopardizing basic fire safety
After the passage of the Bill quite a number of successful examples are precisely the efforts made by owners of various buildings to achieve the fire safety improvement works together with the cooperation of the owners so that the households and visitors of these buildings can have a safer environment
I hope Members will continue to support our efforts to enhance fire safety in old buildings so that Hong Kong can continue to be a place where the general public can feel safe
President the HKSAR Government has made detailed explanations to the Legislative Council and the public on the enforcement actions of the Police on various occasions over the past few months in respect of the motion moved by Mr Andrew WAN Mr Jeremy TAM and Dr KWOK (Ka-) ki today on 12 June
These three motions are unnecessary
On 12 June there was a rally outside the Legislative Council Complex in the morning . It was originally expected that the rally would be held in a peaceful manner
(how ever ) around 8col00 am that morning a large number of people wearing masks rushed out of Lung Wo Road Tin Mei Road Admiralty Road and (%hesitation ) Kok Road in groups
Protesters occupied roads and obstructed traffic . As a result traffic in the vicinity has been seriously obstructed and almost paralysed seriously affecting the service of emergency vehicles
The protesters besieged the Government Building and the Legislative Council Complex and committed some threatening and provocative acts
Nevertheless the Police have all along maintained restraint and tolerance
Subsequently the protesters continued to pile up a large number of railway tracks on the roadside and lifted bricks from the pavements to tear down the barricades on the side of the road to make road barriers
The situation started to deteriorate at around 3col00 pm
At that time some protesters kept charging at the police lines in an organized manner . They even used dangerous weapons such as bricks metal rods iron poles wooden planks etc . to attack the police officers on the scene . The situation was very dangerous and chaotic
The Police have retreated along the line of defence of the Legislative Council until they reached the entrance of the Legislative Council Complex
At that time the Police repeatedly advised and warned the public to stop charging at the police line of defence but the violent charging did not stop
The acts of the persons concerned not only jeopardize public order and public safety but also seriously threaten the lives of all people present including the media police officers on duty and even protesters who use no force
The Police had no choice but to use appropriate and necessary force to combat violent acts to prevent further damage to public order and public safety and to restore order at the scene
The Police arrested a total of 39 people on 12 June and charged them with offences including disorderly conduct in public places unlawful assembly and assault on police officers
A total of 22 police officers were injured in the operation
After investigation and evidence collection 10 people were charged with crimes such as rioting unlawful assembly assault of police officers resistance against police officers and obstruction of police officers in performing their duties
One of the cases involving rioting was referred to the District Court for trial and the other case was heard in the East District Magistrates Courts from December to January
President members of the public have the freedom to hold peaceful assemblies processions and demonstrations and the Police have been assisting members of the public to hold peaceful and orderly public assemblies and processions
At the same time the Police have the statutory responsibility to maintain public safety and public order
In the scene on 12 June protesters unlawfully blocked roads paralysed traffic unlawfully assembled bricks metal rods iron poles wooden planks etc . They attacked police officers on duty and violently charged at the police lines . The Police are responsible for this and must take appropriate actions to restore public safety and public order as soon as possible
The Police have stringent guidelines on the use of force
Police officers will only use appropriate force when necessary
Police officers will issue warnings as far as possible before using force and allow the other party to obey police orders within a feasible range before using force
When the purpose of using force has been achieved the Police will stop using force
On 12 June after repeated advice and ineffective warnings the Police decided to use corresponding force to disperse the crowd and control the scene in response to the actual circumstances (over all ) situation and operational needs at the time . These included police batons pepper sprays tear gas canisters ammunition bullets rubber bullets and tear gas
The purpose of the Police Force is to ensure that public safety and public order will be protected from further damage and to protect the safety of the people present . This is lawful reasonable and necessary
Mr WANs motion mentions that the protests on that day were defined as riots but the SAR Government has not defined the incidents that took place in the Admiralty area on 12 June as riots
The Government has explained time and again that the Commissioner of Police used the term riot to describe the acts of some violent people using various kinds of weapons to deliberately attack and assault police officers
Such descriptions will not affect the decision of prosecution in future
The work of prosecution depends entirely on the evidence collected after an investigation and the decision of the Department of Justice DoJ in accordance with the Code of Procedure when seeking legal advice
There are two motions in todays debate which seek to request the Police to attack journalists
President any allegation must be based on evidence . Anyone who makes a complaint against the Police will be treated fairly and impartially based on facts and evidence under the existing complaint mechanism
The Police have statutory obligations to safeguard law and order public safety and public order . These are the duties and functions required by the law
Therefore both the media and the Police have their own responsibilities . The mutual understanding and cooperation between the two sides is conducive to the work of curbing the curfew
On this basis the Police have been providing as much assistance as possible to the media in their reporting work (with out ) affecting their operational potential
The Police have drawn up relevant guidelines to allow officers to identify and verify the identity of media workers through the documents or documents issued by media organizations or organizations during public activities or other police operations
I call on all workers to carry with them with them their press certificates or company documents or put on a face mask and a face shield during public interviews so as to facilitate the identification of police officers at the scene
When necessary the Police will dispatch media liaison teams to the scene to provide convenience and assistance
In large public events the Police will in the light of the development of the situation disseminate relevant messages to the public through different media in a timely manner to remind the public to be careful and to raise their awareness of their own protection
Members of the public including media workers should also heed the instructions of the police officers at the scene
We understand that some media workers are concerned about the actions taken by the Police on that day
I reiterate that the Police attach great importance to its relationship with the media and will do its best to allow and support media interviews
Mr TAM and Mr KWOK have moved a motion to question the Police for arresting protesters injured in public hospitals and for obtaining information on the injuries of protesters from the computer system of the Hospital Authority which is allegedly not authorized to do so
First of all I have to point out clearly that the Police have the statutory responsibility to prevent and investigate the offences and the health care personnel are responsible for rescuing the injured patients
The Police will do its best to assist all ambulance services to ensure that the injured persons can receive treatment as soon as possible
The Police and health care personnel have all along been working together on the basis of mutual understanding and mutual understanding which is in the public interest . In general police officers are in charge in AE departments
Deputy President first of all I would like to thank Members for their speeches and their views given to the Government
Regarding the importance of safeguarding national security our experience since June 2019 has given us a profound understanding and understanding
Since June 2019 there have been a large number of illegal acts of advocating Hong Kong independence and violent vandalism in Hong Kong
The rioters tried every means to block the road and tried every means to destroy shops and public facilities and threw a large number of petrol bombs
The rioters fired more than 5 000 petrol bombs and the Police seized more than 10 000 petrol bombs
Some 22 000 sq m of pavement has been excavated to cover 48 basketball courts and the 60 km long road barrier has been demolished equivalent to the height of 136 national flag and national emblem
The rioters even beat up and tied up people with different views and even illegally detained them
Rioters set a member of the public on fire and set him on fire causing serious bodily harm to him . A cleaner who was 70 years old was also hit in the head with a brick and died
After the implementation of the National Security Law on 30 June last year the Hong Kong National Security Law has played a deterrent role in reversing the chaotic situation of severe violence in society and greatly reduced the number of acts advocating Hong Kong independence
We have all felt the positive change . The violence on the streets has been greatly reduced and the acts of vandalizing order and social security have also been greatly reduced
Members of the public no longer have to worry about the sudden serious congestion on the roads the damage to the MTR stations and the major damage to the public facilities and shops or the violent attacks by rioters who hurled petrol bombs or set fires to the buildings . Hong Kong has generally returned to peace and the community has returned to normal . Apart from the epidemic the economy and peoples livelihood can be developed again
The legal rights and freedoms of the public will be protected
In the past I have seen some cases related to riots being heard in court and the court made deterrent and instructing rulings
At present the maximum penalty is imprisonment for five and a half years for rioting and related offences
Apart from other different detention terms other penalties include imprisonment in a rehabilitation centre a labour centre a detention centre and a curfew
Criminals involved in criminal damage have also been fined up to 230,000
Since the promulgation and implementation of the National Security Law the Government has actively implemented the legal system and enforcement mechanism to safeguard national security
The Committee on Safeguarding National Security chaired by the Chief Executive was established on 3 July 2020
The Hong Kong Police Force has also immediately set up a dedicated department for the National Security Administration to enforce the National Security Law
The implementation of the Hong Kong National Security Law will have a great deterrent effect on the active elements that endanger national security such as Hong Kong independence and collusion with foreign forces and greatly reduce the risk of endangerment of national security and violent acts
For example five months after the implementation of the Hong Kong National Security Law the number of people arrested for destructive acts caused by demonstrations has dropped by 50 %
Moreover many people who openly support Hong Kong independence collude with foreign powers and foreign forces and advocate violence have withdrawn from the scene and some related organizations have ceased operation or disbanded
The Hong Kong National Security Law enables Hong Kong to turn from chaos to governance and ensures that one country two systems will last forever and Hong Kong will be prosperous and stable in the long run
(how ever ) there are still many challenges in the future . The community has experienced more than one year of riots and the awareness of law enforcement has collapsed . This has dealt a blow to the good law and order and the rule of law in the past
Although the operation of society has generally returned to normal now the past riots and rioters have seriously undermined the mutual trust respect and lawful social culture that society has built over the years
The Police will continue to work hard to uphold the rule of law and communicate with various sectors of the community and establish relationships with the public through various activities such as contact and interaction joint combating of crimes and resistance against drugs
(how ever ) it is equally important for the community to build a common sense of awareness and a correct sense of values as well as a sense of mutual respect for each other and mutual assistance in law enforcement as well as a sense of social culture so that we especially young people can understand that no matter what our respective purposes are we (cannot ) challenge the law and we will definitely be held legally liable for our breaches of the law
The purpose of the Hong Kong National Security Law is to prevent stop and punish acts and activities which endanger national security and combat people who endanger national security by a very small percentage and protect the vast majority of people
Therefore apart from enforcing the law we should also attach importance to prevention and control
In the face of the severe blow inflicted and those who endanger national security may appear to be not very active on the surface but they may only be going underground . We must continue to step up our intelligence work examine changes in the international situation and actively strengthen our own capabilities and systems . We must give our all and give full play to our strengths
In order to prevent and curb acts that undermine national security publicity and education are particularly important . We will continue to work hard to enhance peoples understanding of the National Security Law and the external environment as well as to enhance peoples sense of national security and awareness of law enforcement
In this connection the SAR Government has been introducing the National Security Law and responding to the problems with different persons through different means and channels including various Economic and Trade Offices ETOs
We will step up publicity and education . For example the Government will organize a lecture series on the National Constitution Day this year with the theme of Constitution and National Security discussing the important concept of negating national security and exploring the relationship between the Constitution and the National Security Law as well as how to effectively safeguard national security sovereignty security and development interests
In the days to come the SAR Government will continue to step up publicity and education including using the annual Constitution Day and National Security Education Day to carry out public education work
Secretaries of State will also perform the duties and functions of the Hong Kong National Security Law in their respective areas
I urge Members to support this years Policy Address
President first of all I would like to thank Dr Elizabeth QUAT for proposing the motion and the two Members for proposing the amendments
The Security Bureau fully recognizes the importance of combating false information
Since the outbreak of black violence in 2019 there have been many false messages in society . They have continuously attempted to smear the Government causing hatred among the public against the Government and even advocating violence and disintegrating the community
For example during the black violence period there were rumours in the community that the Government would restrict people from withdrawing from the public transport or going out of the territory of the territory of the public and even impose a ban on footraces . These rumours intended to incite people against the Government . The relevant government departments have immediately clarified and pointed out that the rumours are untrue
Regarding the law enforcement actions taken by the Police during the riots although it has been clearly pointed out in the theme report published by IPCC that there is no evidence at all to support the allegation of the death of a person at Prince Edward Station of the 831 railway and that the allegation is untrustworthy and there is also the allegation that the victim of the 831 railway incident Mr Andrew HAN has uploaded a video clip of himself taking a selfie pose and died but there are still a large number of people who come to the MTR station from time to time to pay their respects . It can thus be seen that some of the false information is actually true
As a matter of fact in the recent incidents of violent clashes the attackers were often affected by the false information on the Internet and messages inciting violence and hatred and then committed some serious illegal acts . The wolf attack on 1 July is an example
False news and extreme ideology are very harmful to society and the Internet has made them spread more rapidly
The Secretary for Home Affairs will study seriously the experience and approaches adopted by other countries and places in dealing with fake news and fake information and provide reference for the next step of work
The Security Bureau will like other government departments do its best to assist in expeditiously clarifying false information and using all feasible administrative and legal means to combat false information that incites hatred and violence
Although there is currently no bogus news law the existing legal framework has provided the relevant provisions for handling information which has been disseminated improperly
For instance acts with incitement under sections 9 and 10 of the Crimes Ordinance or the publication of incitement are considered to be in breach of criminal offences
In addition various offences under the National Security Law also have specific provisions to deal with the dissemination of provocative or extreme thoughts . Among them Article 21 and Article 23 of the National Security Law prohibit any person from inciting or assisting or inciting another person to implement secession and subversion of the national regime . Article 27 prohibit the promotion of terrorism and the incitation to carry out terrorist activities
I must stress once again that the Internet is not an unquestionable virtual world
Most of the laws targeting the real world are applicable to the Internet world so people must use the Internet legally and responsibly
Any acts of inciting others to break the law or to commit cyberbullying acts be they online or criminal are subject to the relevant legislation
The Government hopes that members of the general public will still see the truth with a clear mind . They should not believe in false news and false news and they should not directly or indirectly support violence
We also hope that the whole community will work together to say no to violence so that society can get back on the right track
I believe Members must have a lot of valuable views on the legislation to combat online false information
I will listen carefully to the speeches of Members and respond to them later and make appropriate supplementary remarks
President first of all I would like to express my (heart felt ) gratitude to the Bills Committee Chairman Dr Elizabeth QUAT Deputy Chairman Ms YUNG Hoi-yan and all members of the Legislative Council Secretariat and Legal Advisers for their efforts in the Bill so that the scrutiny of the Bill can be completed smoothly
I am also grateful to different organizations and individuals for expressing their valuable views on the Bill
The Bills Committee has held five meetings to discuss in depth the objectives and provisions of the Bill
The existing legislation does not target at the offence of embezzlement or stealing
Depending on the circumstances of each case the relevant acts will be prosecuted under different provisions of the existing legislation such as the use of computers for the purpose of misconduct the use of computers for the purpose of criminal or unscrupulous intent the offence of disorderly order and behaviour in a public place and the offence of vandalism of public order and so on . (how ever ) there are certain restrictions and difficulties in the prosecution of such offences and the penalties are relatively light
In this connection we propose in the Bill to amend the Crimes Ordinance to provide for four new offences
Firstly the offence of concessions is to deal with the acts of concealing or concealing from others when there is reasonable expectation of privacy
Secondly the authorities should enact legislation to make illegal filming or observation of private parts offence to deal with the so called group photography and action of aerial photography
We have also proposed the enactment of two offences in relation to the image of publication including the first one which is the private image obtained from the publication of the offence of misconduct in office or unlawful filming or observation of private parts and the second one which is the private image obtained (with out ) the consent of the author of the publication or the threat of publication
Among the cases the victim may have agreed to take a private photo but did not agree to the subsequent declaration of behaviour such as a retaliatory action
The maximum penalty for four new offences is imprisonment for five years
The Government also proposes to include a defence clause in the legislation so that the person committing the offence for hisher own purpose may affirm himself or herself as having a legal right or a reasonable excuse by the court as a person who has failed to comply with the defence clause
I am very glad to see that Members and the general public have widely supported the legislative proposals in the Bill
Members of the public agree that the act of taking pictures secretly and releasing private images (with out ) consent should be criminalized
During the process of legislation one of our principles is to send a clear message to the public that such despicable acts should not be committed and that serious consequences will be faced if such acts are committed
By setting down specific offences lawbreakers may be prevented from committing offences of embezzlement of contract and secrely taking pictures and unauthorized publication of private photos thereby protecting the persons who may be affected
At the same time the Bill seeks to ensure that the scope of offences is clear through the drafting of the provisions so that the innocent will not be convicted (with out ) knowing
In the course of enacting the legislation we have continuously improved the proposal to make the Bill more comprehensive
For example the Government consulted the Panel on Security of the Legislative Council in January this year and added provisions to the Bill to deal with the problem of video recording of high noise and loud noise and to cover up the threat of publication of a video recording of a loud noise (with out ) the consent of the relevant party concerned
During the deliberation of the Bills Committee the Government has carefully considered the views of members and will propose amendments to the Bill by committee of the whole Council
Later on I will move a total of 15 amendments to the Bill including amending the definition of privacy image to cover images that have been amended and empowering the Court to give an order to deal with unlawful images of privacy
These amendments have responded to the views expressed by the Bills Committee and the general public . I will explain the amendments proposed by the Government in detail at the Committee stage later
President I move that the Second Reading of the Crimes Amendment Bill 2021 be resumed . I hope Members will support and pass the Second Reading of the Bill as well as the amendments that I will later propose in the committee of the whole Council for the purpose of enacting a law to prohibit serious infringement of the privacy and sexual autonomy of the victim
Deputy President first of all I would like to thank Ms Elizabeth QUAT for proposing the motion and Mr KWOK (Wai-) keung and Mr Tommy CHEUNG for proposing the amendments
President more than 10 months have passed since June 2019 and there have been a lot of violent and unlawful acts in Hong Kong . The rioters have blocked roads vigorously vandalized shops and public facilities recklessly threw a large number of petrol bombs and some rioters even tied up members of the public who held different views and illegally arrested and assaulted them
The violent acts of the rioters have resulted in the injuries of many people including police officers . One member of the public was killed by a rioter who threw a brick at him . Another member of the public was seriously injured after being set on fire by a rioter . I express my severe condemnation to these violent acts
Having consulted the Financial Services and the Treasury Bureau and the Legal Aid Department my consolidated reply to the Members questions is as follows 1 Some organizations have used different nicknames to engage in online mutual destruction . Some organizations have been uncovered by the Police and involved in criminal offences
The Police have arrested the people involved and frozen the assets concerned for further investigation
Members of the public should be alert to this in order to avoid being deceived into using the median wage
The use of any amount of money from a reward must be lawful
The law enforcement agencies will monitor and monitor the operation of these funds in accordance with the actual circumstances and will deal with any unlawful acts in accordance with the law
The SAR Government will not comment on the operation of the individual self – claimed funds
Depending on the purpose of the online fundraising activities the relevant legislation applicable to the fund activities may also regulate the use of funds raised on online platforms for activities in Hong Kong
The Government has also noted that although the activities and platforms of the activities of other jurisdictions are subject to different regulations the relevant regulations are generally based on the nature of the activities and the use of the funds raised rather than the way the funds are raised
The Government will continue to pay attention to and draw reference from the latest developments of other places in regulating online fund raising activities
Third the law enforcement department under Cap . 91 of the Legal Aid Ordinance is the Legal Aid Department LAD
Any person who applies for legal aid generally needs to pass the means test and the case test under the Legal Aid Ordinance
If passed LAD may in accordance with the established criteria appoint a private practising solicitor representative from the List of Legal Aid Solicitors LAD to act in the case
Among the criminal cases there were only a few cases in which the Legal Aid Department LAD appointed a second counsel . LAD advised that in the light of the above situation individual solicitors or solicitors appointed by LAD may propose to another solicitor who is not appointed by LAD to assist in the handling of the case upon the solicitors consent
President I understand the concerns of Mr CHOW
In the main reply just now I mentioned that the Legal Aid Department LAD had provided information about the responsibilities of LAD under the relevant legislation
The Director of Legal Aid also has a committee to monitor the performance of lawyers appointed to handle cases
I will convey this message to LAD
Earlier when I answered other questions in other Council meetings I made it clear that the funding arrangement will enable people who have broken the law to act in a systematic and systematic manner . This is the key point that we must address
Therefore it is one of our important action targets to restrict the illegal use of capital
Besides we have also enacted a minor rule after the enactment of the National Security Law to empower the Police to freeze the funds raised for conducting activities that endanger national security and the funds to be benefited for the sake of safeguarding national security
The Government has consulted the Bills Committee on all the proposed amendments including the technical amendments and the Bills Committees views have not changed
In the following I will briefly explain the purpose of the Governments proposed amendments and the four major policy issues covered in the amendments
Firstly the definition of private image covers the image that has been altered and one of the recommendations in the Bill is to deal with the act of publishing or threatening to publish a private image (with out ) consent
If the intent of the publisher is to know or disregard the image of the publisher or to threaten the publisher such an image may cause the victim to be insulted frightened or disturbed and thus constitute an offence
During the deliberation of the Bills Committee some public views considered that the Bill should cover the image of the broadcast programme ie . the image of a person being projected on a photo
We understand that with the development of technology it is becoming increasingly easy to create a very symbolic image
The image of the programme is that the victim is harmed not less than the image of the person who gave away the truth
After careful consideration we propose to draw reference from the similar legislation in Singapore in terms of the definition of private image . The definition of private image under section 159aa of the original Bill includes provisions covering the image of a person being revised to indicate the private part of the person or the private action of the person unless the image has been revised to the point where the unreasonable person believes that it is unlawful
The offence of not agreeing to publish or threatening to publish a private image after the amendment applies to a private image that has been amended or has been removed to enhance the protection of the victim
In the amendment we have also adopted the objective test of reasonable person to strike a balance by removing the image of the victim which is obviously not described to clearly define the scope of the offence
We emphasize that the reason for assigning the offence as a crime is that the person concerned does not agree to the relevant behaviour
The legislative intent of the Government is to protect the privacy and sexual autonomy of the victim and it is not intended to interfere in the private will of the public
(how ever ) we have considered that in individual cases the person concerned may not be found to have gone to court for work . For example the person concerned may not be found to have gone to court for work because the image of the person concerned (cannot ) capture the appearance of the person concerned or the person concerned is not willing to go to court for work . If the prosecution has to prove (with out ) any reasonable doubt that the person concerned has not agreed to the relevant behaviour there may be certain difficulties which may lead to the conviction of the defendant
This is not the outcome we wish to see
In our view if the prosecution can prove that the defendant has ignored the consent of the defendant and confirmed other elements of the offence even if the defendant has not testified to prove his will the defendant has already committed the offence
Therefore we propose that the three offences mentioned just now be excluded from the list of offences against which the owner has not given his consent
After the amendment the prosecution does not need to prove that the offender has not been given the consent but still has to prove that the defendant has not cared whether the defendant has agreed to or was observed or dismissed or has not cared whether the defendant has agreed to the release and has established other elements of the offence
The relevant amendment has already been agreed by the Bills Committee and we propose to make corresponding amendments to the title of section 159AAC to express more clearly and briefly the nature of the offence
3 Unlawful filming or observation of private property is an innocent element of crime . The Ordinance Cap . 159AAC1B provides that the offence of unlawful filming or observation of private property is committed firstly for sexual purposes or secondly to be committed by one or all persons in an innocent manner for the benefit of others
Article 159AAC III further elaborates on the definition of benefit
In the discussions of the Bills Committee we have stressed that illegal filming or observation of private crime involves deliberate filming of groupings or violent acts
If a person has unethically filmed or observed private parts of another person regardless of whether the person concerned has done so out of greed stimulation or curiosity or to bully others or for no specific purpose and the person concerned has unethically filmed or observed the matter there is still a chance that he may be liable for the offence of unlawful filming or observation of private parts of another person under section 159AAC
In order to express more clearly that the term dishonesty refers to the way of filming or observation we propose to amend the element of dishonestly benefiting from others conduct to the effect that the defendant will behave dishonestly and remove the definition of benefiting from others conduct
The relevant amendments have been discussed in detail by the Bills Committee and have obtained the consent of the Bills Committee
Fourthly the Court of Appeal should issue a deterrent order . From the victims perspective even if the person who posted the private video has been convicted of the offence if the private video is still circulated on the Internet it will cause persistent harm to the victim
In this connection some members of the public have expressed their views requesting the Court to be empowered by law to order the removal and dismissal of the image of the person involved
Although there are already different ways to prevent the continued circulation of illegal content at present we consider that there are justifications for the existing mechanism to provide a legal basis for the court to order the defendant or other persons including the individual or online service provider to remove delete or destroy the relevant private image in accordance with the circumstances . For this reason we have proposed an amendment to add a section 4 of the Bill to provide for the handling of illegal contents
President there is no need for me to make any clarification
President today I submit to the Legislative Council the Government Brief in response to the Report No . 74 submitted to the Legislative Council by the Public Accounts Committee on 15 July this year
I welcome the Report of the Committee and I would also like to extend my (heart felt ) thanks to Mr Abraham SHEK Chairman of the Committee and all members for their time and efforts
The Government has accepted various recommendations of the Comprehensive Social Security Assistance CSSA Scheme and has given a detailed account of the specific responses of the relevant bureaux and departments in the review
The Standing Committee has conducted an open hearing in respect of the support for arts and sports development funds for sports development in the Report No . 74 and the two chapters of the Olympic Committee of the Hong Kong Sports Federation
Here I would like to briefly explain the major measures and progress taken by the Government and the relevant organizations in respect of the proposals of the Committee
First of all the effective management of the Arts and Sports Development Fund SDF is not only in line with the principle of value for money but also conducive to the Governments promotion of sports development in Hong Kong
LCSD has also improved the arrangement for conducting site inspections of local international events to enhance monitoring
In respect of providing subsidies to HKGCC HKADC has urged HKCCPC to adopt effective measures to follow up on the recommendations made by SFOC and the Stock Exchange of Hong Kong Stock Board SEHK and has requested HKCCPC to submit an action plan to explain how to follow up on the recommendations made by SFOC and SEHK
In order to emphasize the importance of this action plan the Home Affairs Bureau has included the Actions Plan on Timely Submission in the Conditions for Disbursement of Funding under the Strategy Plan for Looking Ahead 2025 to be implemented by HKJC
In September 2020 the Bureau submitted its action plan to the Home Affairs Bureau and has taken a series of improvement measures
In order to improve the operation of the Board of Directors and the Bills Committee the Board of Directors and the Bills Committee have resumed the auditing committee in accordance with their duties and taken additional administrative measures to properly keep the agenda and meetings records of the Board of Directors and the Bills Committee as well as to allow all members of the Bills Committee to fill the first tier of the Report of Interests
In order to further improve the operation the Board of Directors has decided to review the structure and major governance areas of the Board and its committees . The review is expected to be completed by June 2021
In respect of government funding and monitoring the Home Affairs Bureau will continue to closely monitor the performance of HKGM in providing subsidies for football development in Hong Kong through the Fund
The Home Affairs Bureau has taken on board the recommendations of NDO and the Task Force on the implementation of the 2025 2025 Scheme with a view to providing timely funding for three football seasons from 2020 to 2021 and 2022 and 2023
In the funding agreements signed between the Home Affairs Bureau and TBCH performance targets and indicators will be set out
If there is a significant gap between the performance targets and the performance targets HKBA will consider reducing or shortening the funding period with a view to making greater financial impact and making expected improvements
Before the end of 2022 the Home Affairs Bureau will conduct an interim review on the performance of the 2025 Scheme implemented by HKASA and decide on the basis of the results of the review on the allocation of funding to HKASA
SFOC will also take up the responsibility of reviewing its operation and internal monitoring mechanism and make appropriate improvement measures . The Home Affairs Bureau will closely monitor the progress of the review . The Government has all along attached great importance to the proposals put forward by the Audit Commission and the Commission
Later I will pass a motion on the two other bills proposed in order in my name on the Agenda
These two motions seek to adjust the first deadline for the Legal Aid Department LAD and to amend the Crimes Legal Aid Rules in 2020 to adjust the legal aid fees for criminal cases
Let me first introduce the first motion
According to section 7A of the Legal Aid Ordinance the Legislative Council may amend the financial resources amount specified in sections 5A and 5A of the Legal Aid Ordinance by means of this resolution
Legal aid service is an important part of the legal system of Hong Kong
The purpose of the legal aid policy is to ensure that all persons who comply with all the requirements of the Legal Aid Ordinance and have reasonable grounds to lodge or defend their cases in the courts of Hong Kong will not be unable to seek justice through the law due to a lack of financial means
If a person wishes to obtain legal aid he must pass the legal and financial tests required under the Legal Aid Ordinance and the court examination
Therefore the Comprehensive Social Security Assistance Scheme CSSA and the Comprehensive Social Security Assistance Scheme CSSA are subject to different amounts of financial resources under different means
The Chief Executive announced in the Annex to the 2019 Policy Address that the Government proposed to raise the financial eligibility limits for legal aid applicants under the Common Scheme and the Supplementary Scheme by about 30 % respectively
First of all we propose to increase the financial eligibility limits of general schemes by about 30 % ie . to 42,400 from the current 37,130
We have also made comprehensive reference to a series of cumulative changes in the average expenditure indicators of legal professionals such as the litigation costs of civil legal aid cases the nominal wage index and the rental index of private offices and compared them with the changes in the Consumer Price Index C
Besides we propose to increase the financial eligibility limit of the Scheme by about 30 % that is from the existing 1,535,600 to 2,150,000
According to the application records of LAD in 2019 the average and the maximum 10 % of the financial assessment of applicants approved under the Common Scheme or the Supplementary Scheme were far lower than the new financial amounts of the relevant proposals
Therefore I believe that the proposal of adjusting the service price in one go can enable more people who comply with the Ordinance to continue to seek legal services (with out ) having to resort to the means to do so
Deputy President the legal aid system in Hong Kong has always been effective
I would like to point out here that about 90 % of the cases rejected were not conducted by the Legal Aid Department LAD due to the failure to pass the credit review rather than the economic review
Criminal review is a statutory mechanism to prevent legal aid from being abused because of relaxed economic review thereby ensuring the proper use of public money
We have consulted the Panel on Administration of Justice and Legal Services of the Legislative Council on the proposed increase on 27 April . Members have absolutely no objection to the proposal mentioned just now
If the motion is passed by the Legislative Council the adjusted financial eligibility threshold will come into effect after the gazettal
Now please allow me to explain the second motion
Under section 22A of the Legal Aid Ordinance the Legislative Council may amend section 18A5 of the Decision in the light of the passage of the decision to waive the amount of the first payment of the legal aid directorate degree specified in section 18A and section 19B1A so that the director of legal aid who is specified in section 18B1A may reduce the amount of his or her reserve in the event of a serious hardship
If a legal aid recipient withdraws or retains any money or property from a legal aid case in accordance with the requirements of the Legal Aid Ordinance Cap . 18A1 he or she is required to repay the money or property withdrawn or retained from the case to the Director of Legal Aid
The legal aid recipient must pay the sum to the Director of LAD with the money or property he has recovered or retained . This is known as the first loan of the Director of LAD
According to section 18A5 of the Legal Aid Formulary the first payment scheme for the Director of Legal Aid is not applicable to the previous spouse or the previous spouse
In addition the Director of Legal Aid may exercise his discretion under section 19b1 of the Legal Aid Ordinance to reduce the amount of his or her reserve in respect of any person suffering from serious hardship including statutory penalty
We propose that the amount specified in section 18A5 be increased from 4,800 to 9,100 and that the amount specified in section 19B1A be increased from 57,400 to 108,850 with the proposed increase of 89.6 %
Therefore it is unnecessary to amend the Legal Aid Ordinance to introduce a review mechanism every year
We already consulted the Panel on Administration of Justice and Legal Services on the review results and the way forward in April 2018 . Members did not have any objection at the meeting
If the Legislative Council passes the resolution we will publish the report and implement the proposal
Under section 9A1 of the Ordinance the rules of amending must be approved by the Legislative Council
To ensure that the Legal Aid Department and the Department of Justice do not allow unfair advantage to be taken by any party when seeking the services of the same group of lawyers DoJ will refer to the same fee form and appoint private professional counsel and lawyer to handle criminal cases on behalf of the prosecution
As for legal representative lawyers who are serving as a legal representative for the Magistrates Courts and the Court of Appeal through the service of duty lawyers they will be paid the fees of duty lawyers
During the two years of the review the Government mainly considered whether there were any difficulties in making reference to the general price changes and hiring barristers and solicitors
Deputy President in reference of the two years of review completed in 2018 that is from July 2016 to July 2018 the percentage of increase in the value of a consumer product has increased by 4 % . Therefore we propose to increase the three fees by a corresponding rate of 4 %
The general price changes after July 2018 will be reflected in the next round of review in two years time
In January 2019 we consulted the Panel on Administration of Justice and Legal Services on the findings of the review and there was no objection to the proposal
In the implementation of the criminal legal aid fees increased by LAD the Department of Justice will adjust the fee list in an administrative manner
The Administration will also adjust the fees for legal aid services accordingly through administrative means
If the Legislative Council passes the resolution we will implement the Amendment Rules on 20 July this year
Deputy President I implore Members to support the three motions
President I move that the motion under my name as printed on the Agenda that is this Council agrees to appoint Mr Anthony CHEUNG as the Chief Justice of CFA
The Court of Final Appeal is the final court of the Hong Kong Court of Final Appeal in respect of civil and criminal appeals
The Chief Justice of CFA is the Court of Final Appeal of CFA and also the head of the Judiciary . He is responsible for the administrative work of the Judiciary and the enforcement of various statutory powers and functions conferred on the Judiciary
Article 88 of the Basic Law stipulates that Judges of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region shall be appointed by the Chief Executive in accordance with the recommendations of the local judges and the independent committee of the legal profession and the other significant persons concerned
The Judicial Recommendations Committee JORC is a statutory body established under the Judicial Recommendations Ordinance Cap . 92 which is responsible for performing the functions of the independent committee under Article 88 of the Basic Law
Regarding the appointment of the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal the Basic Law stipulates in Article 90 that the Chief Executive shall in addition to acting in accordance with the procedural practice specified in Article 88 of the Basic Law obtain the consent of the Legislative Council and report the appointment to the Standing Committee of the National Peoples Congress NPCSC for the record
According to Article 92 of the Basic Law Judges of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region should be elected according to their judicial and professional abilities
At the same time Article 90 of the Basic Law also stipulates that the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region shall be appointed as a Chinese citizen of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region a permanent resident of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region which has no right of abode (over seas )
The Judicial Officers Recommendation Committee REO is chaired by the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal CFA and other members include the Secretary for Justice and the seven other members appointed by the Chief Executive
They are two Judges a barrister appointed after consulting the Hong Kong Bar Association a solicitor appointed after consulting the Hong Kong Law Society and three people who have nothing to do with law practice
The incumbent Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal John MALLET will retire on 11 January 2021
The Commission held three meetings between November 2019 and January 2020 to decide on the selection procedures and appointment criteria of the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal and the suitable candidates for appointment
Following the selection process under the agreement the Recommendation Committee recommended the Chief Executive to appoint the former Judge of the Court of Final Appeal Mr Anthony CHEUNG as the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal and the recommendation would come into effect on 11 January 2021
She joined the Judiciary in 2001 and was appointed as the Chief Justice of the High Court in 2011 to take charge of the judicial and administrative affairs of the High Court
Mr CHOW was subsequently appointed as a permanent judge of the Court of Final Appeal in October 2018
Dr CHEUNG was not able to stay calm and was highly respected in the Judiciary and the legal sector
Dr CHEUNG is a highly talented judge who has written important and significant bills with significant significance in many major and complicated cases including constitutional and public law cases
The Chief Justice of the High Court served as the Chief Justice from 2011 to 2018 and had to perform a rather heavy administrative duty
On matters relating to the operation of courts and their strategy he is also the principal advocate of the Court of Final Appeal CFA
From this we can see that the Judges with rich administrative experience and outstanding leadership are capable of performing the duties of the head of the Judiciary
The Chief Executive has already taken on board the recommendation of appointing Mr CHEUNG Kwai Lams as the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal
If the Legislative Council agrees to this the appointment will come into effect on 11 January 2021
President in accordance with the procedures passed by the House Committee in the past the Government notified the House Committee on 24 March 2020 and the Executive Council set up a recommendation committee to recommend the appointment
On 26 May 2020 the Clerk to the Government Representative and Chairman of the Commission attended the meeting of the (sub committee ) on Proposed Senior Judicial Appointment and responded to Members questions
I would like to take this opportunity to sincerely thank the Chairman of the (sub committee ) Dr Priscilla LEUNG and other committees for their support
Deputy President I move that my third motion as printed on the Agenda be passed
President my reply to the question raised by Ms Elizabeth QUAT is as follows The rule of law is the core value of Hong Kong and the cornerstone of Hong Kongs success
There are many essential elements in a society governed by the rule of law including clear laws equality of persons before the law public awareness of the law and independent judicial power
In order to ensure that Hong Kong residents are protected by Article 25 of the Basic Law and are equally protected by the law all persons who make a law or defend themselves with reasonable grounds will not be deprived of the right to seek justice or to protect their rights through the law due to their economic abilities . This is precisely the important role played by the Legal Aid or Legal Aid Scheme in safeguarding the rule of law in Hong Kong all along
Section 91 of the Legal Aid Ordinance stipulates that only applicants who have passed the criminal and economic assessment at the same time may be granted legal aid
Upon approval of the legal aid application the Director of Legal Aid may perform the duties for the solicitor through the professional lawyers of the Legal Aid Department LAD but he may also appoint the director or solicitor to perform the duties on behalf of the private practice lawyer nominated under the Legal Aid Lawyers List
(how ever ) if the nominated lawyer is not an appropriate candidate such as the number of legal aid cases handled by the nominated lawyer has reached the upper limit and the past performance of the lawyer is poor and has been penalized by the regulatory body for disciplinary offences or may jeopardize the interests of the solicitor in the litigation due to the language used in the litigation or the solicitor has repeatedly or very late in the litigation under unreasonable circumstances refused to attend the hearing or refused to attend the hearing due to the lack of legal advice from the regulatory body the solicitor may not be eligible to receive legal aid
From 2015 to 2020 the Legal Aid Department received a total of 4 680 applications for legal aid cases relating to judicial review
These judicial review applications accounted for about 4.5 % of all legal aid applications . Of the 386 applications approved by LAD the success rate was about 8 % and only accounted for 0.75 % of all legal aid applications
The legal costs of legal aid cases can be said to account for about 10 % of all legal aid costs
Deputy President my reply to the various parts of the question is as follows 1 The information on legal aid cases is set out in Annex I to the Judicial Review Ordinance
In the process we will consult the Legal Aid Services Council LAMB which is a statutory body comprising representatives from two law firms the Hong Kong Bar Association HKBA and The Law Society of Hong Kong HKBA . There are also some people from the legal profession in LAMB who can be said to be a very representative statutory body
Therefore in this review we fully understand the concerns of Members and we have given a very positive response in the main reply . We will definitely conduct a review on the areas that we consider feasible and we will not withdraw from the review . But we will definitely consider the areas that are feasible fair and just
President my reply to Dr Priscilla LEUNGs question is set out in parts 1 and 3 of the main reply . On 4 September this year the Chief Executive put forth four actions in the hope of resolving the predicament in Hong Kong
One of the proposals is to invite social leaders experts and scholars to conduct independent studies and reviews on deep social problems and to make recommendations to the Government
While the Government is making preparations for the incident the violent clashes in recent years have significantly increased the level of violence inflicted by radical protesters . Therefore the Governments task at hand is to stop the violent acts as soon as possible
We will expeditiously commence our work on the independent work group on the profound conflicts in Hong Kong upon a more stable situation
The scope and model of the study of the independent team will be submitted to the team members for deliberation but it is expected that the study will draw reference from the approach adopted by the British Government in handling local disturbances in 2011 . Through listening to public views and making reference to the reports of different organizations experts and scholars the study will examine the causes of the social incidents that have occurred in the past few months and draw reference from them to study the deep – rooted problems in Hong Kong society
Meanwhile the Independent Police Complaints Council IPCC has set up a task force and an international expert panel to examine and announce to the public the major public events in Hong Kong since 9 June as well as the corresponding actions taken by the Police in phases
We understand that the first report will cover the incidents that took place at the MTR Tai Chi Centre in Yuen Long on 31 August and the incident at the New Housing Development Complex in New South Lanka . IPCC has also set up a number of channels for stakeholders including the media and members of the public to provide information . The Government will pay close attention to IPCCs report and recommendations and will study carefully before deciding how to follow up
President I thank Mr LEUNG for his views . We will definitely give due consideration to his views
We also hope that the timetable will be set and we are all very concerned about this
Thirdly we must really deal with these deep contradictions including studying the experience of the United Kingdom
The Government hopes that we can do something . We have set up a committee and set up an independent task force in the hope of finding a way out
The Government will certainly provide support in respect of resources (man power ) (man power ) and even the areas in which we can provide support . Otherwise it will be meaningless
President I thank Mr WOK for his views and supplementary question
(how ever ) they have been very serious in this regard with the aim of finding out the truth and finding out the direction for the future
The report published early next year is not the final report but the first stage of investigation
I would like to quote from the last sentence of the motion which reads The Independent Police Complaints Council has stated that the preliminary report issued by the Audit Commission will cover sufficient facts and preliminary conclusions so that the Police and the SAR Government can take appropriate next steps on the basis of the Constitution to lead Hong Kong towards a stable and sound society
As I have pointed out in the main reply the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal has made it clear that he has requested the leaders of various courts to study different options so as to achieve the goal of handling the case expeditiously in a feasible manner
When implementing the new measures the Judiciary will also consult a series of stakeholders concerned and they will also be involved in the process so there must be communication between the Judiciary and the public
President the poverty line was established to provide an objective tool for measuring the poverty situation in Hong Kong so as to continuously monitor and understand the situation of poverty formulate poverty alleviation policies and examine the effectiveness of policies and provide a common foundation for discussing the poverty situation in society
The Census and Statistics Department and the Office of the Government Economist also update the poverty statistics annually on the basis of the poverty line analysis framework with a view to regularly monitoring the poverty situation in Hong Kong
So far the Government has published eight annual reports on poverty
My reply to the various parts of the question raised by Mr Tommy CHEUNG is as follows 1 The assets of households and individuals are private and highly sensitive information . It is difficult and unreliable to collect such data through household survey
The Census and Statistics Department has actually attempted to do so but as many respondents were unwilling to disclose the information on asset values it was ultimately unsuccessful
The concept of relatively poor is adopted by most of the developed economies including the Economic Cooperation and Development Organizations and the European Union
According to the statistics of the Concept Study the international comparison is simple and easy to understand
For many years some NGOs in Hong Kong have adopted the concept of relative poverty as the basis of their analysis of poverty and this reflects a certain degree of credibility of the relevant analysis framework
The concept of absolute poverty aims to measure the standard of adequate survival or basic living needs . For example the World Bank has set the international poverty line at US1.9 per day . This kind of standard is more like the development of economic wealth
The third problem is that the population of Hong Kong is ageing and the number of retired elderly people is on the increase . Since most of them do not have fixed income they are statistically classified as poor
The Government and the Commission of Poverty understand that the single indicator of poverty as an indicator of the analysis framework of the poverty line of household income is valid
Therefore the Commission agreed to introduce some supplementary analyses so that the public can have a more comprehensive understanding of the poverty situation
While some elderly households may have a certain amount of assets or their children who are not living in the same household may directly pay the expenses the poverty line framework does not take these factors into account and may thus underestimate the actual living standard of elderly people
Therefore when the Government analyses the situation of the poor elderly it will at the same time extract other statistical data and make supplementary analyses from various perspectives so as to fully reflect the living conditions of the poor elderly and to a certain extent supplement the current analysis framework (with out ) taking into account the asset limit
In respect of elderly people with a certain degree of value of income from poverty in the Hong Kong Poverty Situation Report 2019 a dedicated analysis has been published . It is estimated that those living in home ownership where there is no mortgage or loan and the monthly annuity or mortgage value of the property are not lower than the poverty line . All members of the public aged 55 or above may be entitled to a certain amount of housing to provide for their families
I would like to clarify two points
These problems are complicated in a nutshell
This year is the second year
In 2020 the Government has spent some 300 billion on the Anti epidemic Fund AEF . Most of the people in need of support for employment are not included in AEF because it is a one off arrangement . This is our defect
The simplest example is the Comprehensive Social Security Assistance CSSA Scheme . Considering how much money has been spent on CSSA how much help has been provided to the elderly to reduce their poverty by 2.2 %?
According to the Governments Plan for Rehabilitation the estimated annual income of about 2 million is about 5,000 per month which has already exceeded the poverty line of 4,500 for single elderly persons
Earlier on the Government introduced a minimum wage or a safety net to protect some people but it still failed to do so
All of them are eligible for working family allowance
We all understand that poverty is a very complicated social issue
In fact the poverty alleviation effect this year is reduced by 5 % or so which is the best in the past three years . It is not bad but it (cannot ) solve the economic environment . Trade disputes trade conflicts between the country and the United States coupled with the black violence which is very complicated as we know in 2019 have dealt a blow to the economic environment of the community
(how ever ) we hope that with the economic recovery and stabilization of the situation in 2020 the most important thing is that employment will be stable . This is important
Later on I found that we should conduct a thorough study and so we have been continuously enhancing the system moving forward and learning
Deputy President the review and reorganization of the government structure is originally a very loyal and pragmatic issue which can be discussed jointly with Members in the Council
(how ever ) Mr Kenneth LEUNG who moved the motion is justified in saying that he considers that the current Government has continuously made policy blunders and there are problems with the policy areas functions and work coordination among Policy Bureaux . Dr Helena WONG who proposed the amendment has also retained the wording that the current Government has continuously made policy blunders
As the Chief Secretary for Administration I must give a reply and clarify this point on behalf of the SAR Government so as to be fair
The responsible team of the current Government is composed of people who are steadfast in their efforts in seeking changes at a timely and pragmatic manner . With the support of more than 170 000 civil service personnel we have been working tirelessly to overcome the difficulties
Over the past four years or so the Chief Executive has gradually implemented the manifesto and the five pledges in the Policy Address when she was running in the election
The incumbent Government is also committed to economic development and improving peoples livelihood
We advocate that we should seek changes in a stable and orderly manner attach importance to long term planning and abandon the short term (mind set ) . We also care about the grass roots and other disadvantaged groups and we are determined to build a caring and inclusive society
Over the past four years or so we have seen quite a number of results in different areas of the current term of Government
In respect of the economy and finance we have developed new areas including the joint opening up of the Hong Kong Stock Connect and the ShenzhenHong Kong Stock Connect and the basic implementation of Hong Kong and the Mainland free trade
Apart from consolidating the traditional industries with advantages the Government has also set up the Innovation and Technology Bureau to enhance the (over all ) competitiveness of Hong Kong through promoting the application of innovation and technology
The economic achievements of Hong Kong are based on our persistence and respect for the rule of law . We will continue to develop Hong Kong into a centre for international legal and dispute resolution services in the Asia Pacific region and enhance Hong Kongs position in the relevant sectors such as international legal and dispute resolution
Hong Kong has a high level of global economic support in many areas
This year Hong Kong has once again been awarded the title of the worlds freest economy by the United States Convention and Exchanges and Closures Foundation which is the third time in a row that Hong Kong has been awarded the title of the freest economy in the world for 13 consecutive years
Hong Kong was also awarded with the International Institute for Management Development in Lausanne Switzerland and ranked it among the most competitive economies in the world . Canada was also awarded with the Fisheries Institute of Canada and ranked it among the best in the world in terms of economic freedom
Deputy President these achievements are not easy to come by and they are the fruits of the concerted efforts of Hong Kong people
In addition we have also made comprehensive efforts to further strengthen the relationship between Hong Kong and the Mainland and our major (over seas ) trading partners . Apart from setting up more liaison offices in the Mainland the total number of liaison offices has been increased from three in the previous term of the Government to 11 and they are currently located in the eastern part of the Mainland the southern part of the Mainland the northwestern part of the Mainland and the central part of the Mainland . Last year we also set up an Economic and Trade Offices ETO in Yee Ka Tat
The Government attaches great importance to improving peoples livelihood and supporting the disadvantaged
As we can see from the Budget just now the social welfare expenditure in 2017 and 2018 was as high as 73.3 billion which was a significant increase of 71.5 % as compared with the expenditure of 4.28 billion in the year of its inception on 13 November 2012
In the area of poverty alleviation elderly care and home ownership we have introduced a number of important relief measures including the Old Age Living Allowance the Low Income Working Family Allowance Scheme on Preliminary Rehabilitation Services for Schools as well as the 2 fare concession scheme to green green minibuses
Moreover we have made a groundbreaking proposal for formulating the first official poverty line in Hong Kong
In the third consecutive year the poverty population was below 1 million and the poverty rate remained at a low level of 14.3 %
I have to emphasize that we are not implementing social welfareism to spend a lot of money to please the public . Instead we are providing targeted and visionary measures to step up our efforts in assisting the disadvantaged to actively address the poverty problem in Hong Kong
As Members have said just now this is the basis of the sustainable development of Hong Kong society economy and peoples livelihood
The Government strives to promote land supply in a stable and sustainable manner and it has adopted a multidisciplinary approach to develop land supply strategies with a view to achieving gradual results
As regards the private market it is expected that in the next three to four years the supply of primary residential properties will reach 94 000 units which is 45 % higher than that when the current Government took office
Starting from the year of 2017 the Hong Kong Housing Authority and the Hong Kong Housing Society HKHS are expected to build around 94 500 public housing units
Besides there are also many results of governance in other policy areas . To put it simply we have implemented the Productivity Scheme launched the Governments Electronic Health Record Sharing System implemented new agricultural policies opened the Guangdong – Hong Kong Island Line East section of the Kwun Tong Line and South Island Line East section of the Guangzhou – Shenzhen – Hong Kong Express Rail Link and implemented free quality kindergarten education since the school year of 2017 – 2018
During this term of Government we have conducted public consultation on a number of important issues affecting the future development of Hong Kong . We have also set out some policy directions such as the Long Term Housing Strategy retirement protection population policy regulation of private health care institutions voluntary health insurance policy (blue print ) on four environmental issues and railway development strategy
From these examples we can see that the current Government has all along been committed to formulating effective policies and measures to respond to the aspirations of the general public
Many of the issues mentioned above involve interdepartmental communication and coordination . It can thus be seen that the Government of the current term has all along been giving full play to the spirit of team spirit by working together to seek welfare for the public and creating a more competitive environment for Hong Kong to meet future challenges
In respect of the subject of todays debate I would like to point out that the SAR Government of the previous terms had also reorganized the structure to enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of governance
The objective of the restructuring is to tie in with the policy vision and priorities of the then Chief Executive in response to the changes and aspirations of society
For example in the first term of the SAR Government the housing policy was a single area of the Housing Bureau and in the second term the Housing Bureau merged the planning and land policy into Housing and Planning and Land Policy Bureau . In the third term the housing policy was a single area of the Transport and Housing Bureau
For example in response to the concerns of the community about protecting the environment and sustainable development the third term Government set up the Environment Bureau to deal with issues such as air pollution waste management energy efficiency etc
In addition in order to effectively implement the Progressive Development Vision advocated by the third Chief Executive the last Government set up the Development Bureau for Planning Lands Development Works and Cultural Conservation so that the work on cultural cultural and other balanced development could be fully committed at the early stage of planning land use and project implementation
Despite the differences in the size and complexity of the reorganization of the government structure the Legislative Council passed and supported the amendment of legislation and the adjustment of the estimated expenditure as well as the creation of new posts in this respect
The motion moved by Mr LEUNG (Kwa-) chun urges the Government to review the policy areas functions and work objectives of various Policy Bureaux and to make structural reorganization and adjustment and division of responsibilities as necessary so as to achieve better use of public resources enhance governance efficiency and governance capability
In principle according to the examples I have cited just now the SAR Government of the previous terms of Government agreed with this direction and practically implemented the work in this regard
The current Government is no exception . We have put forward a proposal for a more extensive in depth and forward looking structure
Regrettably the Bill was opposed by a small number of Members during the scrutiny of the Legislative Council and the passage of the motion was prevented by filibustering
Finally the Structural Reformation Programme (cannot ) fail to take effect before the end of the fourth term of the Legislative Council
During the term of office of the current Government the Government has worked hard and finally with an unswerving spirit set up the Innovation and Technology Bureau to respond to the aspirations of the relevant sectors in setting up a dedicated bureaux to formulate comprehensive innovation and technology policies and establish an official industry research institute to speed up the development of innovation technology and related industries in Hong Kong
Since 2012 this project has been delayed three times in the first place due to the filibuster of the Finance Committee of the Legislative Council . It was not until 6 November 2015 that the Bill was passed by the Finance Committee of the Legislative Council and formally passed on 20 November of the same year . The process lasted four legislative years
I will now give a key response to Members concerns
Article 2 of the Basic Law clearly provides that the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region shall enjoy the powers of executive administration legislative powers independent judicial power and final adjudication
Article 85 of the Basic Law also clearly provides that the courts of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region will be independent and will not be subject to any intervention
The Judges have all along followed the principle of judicial independence to judge cases in accordance with the law
As clearly explained by the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal in the opening ceremony of the legal year this year the judges must ensure that the trials are fair and just and strictly comply with the legal requirements
When a judge performs his duties he will only act in accordance with the legal provisions and the legal spirit . He will not take into account the legal considerations political economic and social factors
All Judges and Judicial Officers must take an oath when assuming office that they will uphold the Basic Law of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the Peoples Republic of China bear allegiance to the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the Peoples Republic of China adhere to the Chinese law abide by the law and uphold the law and be impartial and uphold the rule of law with the spirit of being fearless impartial selfless and impartial presiding over political affairs and serving the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the Peoples Republic of China
All Judges and Judicial Officers regardless of their nationalities must exercise their judicial power independently and strictly in accordance with the law when performing their judicial duties
As regards the political neutrality of the courts some Members have expressed their views on a few cases in recent days
As repeatedly stressed by the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal the Judges must exercise independent judicial power and handle cases fairly and impartially (with out ) any fear and (with out ) being biased in any way . In terms of perception they should not be reasonably interpreted as biased against any person or cause
These principles are set out in the Guidelines for Judicial Conduct
According to the guidelines the Judiciary has established common law principles and practices to deal with biased matters in its judicial proceedings
The Judiciary also reminds all judges and judicial officers from time to time the importance of the principles I have just mentioned
The SAR Government has stressed that the rule of law and judicial independence must be safeguarded jointly by all Hong Kong people . Even if people have different views on court proceedings they should not exert any pressure on judges or judicial officers or the court in any way or even make any personal attack so as to prevent the court from making a ruling on the ground of legal justifications
I would like to give a brief response to the concerns raised by some Members just now about the position of judges
Article 92 of the Basic Law clearly provides that Judges and Judicial Officers of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region HKSAR shall be elected on the basis of their judicial and professional abilities and may be appointed from other common law jurisdictions
Article 90 of the Basic Law stipulates that The Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal and the Court of First Respite shall be held accountable to the permanent resident of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the Peoples Republic of China in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region who has no right of abode (over seas )
Apart from the Court of Final Appeal and the Court of First Instance of the High Court the Basic Law does not provide for the dereliction of duty on the part of other judges and judicial officers
Article 82 also clearly provides that the Court of Final Appeal may in accordance with necessary invite other judges of common law jurisdictions to stand trial
According to the Hong Kong Court of Final Appeal CFA in hearing and ruling the appeal case the Court of Final Appeal CFA is made up of five Judges including a non permanent Hong Kong Judge or a non permanent common law Judge
The 15 existing Judges in the applicable jurisdictions of the Ordinance are Judges from the United Kingdom Australia and Canada
They have rich judicial experience and a high professional status and reputation which will help the Court of Final Appeal to continue to effectively uphold the rule of law
Regarding the enactment of the National Security Law just now a number of Members from the pan – democratic camp have put forward their views claiming that the enactment of the National Security Law would affect the judicial independence of the Special Administrative Region SAR
I would like to emphasize here that this is completely wrong
In fact the HKSAR is an inalienable part of the Peoples Republic of China . It is the constitutional responsibility of the HKSAR to safeguard national sovereignty safety and development interests
The enactment of legislation on safeguarding national security at the national level is necessary and urgent to fill the gap in national security
The enactment of the Law of the Peoples Republic of China on Safeguarding National Security in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region by the Standing Committee of the National Peoples Congress under the Constitution of the Peoples Republic of China is in line with the principle of one country two systems as stipulated in the Constitution and the Basic Law . It is constitutional lawful and reasonable
The purpose is to ensure that one country two systems will be implemented in a sound and orderly manner and Hong Kong will maintain its long term prosperity stability and long life and better protect the lives and properties of the people . The basic rights and freedoms of Hong Kong will definitely not undermine the judicial independence of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region HKSAR
As I said just now judicial independence is fully respected and protected in HKSAR
The National Security Law which will be enacted by the Standing Committee of the National Peoples Congress NPCSC and included in Annex III to the Basic Law as well as the National Security Law to be announced by the Hong Kong SAR Government will fully support NPCSC in completing the legislative work
In the process we will actively reflect the specific and actual situation of HKSAR
During the debate just now Mr HUI (Chi-) fung and two other Members considered that the appointment of the Secretary for Justice as a member of the Select Committee would undermine the independence of the Select Committee
(how ever ) I would like to stress that the Government fully disagrees with this view
In fact the Secretary for Justice is only one of the nine members of the Recommendation Committee and does not have the veto
As a politically appointed official his position will not prevent the Secretary for Justice from taking the oath he took when he was first appointed as a member of the Legislative Council . This oath is based on the spirit of not being afraid not being biased and not being greedy and not being spiteful . It is an oath that the Secretary for Justice will in accordance with the conditions of the Judicial Officers Recommendation Commission refer to all matters relating to the Judicial Officers Recommendation Commission and directly provide his personal views to the Chief Executive of Hong Kong
The Chief Secretary for Justice is an important role model and a guardian of the rule of law who plays a role to safeguard public interests under the legal system of Hong Kong . She is also the Chief Executive Chief (advisor ) on legal matters . Therefore it is appropriate for her to be a member of the Commission of Inquiry and take part in the work of recommending judicial officers to the Chief Executive
During the debate just now I also noticed that Mr HUI Chih Fung also criticized the Department of Justice for undermining the rule of law
I absolutely (cannot ) accept such a remark . In fact it is totally unrealistic irresponsible and even intentionally harmful
During the debate some Members accused the Mainland of interfering with the judicial independence of Hong Kong . I have to reiterate that if Members can recall the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal made a statement on 15 April this year stating that since she took office as the Chief Justice of the Court of Final Appeal in 2010 she had never encountered or felt that the Mainland authorities had used any form to interfere with the judicial independence of Hong Kong
The full protection of judicial independence under the Basic Law is also an important part of the rule of law in Hong Kong
I understand that Dr Junius HO is concerned about the (man power ) problem of the Judiciary especially the (man power ) problem of the Judicial Officers but he has not responded to this issue at all
Besides before there is a vacancy in Judicial Officers the Judiciary will continue to provide short term judicial (man power ) to maintain the level of judicial (man power ) required by courts at all levels in the feasible area
Besides I fully understand and agree with Dr Priscilla LEUNGs call for the Judiciary to expedite the use of information technology or to conduct remote trials
As a matter of fact the Judiciary has been comprehensively stepping up its efforts in this regard as part of the long term strategy and actively implementing the use of information technology to support and facilitate the implementation of court proceedings on a wider scale
In fact the Government has also proposed to amend the Ordinance in the hope of obtaining approval from the Legislative Council as soon as possible so as to fully and effectively implement electronic information technology and information technology
In fact I have already responded to a written question earlier
The Judiciary has explained that the sentence of imprisonment is a judicial function which should be exercised independently by the court and it is also a function of the court
The courts have to deal with a large number of different cases every day and the specific circumstances of each of them are different
It is indeed a very challenging and difficult task for the court to make fair and appropriate penalties in each case . It also involves the exercise of judicial judgment to seek a balanced and fair decision
(how ever ) in the case of litigation if all parties including the Secretary for Justice consider that the sentence is inconsistent and the penalty is too heavy or too light they may lodge an appeal or apply for a review of the penalty
Deputy President judicial independence has always been the cornerstone of Hong Kongs stability and prosperity and it will continue to be so in the future
Hong Kong people have high expectations for the judicial system to ensure that the rule of law and judicial independence are maintained and that personal rights and freedoms are protected
The SAR Government supports the proposed appointment of the Commission of Inquiry . Given the rich judicial and administrative experience of the capable judges we have full confidence in the Chief Executive and the entire Judiciary in safeguarding the rule of law and the interpretation of the Basic Law
Deputy President I implore Members to agree to this motion
In the fourth Policy Address delivered by the Chief Executive in November last year the theme of the Policy Address is Reaching New Horizons . The Policy Address proposes more than 250 new measures in the short medium and long term to integrate into the traditional advantages of Hong Kong and integrate into the opportunities arising from the (over all ) development of the country through various areas of development . The Policy Address also makes adjustments in the policy (mind set ) to promote many major policies
I will cite a few examples including the introduction of hotel or guesthouse as a pilot scheme for transitional housing the implementation of a cash allowance scheme for the grass roots who have been waiting for PRH for more than three years the introduction of a standard premium premium subsidy and the expedited redevelopment of old industrial buildings to support graduates of local universities in the Greater Bay Area to pursue their career in the urban areas
All in all this Policy Address not only focuses on the challenges and problems that we are facing now but also sets a solid and solid foundation for the long term development of Hong Kong . It can be said that it is a policy (blue print ) with a high end and a long reach and is pragmatic and effective . It also fully reflects the determination and commitment of the current term Government in governance
Just now the two Secretaries of Departments and the eight Directors of Bureaux have given a concise response on their respective policy areas
Here I would like to focus on the implementation of one country two systems the Central Authorities full support for Hong Kong the latest development of the Anti epidemic Fund and the relationship between the executive and the legislature
We are very concerned about the implementation of one country two systems
Last year marked the 30 anniversary of the enactment of the Basic Law
The Policy Address is an independent chapter with detailed explanations of the SAR Governments comprehensive accuracy and accuracy in its commitment to the implementation of one country two systems and safeguarding the Basic Law
After going through the most severe political challenge since the reunification the constitutional order and political structure of the SAR have to be restored to their original state and the problems can be resolved at once
It has been more than 23 years since Hong Kongs reunification with the Motherland . This fully proves that one country two systems is the best system arrangement to maintain the longevity of prosperity and stability in Hong Kong and an important foundation for further development of Hong Kong
In order to ensure the steady implementation of one country two systems Hong Kong society especially young people must have a comprehensive and correct understanding and understanding of one country two systems . They should understand that national security territorial integrity national sovereignty and (over all ) interests (cannot ) be touched by the bottom line and this is something that every Hongkonger should be sure of
After all one country is the prerequisite or the root of two systems and this is very important
Therefore to build a good Hong Kong it is necessary to strengthen the sense of national identity of the people and their understanding of the Constitution the Basic Law the National Security Law and one country two systems
First of all in respect of national security education the National Security Law was implemented in June last year and it has played the role of a tranquilization of the crisis . This has enabled Hong Kong to return to the right track in respect of law and order and the society has obviously returned to stability
The SAR Government will consolidate the achievements in this regard and continue to implement the content and spirit of the National Security Law . Through different means of publicity and education especially the introduction of national security education in schools will help students to properly understand the Hong Kong National Security Law and enhance their sense of national security and law enforcement awareness so as to jointly safeguard national security
The SAR Government attaches great importance to strengthening the sense of national identity and the sense of national identity among Hong Kong people . Young people should have a correct sense of national identity national identity and sense of history
The implementation of national education is the responsibility of schools . Schools should strive to enhance students understanding of national development Chinese culture and nationalities deepen the education on the Constitution the Basic Law and national security cultivate students positive values strengthen their sense of national orientation sense of identification and sense of belonging and lay a foundation for Hong Kongs prosperity and stable development and talent
The SAR Government will continue to step up its efforts to enhance the publics understanding of the Constitution the Basic Law the Hong Kong National Security Law and one country two systems with a multi – pronged effort to step up the promotion and education work of various social strata and different groups
As explained by the Secretary for Labour and Welfare just now the Government has introduced an arrangement for new civil servants requiring all civil servants who joined the SAR Government on 1 July last year or later to take an oath or sign a declaration as part of the employment terms
As for the civil servants employed before 1 July last year the Civil Service Bureau issued a notice on 15 January requesting the staff concerned to take an oath to sign the declaration
Although the declaration contains only one piece of document it actually carries a profound and profound meaning and has a symbolic effect in allowing civil servants to openly affirm that the responsibilities they bear are to genuinely fulfil the civil servants demands of the country and society and to further safeguard and promote the core values that civil servants should uphold . It also ensures that the SAR Government can effectively govern and fully implement the principles of civil service
As for the oath taking arrangements of other public officers we are actively studying this issue and will announce the specific directions in due course
In the session on fully supporting Hong Kong in the Central Government I would like to emphasize that the country is the backbone of Hong Kong and also our opportunity
The development of Hong Kong is inseparable from the country and Hong Kong has always contributed to the country by participating and integrating its development in different roles and forms
The country fully supports the economic development of Hong Kong . We must grasp the new opportunities brought by the development of the Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao Greater Bay Area GBA and the Belt and Road Initiative and actively become the participants of the Mainland Great Circulation and the facilitator of the international circulation so as to open up new room for Hong Kongs economic and social development in the future and inject new impetus
The Policy Address covers the support of the Central Government in seven major policy directions . The scope of the initiatives it provides is far greater than that of the previous Policy Addresses . It is indeed a reminder to the eyes and one of the highlights of the Policy Address
The measures are focused on the Greater Bay Area development which will help consolidate and enhance Hong Kongs status as an international financial centre and an international aviation hub . They aim at developing Hong Kong into an international innovation and technology hub and will improve the land boundary construction of the Hong KongShenzhen International Airport and assist Hong Kong enterprises in exploring the Mainland market
The Central Government strongly supports the development of an international innovation and technology hub in the Greater Bay Area under the Hong KongShenzhen Cooperation Programme and agrees that the Hong Kong and Shenzhen regions under the Shenzhen Innovation and Technology Cooperation Zone and the Hong Kong Zone located at the Lok Ma Chau Loop can be jointly developed by the Hong Kong and Shenzhen Governments so as to realize the idea of one country two systems and one region two parks located on the banks of the river . This will promote the development of an ecological chain of innovation and technology industries between Hong Kong and Shenzhen and create a more vibrant environment for young people in Hong Kong
The Security Bureau and the Shenzhen Municipal Government have also set up a dedicated task force to follow up the cooperation between the Mainland and Hong Kong on the cohesion of the cohabitation of Hong Kong people at the Huanggang Port
The Central Authorities support for Hong Kong in a holistic manner not only helps Hong Kong integrate into the (over all ) development of the country but also offers assistance to Hong Kong in various aspects including the anti epidemic work for the novel coronavirus pneumonia which is well known to all of us
With the strong support and assistance of the Central Government we have greatly enhanced the communitys testing capacity provided additional community treatment facilities and constructed temporary hospitals . The temporary hospitals were formally commissioned yesterday
This fully reflects the Central Authorities care and support for Hong Kong all along and shows that the country is after all the strongest backing for Hong Kong
The Government fully understands that the persistent epidemic situation and the measures to combat and fight the epidemic have brought different levels of impact to peoples daily living employment and business operation . Many people have become unemployed or underemployed and their businesses have become increasingly difficult
In the face of such a serious challenge the Government set up the Anti epidemic Fund AEF in February last year and applied to the Finance Committee of the Legislative Council for funding it in April September and December . On the one hand it aims to enhance Hong Kongs anti epidemic capability and on the other hand it aims to provide appropriate assistance to industries and the public which have been adversely affected by the epidemic or affected by AEF measures
The total commitment approved by the Fund is 1,623 billion
After I became the Chairman of the Bills Committee the Bills Committee approved a total of about 120 measures totalling about 160 billion . The projects cover a wide range of industries and different groups of people in need
As at 18 December last year some 40 projects of the Fund were completed and more than 130 billion had been spent on applications for which over 6.8 million applications were received and nearly 780 000 applications were submitted by enterprises and commercial organizations
We have already distributed around 10 million masks of the Anti Virus Mask Mask Mask mask
The Government will continue to implement the existing fund measures and the Steering Committee will also continue to consider the development of the pandemic and take into account the views of stakeholders from different sectors and the actual operating environment . In accordance with the recommendations made by the relevant Policy Bureaux and departments we will make optimal use of the remaining funds in the fund to provide support and assistance to industries hit hard by the pandemic
Together with a series of measures announced in the Budget and the Policy Address last year the Government has introduced a relief measure totalling more than 310 billion accounting for about 11 % of the Gross Domestic Product
President I would like to talk about the relationship between executive and legislature
The current Government has repeatedly emphasized and taken actions to prove that we attach great importance to the function of the Legislative Council and respect the Legislative Council in exercising its power to exercise checks and balances on the executive authorities . We also hope that the executive authorities and the legislature can work together to reach a consensus on a more controversial issue which is closely related to the long term interests of Hong Kong
A number of policy initiatives of this Government have taken on board Members views including those of the political parties . For example the establishment of a single statutory holiday and public holidays the provision of additional convention and exhibition facilities the promotion of financial development in the Greater Bay Area the relaxation of the threshold for the installation of elevators at the Cheung Tung Tunnel and the subsidy to owners of old buildings for upgrading of old elevators
Yesterday some Members pointed out in their speeches that the Government had taken on board the views of their political parties in the Policy Address . This is one of the reasons
Besides the Chief Executive has also undertaken to resume one question every month for a period of half an hour . Members questions will be answered within 30 days
The next Chief Executives Question and Answer Session will be held on 4 February
On the other hand I meet with the Chairman of the House Committee and the Chairman of the Government every week to have frank exchanges on issues of particular concern to Members
After each meeting I will definitely share with the Chief Executive and Secretaries of Departments and Directors of Bureaux the views aspirations and expectations of Members and political parties on the Governments policy implementation and work so as to enable us to make appropriate follow up or improvement
I am very pleased to note that in the legislative session of 2021 the 13 projects worth 371 million have been approved by the Finance Committee and 30 billion has been allocated for scrutiny by the Public Works (sub committee )
In respect of the Bill six bills have been passed
President we will continue to work closely with the Legislative Council to enhance the Governments enforcement capability in a bid to achieve the principle of making a decision on the basis of its intention and the principle of taking action on the basis of its decision . We will also work together with the development of Hong Kong to bring welfare to the general public
President Hong Kong and the country share the same fate . Hong Kongs development is closely linked to the country
As long as Hong Kong is committed to implementing one country two systems and giving play to the unique advantages of one country two systems and adopting a new strategy in response to the new strategies and strategies of the country and making good preparations and new strategies to strike a new opportunity and better integrate into the (over all ) development of the country Hong Kong will definitely be able to do something
(how ever ) Hong Kong is now hit hard by the economic and livelihood problems due to the epidemic
After a year of fighting against the epidemic we understand very well that the days of the public and enterprises are not easy at all and they have been seriously affected in terms of family life employment education and social life
Our top priority is certainly to do a good job in fighting the epidemic . This is the prerequisite for revitalizing the economy and improving peoples livelihood
The SAR Government will adopt the (mind set ) and mentality of fighting the epidemic to strictly control the spread of the virus and prevent the importation of the virus and the reproduction of the virus . We will strive to achieve the objectives set out in the Policy Address namely to make full use of resources to fight the virus and to prevent the importation of the virus by early detection of the virus and early isolation and early treatment
In addition the Government will make comprehensive plans to expeditiously launch the vaccination programme in order to bring hope and hope to the public
At the same time the Government will expeditiously implement measures to benefit the people and support enterprises as set out in the Policy Address
After the rain of luck Hong Kong can definitely get out of the predicament and start afresh with the efforts and support of the Central Authorities
President I so submit
President I move the motion under my name as printed on the Agenda that the Crimes Amendment Rules 2021 be added to the Agenda
Under section 9A1 of the Ordinance the Amendment Rules must be approved by the Legislative Council
To ensure the fairness of the employment of lawyers by LAD and DoJ in the provision of services DoJ will make reference to LADs fee table for criminal legal aid cases and pay private practice barristers and solicitors who have been appointed as representatives of the prosecution to handle criminal cases
As for the legal representative of the defendant who serves in the Court of Reference and the Court of First Instance through the service of a lawyer on duty he will be liable to pay the legal fees for his service on duty
During the review the Government will mainly consider whether there were any difficulties in making reference to the general price changes during the period and in appointing barristers and solicitors
Therefore we propose to increase the corresponding rates of these three fees by 2.3 %
The Committee had no objection to the increase in the proposal
In the implementation of the upward adjustment of criminal legal aid fees under the Legal Aid Department LAD the Department of Justice will adopt an administrative approach to synchronize and adjust the fees and charges of prosecution fees and the Administration will also adjust the fees and charges of legal aid services through an administrative approach
If the Resolution is passed we will implement the Amendment Rules on the day of gazettal
President my consolidated reply to the question raised by Mr Christopher CHEUNG is as follows 1 and 2 The HKSAR Government fully understands that the persistent novel coronavirus epidemic and the relevant preventive and control measures will have a certain and varying degree of impact on the daily lives of the public and the operation of the enterprises and have resulted in a number of industries being hit hard by the epidemic
In the face of this severe challenge the Government has taken the urgent need of the people and supported enterprises safeguarded jobs and relieved peoples burden (with out ) hesitation in using its fiscal reserves to expeditiously launch the Anti epidemic Fund AEF to enhance Hong Kongs (over all ) ability to fight the epidemic . On the other hand the Government has stepped up its efforts in the fight against the epidemic or in the fight against the epidemic and social distancing measures introduced by the Government
On 21 February this year the Government obtained approval from the Finance Committee of the Legislative Council for setting up a 300 billion commitment to set up a fund . Subsequently on 18 April and 28 September the Government obtained approval from the Finance Committee for injecting 120.5 billion and 5.4 billion into the fund to implement the second and third rounds of measures
The total commitment of the 3SF is 155.9 billion which covers a wide range of industries and different groups of people in need
So far the Steering Committee chaired by the Chief Secretary for Administration has approved 99 projects . Apart from the 83 projects listed in the Finance Committee paper the Steering Committee has approved 16 new projects in accordance with the recommendations of the relevant bureauxdepartments after considering the development of the epidemic situation the views of stakeholders from different sectors and the actual operating environment
The total commitment of the 99 projects is about 155 billion
The detailed figures and the number of beneficiaries of the measures are set out in Annex I
As at 9 October 2020 the Fund has issued a subsidy of over 107 billion for over 4.4 million people and nearly 450 000 applications submitted by enterprises or merchants
As at 9 October 2020 27 projects under the Fund have been completed . As the remaining 72 projects are still in progress it is difficult to accurately estimate the final expenditures or balance of the projects at this stage
The Government will continue to implement the existing fund measures and provide support and assistance in the light of the development of the epidemic and the situation of different trades and industries by making good use of the original funds when necessary
Apart from the fund measures the Financial Secretary has also announced a series of relief measures in the 2021 Budget
The relevant information on these measures is set out in Annex 2
The estimated expenditure on the measures involves about 80 billion which is not a recurrent expenditure
The commitment for each (air craft ) leasing project must be used for specified purposes and the surplus (cannot ) be used for other purposes
The Governments budget for income protection measures involves about 4.2 billion . Most of the measures are in place and the amount directly reflected in the Governments actual income such as the rates concession
The three rounds of fund measures and various relief measures in the Budget involve a total amount of over 300 billion which is equivalent to 10 % to 11 % of the Gross Domestic Product . It is expected that the measures can provide a support effect of slightly more than 5 % for the Gross Domestic Product
In regard to part 3 of the question the SAR Government is actively following up the arrangements for Hong Kong residents currently living in Guangdong Province and Macao to return to Hong Kong (with out ) 14 days of compulsory quarantine on condition that they are in Hong Kong
To ensure the smooth operation of the measures introduced in the initial stage we will consider implementing a quota system to enable Hong Kong residents who have obtained the quota and meet the conditions to return to Hong Kong (with out ) 14 days of compulsory quarantine
The SAR Government will announce the specific arrangements to the public as soon as possible
In order to resume early access for cross border personnel between Guangdong and Macao the SAR Government has all along maintained close liaison with the Guangdong Provincial Government and the Macao SAR Government . Under the framework of joint prevention and control among Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao the three places have jointly discussed the implementation of a proposal to make mutual exemption from mandatory quarantine of entry for the health code interchange among the three places and the mutual recognition of test results
As for the specific arrangement and implementation time it still depends on the development of the epidemic situation and the further negotiation and implementation by the three sides
The SAR Government is closely monitoring the epidemic development and is actively negotiating with the Guangdong and Macao Governments with a view to striving for the early implementation of the relevant arrangements
Deputy President in respect of the full range of learning subsidies mentioned by Mr YIU just now schools are actually flexible in the arrangement of suitable activities including local tours
Certainly if the resources are used to set up Mainland exchange tours they will not be able to come to Hong Kong directly . (how ever ) as we can see the resources of schools including the recurrent allowance are sufficient to organize local tours such as visiting museums libraries libraries or other places in Hong Kong for further studies
We have to say that Mainland exchange is slightly different from local tourism in terms of learning
We hope that through Mainland exchange we can enhance their understanding of the country
In this regard I believe that we will encourage schools to organize more activities in the hope that the epidemic situation can be under control and exchanges with Mainland personnel can be smoother
Deputy President as I have said in the main reply schools usually arrange Mainland exchange activities in two ways
We are responsible for the planning of the route . The Education Bureau will consult the contractors and we will be involved in the planning and content of the route
We may have colleagues attending certain tour groups together so that we can directly see what the exchange tour groups are doing
We will also submit questionnaire questions to students teachers and parents to find out the results of the survey
The deeper question raised by the Member just now is about the attitude
In other words students may choose not to believe many facts
Of course this is what we want to achieve in education or learning today that is when we lay down some facts for students what kind of attitude should we adopt to make them reflect and make changes in their attitude or performance in the future . This is exactly what we want to achieve in education as a whole
(how ever ) I also admit that we are facing a lot of problems today . For example young people nowadays are often affected by some wrong information on the Internet . This has given them an established view which makes it even more difficult for them to open up and accept the whole picture . For example they find it difficult to see the whole picture on the Mainland
This is a joint effort that we have to make . We also hope that all sectors of society will join hands to say the right things and the facts so that more voices can be heard in society
Therefore during this period of time we should all see that we hope to strengthen the training of teachers before joining the job or the current training of universities so that teachers can understand and have a better understanding of the national situation of the country so that students can better understand the country and the national situation of the country
Instead of a compulsory approach the Government should adopt an encouraging approach and schools are also willing to help students
In this reform on the LS subject in the secondary schools we have added one element that is in the curriculum we require students in the secondary schools to visit the Mainland once during the LS lesson for the purpose of conducting a personal examination and exchange programme
Of course this is in line with the development of the curriculum
As for other subjects we often adopt a voluntary approach . For example we have arranged for students to learn history and geography and encouraged them to practice on the Mainland
(how ever ) we have not done so for the time being in other subjects . Let us first take a look at the effect of the subject of Liberal Studies LS when it is included as part of the curriculum
(how ever ) we have already taken this step in the LS subject this time . Let us see how the effect will be determined
Therefore we will try to organize special programmes or exchange groups to cater for the needs of students in order to enhance the benefits of their learning
President the Government has given the following response to some of the amendments in the sixth joint debate
The amendments proposed by some Members propose to reduce the estimated expenditure on various projects under Head 53 ie . the Home Affairs Bureau
The Government opposes these amendments
Young people are the future pillars of society . In order to promote young peoples development the Home Affairs Bureau has all along been committed to promoting diversified and outstanding culture providing young people with diversified activities and facilities and organizing youth development activities through funding different organizations with a view to cultivating talents broadening their horizons and building up confidence and positive values
The International Youth Exchange Programme organized by the Home Affairs Bureau will provide opportunities for young people to broaden their horizons and broaden their international horizons
In view of the rising demand for international exchange among young people the Budget has allocated an additional 100 million to expand the existing International Youth Exchange Scheme to expand the scale of international exchange among young people
At the same time the Home Affairs Bureau has funded community organizations to organize youth exchange activities in the Mainland through the Youth Exchange Scheme for the Mainland with a view to enhancing the understanding and understanding of the Motherland by young people and facilitating exchanges with the Mainland people
We have also launched the Young Mainland Internship Subsidy Scheme to subsidize community groups to organize internship activities in the Mainland to help young people to learn the experience of working in the Mainland and establish connections in the Mainland thereby enhancing their employment competitiveness in the future
The total number of participants in the above three projects was 22 000 in the last year and it is expected that the total number of participants in this year will increase to about 25 000
The three schemes can help young people in Hong Kong to enhance their knowledge and vision thereby helping them to grow personally
If the aforesaid amendment seeks to reduce the operating expenses of Head 53 subhead 000 the Bureau will be affected when it launches activities related to youth development
Besides the Home Affairs Bureau has been working closely with the Commission on Public Education and the Commission on Youth Affairs to promote national education to members of the public particularly young people outside schools
In the year of 1718 the estimated funding for promoting national education was 58.7 million
In order to promote interaction and exchange between Hong Kong young people and young people from Belt and Road countries the Home Affairs Bureau and the Civic Education Commission launched the Belt and Road Exchange Subsidy Scheme in a pilot manner in the second quarter of 2016 to support young people aged between 15 and 29 in Hong Kong to engage in deep exchanges with young people in the Belt and Road regions thereby achieving a common goal
Last year 630 young people from Hong Kong participated in this project and more than 20 countries in Southeast Asia South Asia Central Asia the Middle East and Europe participated
According to our understanding the funding scheme has achieved satisfactory results and media coverage has also been extensively broadcast of the exchanges of individual recipients of funding
On the other hand the Panel on Civic Education organizes a series of activities to promote the Basic Law every year so that members of the public can have a deeper understanding of the Basic Law
Since 2014 the number of participants in the Basic Law Question and Answer Session and the lectures held by the Committee were more than 10 000 which was a very good result with the number of participants in 2017 exceeding 16 000
The Home Affairs Bureau considers that the activities concerned are well received by the public and are worthy of continuing to be organized to promote the Basic Law at the district level
Every year the Home Affairs Bureau and the Civic Party launch the Civic Action Fund and the Civic Action Fund in 18 districts to support community organizations in promoting national education
In addition the Home Affairs Bureau in collaboration with the Commission on Youth has launched the Youth Exchange Subsidy Scheme for the Mainland to provide funding for community organizations to organize youth exchange tours in the Mainland to promote the understanding and understanding of national affairs and exchange with Mainland compatriots in Hong Kong by young people
The aforesaid amendment will reduce the operating expenses of head 53 and subhead 000 thereby preventing the Bureau from implementing the aforesaid activities in 2017 and 2018
Therefore we oppose the amendments proposed
Besides we also oppose Mr CHAN Chitsrs amendment which seeks to reduce the estimated expenditure for organizing celebration activities for the 20 anniversary of the establishment of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region HKSAR by about the same amount as that for the Home Affairs Bureau
The HKSAR Governments theme is Working Together for a Brighter Future Capturing New Opportunities and it has organized a series of activities to celebrate the 20 anniversary of Hong Kongs return to the Motherland
There are four major highlights of the celebration activities
The first key point is to organize a number of rich cultural and sports events as well as to bring the city into a beautiful state of beauty so that people can celebrate the return of Hong Kong and share the joy
Secondly we hope that through the opportunity of the 20 anniversary of Hong Kongs reunification we can achieve universal participation by demonstrating care and integration in society including implementing a series of programmes to encourage and facilitate the participation of the elderly young people and the minorities in the disadvantaged groups in the reunification
The third key point is that celebration activities are closely related to the economic and social development of Hong Kong
We hope that through organizing a number of international meetings and activities we can jointly review the achievements and prospects of Hong Kong over the past two decades with various sectors of the community and explore new market opportunities for Hong Kong
The fourth key point is that we hope to celebrate the 20 anniversary of Hong Kongs return to the Motherland and enhance publicity in the Mainland and (over seas ) by organizing a number of celebration activities in over 80 cities in the Mainland and (over seas ) and promoting Hong Kongs culture arts creativity and development opportunities in these cities
The Labour Department LD has all along been providing diversified employment services to help job seekers find suitable jobs and help employers fill vacancies
In order to promote and maintain the harmony of employers and employees the Labour Department encourages employers to adopt good personnel management measures to enhance their knowledge of labour laws and provide voluntary adjustment services for employers and employees so as to help them resolve differences through dialogue
The Labour Department also inspects workplaces and investigates cases of alleged breach of the Ordinance and prosecutes against unlawful employers and handles compensation claims against employees to protect employees rights and interests
In respect of occupational safety and health we will continue to strive to protect the occupational safety and health of working people through inspection law enforcement education and training publicity and promotion of a triage system
This year a number of fatal industrial accidents have occurred in which the Labour Department has stepped up its enforcement efforts and launched special law enforcement actions . During the actions the Government has paid particular attention to the higher end industries and stopped and stopped unsafe construction
Anyone who is found in breach of the work safety requirements will immediately take enforcement action including issuing a temporary suspension notice when necessary and filing a prosecution case against the person in breach
Besides LD has also stepped up television and radio publicity on occupational safety and health and launched a series of educational activities to remind stakeholders of the importance of construction safety
In respect of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of the provision of
If the annual estimated expenditure for additional funding provided by the recipients of the personal emoluments and remuneration packages of LD is reduced the services provided by LD to the public will be seriously affected and the recipients of the packages will not be entitled to a one off extra payment . For this reason we oppose the relevant amendment
The amendment proposed by Mr CHAN (Chi-) chuen seeks to reduce the estimated expenditure for the Leisure and Cultural Services Department LCSD to organize the Hong Kong 20 Anniversary Celebrations of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region HKSAR . The Government opposes the amendment
In terms of performance and arts the highlight shows included the opening show of the Chinese National Ballet Ensemble Chinese Opera 2017 the performance of the opera group Qunyinghui and the opera troupette orchestra of Pan Lam Ai O
In addition LCSD has organized more diversified sports and recreation programmes and enhanced their scale of activities to encourage wider participation by the public
The major events included the Sixth National Sports Festival the National Sports Day the 2017 Hong Kong Flower Show the World Golf Tournament Challenge and the Play Turn Park Day etc
LCSD will also encourage and support various sports associations to organize more events to enhance the scale and content of existing events to commemorate the 20 anniversary of the establishment of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region HKSAR
If the estimated expenditure for organizing such activities is cut members of the public will lose the opportunity to participate in and watch the relevant programmes
On education some Members have proposed amendments to reduce the estimated expenditure on the Education Bureau Secretaries of Education and their colleagues as well as the Fund for the Primary Three Basic Ability Assessment Study Programme . The Government opposes these amendments
These have become the major education policies we have formulated in recent years such as quality kindergarten education vocational and professional education electronic learning technology STEM education and life planning so as to facilitate the effective and expeditious implementation of these policies
On the other hand we will show the achievements of Hong Kong education to the international education sector and develop and enhance the education ties and cooperation between Hong Kong and other places so as to consolidate Hong Kongs role as a regional education hub . In particular this is an opportunity for students to exchange and practise in order to promote the exchange of talents
My colleagues and I have also been invited from time to time to attend international education forums or seminars held around the world on behalf of the HKSAR Government and exchange views with people and officials from around the world on education policy and explore the future development direction of education policy and the teaching and learning profession as well as the feasibility of bilateral cooperation
Every time I visit I meet and exchange views with local students in Hong Kong so as to show my concern and understanding of their learning and living conditions in Hong Kong and encourage them to return to Hong Kong after completing their studies so as to make contribution to the future of Hong Kong
In addition the rapid development of our country in economic and technology in recent years coupled with the implementation of the Belt and Road Initiative has brought unprecedented new opportunities to Hong Kong and opened up wider pathways and more development opportunities for Hong Kong students
For example the Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao Academic Alliance set up last year is an effective platform for future work and development
The proposals were supported and agreed by schools parents and school organizations
The (over all ) work of the Study Programme has been going smoothly
In the 2016 Pilot Scheme a number of measures were adopted to remove the incentives for drilling including the improvement of design of the subject
The participation of private schools and parents reflects that there is no need to conduct any training for the purpose of participating in the scheme . The (over all ) feedback is very positive
The information on the Basic Competency Assessment Programme launched in 2017 will not be used―I stress―will not be used to assess the performance of schools and there is no need for schools to carry out additional training
The Education Bureau has all along expressed its position that it absolutely does not agree with the provision of additional supplementary training to prepare for TSA or BCA . For this reason it has clearly stated that public schools will not purchase any additional training for preparing for TSA or BCA but will make good use of online resources and assessment information for students learning and improvement
I would like to emphasize here that all the participation in the BCA 2017 Study was conducted in a consensus among the industry from the professional perspective after comprehensive consultation by the school principals teachers and parents
The amendment proposed by Mr Nathan LAW seeks to reduce the estimated expenditure on various items of the University Grants Committee UGC projects . The Government opposes these amendments
The major function of UGC is to provide the Government with impartial expert advice on the funding and development of higher education in Hong Kong and to assure the Government and the community that the operation and teaching activities of UGC subsidized institutions have maintained a good standard and are cost effective
The Education Council is committed to promoting mutual understanding between various institutions the Government and various sectors of society and coordinating higher education matters among institutions and government authorities
On the one hand UGC upholds the academic freedom and autonomy of institutions and on the other ensures that public money is used properly
UGC and its Secretariat must have sufficient resources to cope with the various important tasks they are responsible for
If Members move amendments to reduce the expenditure on meetings and (man power ) of UGC they will seriously impede the normal operation of UGC which will have a significant negative impact on the welfare of the higher education sector and teachers and students
Lastly I would like to talk about the reason why the Secretary for Education did not attend the public hearing this week
I hope Members will understand that many trade associations and activities in Hong Kong have strengthened their life planning and education through their contact with students . Students in Hong Kong have particularly gained internship opportunities and gained a better understanding of the industry
It is very important to have such contact and understanding
Therefore I can assure Members that our time is well spent and it is worth it
I would like to make this clarification here
With these remarks President I implore Members to vote against the amendments concerning the sixth joint debate
President schools are places where people show care support and respect for one another
The Education Bureau has all along adopted a zero tolerance policy against discrimination bullying and violent acts in schools . It absolutely does not accept bullying acts in any form or for any reason . It also reminds schools from time to time to pay attention to the performance and behaviour of their students . No person should commit acts of hatred bullying or even violence for reasons of different positions different political views or the occupation or background of his family members
Schools are also obliged to take active measures to ensure the safety of students in schools and prevent bullying including explaining to teaching staff and students in detail their policy and measures to combat bullying at school and ensuring the practical implementation by teaching teams
My reply to the question raised by Mr KWOK (Wai-) keung is as follows 1 Regarding the cases of suspected bullying of police officers and their children arising from social incidents the Hong Kong Police Force and the Education Bureau have established a notification mechanism . If the Police Force receives the relevant cases it will report them to the Education Bureau for follow up
The Education Bureau has also received some direct cases
From June last year to early October this year there were a total of 25 cases including complaints or cases of dissatisfaction
The schools have also dealt with these cases in accordance with the guidelines of the Education Bureau taking care of the students emotions concerned and providing them with appropriate (counseling ;) . The schools have also informed parents of the incident
After the investigation the school has taken disciplinary actions against the teachers or students involved and the Education Bureau has also followed up the investigation and taken action against the teachers involved
2 and 3 The Education Bureau is now adopting a multi pronged approach to implement policies on prevention and treatment of school bullying on various fronts . It also requests schools to adopt a school participation approach in formulating and implementing strategies against bullying including a clear understanding of the tolerance barrier reporting channels and handling procedures high transparency in monitoring and a proactive and serious attitude in following up bullying incidents
We adopt the principle of prevention is better than cure and recommend that schools should nurture the spirit of mutual respect and care among students at an early age
On preventing school bullying the Education Bureau has been actively promoting the values of education through school curriculum education resources and different student growth programmes and activities with a view to nurturing positive values and attitudes of students to follow their own ways respect each other and share love
In terms of curriculum the curriculum of the subject of Primary and Secondary Education covers positive values and attitudes such as personal growth humanistic relationship and self protection nurturing empathy and respect for others to prevent problems such as bullying and violence and to equip students to face such inappropriate incidents such as bullying with positive attitude and courage and to handle them in an appropriate manner
The Education Bureau also continues to organize diversified student growth activities for schools such as the Sky Programme on Growth in Primary Schools and the Diversified Intellectual Development Programme for Secondary Students to promote the spirit of caring respecting and selflessness
In order to support schools in dealing with bullying problems the Education Bureau continues to organize professional development programmes for teachers to enhance teachers ability to prevent and deal with bullying and irregularities in school premises
In addition we also provide diversified learning and teaching resources and relevant information for schools to enhance teachers understanding of school bullying and to assist schools in formulating strategies to prevent handle and follow up school bullying
Parents play a very important role in the growth and learning of their children
The Education Bureau has encouraged and promoted the cooperation and communication between families and organized different parent education activities to enable parents to understand their childrens needs during their growth process
Schools must monitor the conduct of the inappropriate teaching staff followed up by their teachers . The Education Bureau will follow up seriously and issue advice warning letters or condemnation letters to the teachers concerned according to the severity of the cases and even consider abolishing the registration of the teachers concerned under the Education Ordinance . All in all our existing policy and mechanism will prevent and deal with incidents of school bullying from various perspectives
President the Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority HKEAA was established in 1977 under the Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Ordinance Cap . 261 . It is an independent statutory body whose main functions are to facilitate the local education system and meet the needs of the community in organizing public examination and various professional and international examinations in Hong Kong
The Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority HKAA has all along maintained its daily operation on the basis of its own income and assets under the Ordinance and has not accepted any recurrent funding from the Government
Under section 9 of the Ordinance the resources of the Hong Kong Examinations Authority include the fees charged for public examination or other exams the fees charged by the Hong Kong Examinations Authority for other services and the subsidies provided by the Government for assisting the Hong Kong Examinations Authority in holding public exams
At present the income of HKEAA mainly comes from examination fees . The second source of income comes from property sales and fees for providing individual and group services related to assessment
My reply to the question raised by Mr LEUNG is as follows 1 According to the population projection of the Census and Statistics Department the population expected to live in Hong Kong in the age group of 15 to 19 will continue to fall from 2018 to slightly rise in 2022
Although the projected number is not equivalent to the number of candidates participating in the Hong Kong Diploma of Secondary Education Examination it is expected that the number of candidates in the Diploma of Secondary Education Examination in the future will be adjusted with the changes in the population of the relevant categories
As the projection of the Census and Statistics Department fails to identify whether students with special educational needs and whether they will apply for special examination arrangements when taking the Civil Ceremony Examination will be unable to provide the number of students who are expected to take the special examination in the next five years I believe that the percentage will rise slightly . (how ever ) as the (over all ) number of students will drop it is difficult to estimate the actual number of students who will take the special examination
(how ever ) the Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority will continue to review the special examination arrangements for candidates with special educational needs . Part 2 and part 3 of the question are as follows Under the Ordinance the Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority will submit annual annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized annualized
Over the past five years HKEAAs fiscal position has been generally sound
In view of the fact that the major revenue of HKEAA comes from examination fees and the decline in the number of candidates in the examination in recent years and with reference to the relevant population projection data HKEAA has been actively adopting measures to address the problem including limiting the number of staff members reducing the number of temporary staff members reducing the amount of overtime allowance and reducing the rent
In order to ensure the quality of the assessment and to ensure that candidates can receive a fair assessment HKEAA will continue to take appropriate steps to take forward the necessary projects including the special examination arrangements for candidates with special educational needs . While HKEAA reviews the fees for the Civil Ceremony Examination every year it will also consider a number of factors including the cost of the examination the economic conditions of Hong Kong the inflation rate the affordability of candidates and their parents and the eligibility criteria for the assessment
The Government will also consider these factors when vetting and approving the proposal to adjust the public examination fees
In order to ensure that students will not be denied access to open examination due to financial reasons the Working Family and Student Financial Assistance Agency has put in place a scheme to reduce examination fees for eligible civil service candidates
The Hong Kong Examinations Authority HKEAA is a statutory body organizing open examination in Hong Kong and a strategic partner of the Government in the provision of a quality education system
The Government will continue to keep abreast of the financial situation of the Policy Bureau and the daily operating expenses and examination fees for holding open exams so as to encourage the Policy Bureau to continue to open up more sources of revenue and discuss with the Policy Bureau the feasible financial options in a timely manner so that the Policy Bureau can continue to provide the public with professional assessment services despite the drop in the number of candidates for the Hong Kong Diploma of Secondary Education Examination
President the (man power ) requirement or (man power ) number of the Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority HKAA is of course based on the actual operational needs
In 2014 the Government conducted a consultancy study to consider the adjustment of the employees wages
In the past few years if we look at the whole picture we can see that the (over all ) financial position of Hong Kong has remained stable in the past five years as I have mentioned in my main reply just now
We will continue to have in depth discussions and conduct studies with the Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority HKAA on its future financial position . But at this stage I do not think it is necessary to say how the Government should intervene
President I do not know how carefully Mr IP has read the figures of the Hong Kong Examinations and Assessment Authority
(how ever ) we have a very responsible view on public resources
Therefore in our discussion with HKEAA we must satisfy the Government that the operation cost of HKEAA is reasonable and the loss or surplus is reasonable
Only then can we think about the next step
In any case the maintenance of a fair and efficient examination system is very important to the education system as a whole and it is also the first and foremost requirement for us to consider
President as stated in the main reply the source of income is one of the examination fees the examination fees of other places the services of other places and the subsidies provided by the Government
Therefore if it is necessary to increase the income level these several measures are also possible
As a matter of fact although the annual examination fees may be adjusted the fees have not yet been fully recovered
Most of the candidates will only take part in one civil service examination in their entire studies and the examination fees will not be a heavy burden on their families
In the case of students in need of financial support the Working Family and Student Financial Assistance Agency has put in place a scheme to reduce examination fees to ensure that families or students in financial need will not be unable to take the examination due to a lack of resources
I am not saying that we will never consider it but it takes a long time to conduct a policy study and consider the outcome and implications
We have been working on this issue for many years but we have not seen any direct impact on the quality of education . Therefore I think we can continue to explore this issue
When we discuss with him we will consider the proposal as one of the options
In recent years the level has dropped gradually
In view of various places in the world the biggest source probably comes from the burning of garbage . Garbage is burned in different places around the world . The second source may come from the burning of trees and fires
President as far as I understand it adoption is in progress in November and it will take a few weeks for the adoption results to be released
President since the outbreak of the epidemic it is difficult for us to do accurate statistics on the number of masks that Hong Kong people use
(how ever ) with the total population of Hong Kong standing at about 7.5 million and the working population of about 4 million as the estimate the number of disposable masks worn out every day during the epidemic can be as many as 2 million
If the daily consumption of 4 million to 6 million masks in Hong Kong is estimated to be about 2 to 3 grams each mask the daily amount of masks disposed of in GBA will be about 10 to 15 tonnes
According to the report on monitoring of solid waste in 2018 the daily disposal of municipal solid waste in landfills is 11 400 tonnes . It can thus be estimated that the disposal of municipal solid waste in landfills by disposable masks during the epidemic is about one thousandth of that in total
The main materials of the face masks are nonwovenly fabrics and filaments . The rubber bands are also made from plastic materials . Waste plastic is the third largest component of municipal solid waste in Hong Kong . According to the above mentioned report in 2018 the amount of waste plastic disposed in landfills per day was about 2 300 tonnes
Therefore the amount of gas emitted from waste plastic during the epidemic is about 5 % of the total amount of gas emitted from gas masks
As the gas masks including N95 masks and surgical masks are made of complex materials such as different materials and metal that are difficult to disassemble they are not recyclable and should not be mixed in the recycling bins to avoid contaminating other recyclables
In addition the Government announced on 5 May that it would distribute reusable masks to all Hong Kong people . This will help reduce the usage of reusable masks
At present waste masks produced in hospitals and quarantine centres for handling suspected and confirmed cases are regarded as medical waste which are sent to chemical waste disposal centres where the temperature is around 1 000°C for burning and disposal
All these tobacco products will be handled by advanced air pollution control facilities to ensure that they meet the stringent emission standards to protect the environment
As for the masks commonly used by the public they will mainly be disposed of at home through waste collection vehicles directly or through waste transfer stations to the landfills for disposal
At the transfer station the goods will be sent to the special sealed containers through an automated transmission system and then sent to the landfill for disposal by sea or land
The disposal of waste at the disposal stations is mainly done by machines and staff at the stations are not required to use manual contact or handling of the waste
Transfer station operators will regularly and regularly clean the waste leaking to the lobby to avoid the waste transportation system and the containers and goods vehicles carrying the waste to maintain environmental hygiene . The sewage generated will be properly handled before being discharged into the public sewage channels and then sent to the Governments sewage treatment plant for further treatment
The waste charging areas are designed and constructed in a holistic manner . They are equipped with a multiple layer integrated infill system to cover the entire site and the infill will be narrowed and properly handled
Upon the completion of waste collection on a daily basis the contractor will cover a layer of about 150 (%hesitation ) of soil on the surface of the glacial surface of the wastage area and then spray a kind of antifungal dust to the covered layer to ensure environmental hygiene and prevent the emission of odour
Moreover the steam generated in the landfills will also be recycled through a preset gas collection system for better use and waste reduction
In the case of oxidation desalination and high humidity in landfills the decomposition process of domestic waste will generate heat and increase the temperature in landfills which will kill the source of diseases including bacteria and viruses
Larvae are often used as indicators of microorganisms in environmental diseases . The lower their content the fewer chances of microorganisms in the environment
We have conducted a test on the lymphoma of the Tseung Kwan O settlement . The test result shows that there is no lymphoma at all in the settlement of Tseung Kwan O . Therefore the use of face masks in Tseung Kwan O will not cause the disease to develop
During the pandemic the relevant departments have been stepping up their efforts to clean up discarded face masks and waste . They have also reminded their staff to remain vigilant dispose of waste properly and maintain personal hygiene
As quite a number of people have visited the countrys outskirts such as country parks recently the relevant departments including the Environmental Protection Department and the Agriculture Fisheries and Conservation Department have stepped up inspections and cleaning of the venues . At the same time through various channels and publicity efforts the Government has studied the number of places in country parks with more people and posted posters to urge country people to maintain environmental hygiene and protect their ecology
During the patrol of country roads AFCD staff will also call on hikers to take away their own rubbish . If they find people throwing rubbish they will take appropriate enforcement actions
In order to remind the public to fight the epidemic together the Government has through different channels strengthened the advice to the public to maintain personal hygiene and maintain appropriate social distancing over the past few months . It has also reminded the public to remember to properly use personal protective equipment such as masks and to dispose of them appropriately after use in a garbage bin with a lid . It should definitely not be thrown around carelessly to avoid causing health risks and affecting the ecological environment
President I thank Mr CHUNG for his supplementary question
In this regard the Government will do its best
Therefore I think the Government should adopt an appropriate approach and encourage other places to do the same so as to reduce the health risks to (front line ) staff
I think on the one hand we all agree that we should pay attention to the situation of (front line ) colleagues or (front line ) staff handling some disposable objects including masks being mixed up . As I have just said all the existing buildings of the Government be they open to the outside or places for employees to go to work we have internal guidelines and we have also looked into them
I think it can be divided into two parts . First as mentioned by Mr CHUNG just now we may need to diversify the use of waste waste and waste by encouraging the use of reusable masks so that there will be less waste at source
At the same time different people have different needs so these disposable masks must be used . We will try our best to install new masks in public spaces where the Government has set its own standards so that people can dispose of them conveniently and appropriately
Recently the Government has introduced a reusable mask which can be used by all Hong Kong people for free
I think this is the direction that the Government the business sector and the public have all seen
I agree with the Honourable Member that we should step up publicity
Some employees may have to abandon their masks at work after they have returned to the company . They may have to wear masks on the street before they return to the company . They may not throw away their masks on the street
I believe this can cope with some of the circumstances
President I understand what Members have said but as I said just now the analysis shows that of course different situations can happen
Certainly if we look at the original intent of Mr CHUNG we can see that the Government should encourage the use of reusable masks by reducing waste at source . This is a major direction supported by Members and can address many problems including waste disposal and hygiene problems
President having consulted the Development Bureau my consolidated reply to Mr Kenneth LAUs question is as follows 1 In order to improve the air quality of Hong Kong including Tuen Mun the SAR Government has all along been committed to reducing the emission of local pollutants and enhancing the air quality of the region with the Guangdong Provincial Government
Will Honourable colleagues from the Development Bureau please supplement
Certainly this issue can be considered from different perspectives be it from the perspective of individual development cases or from a more general perspective
This is a very interesting question because in Hong Kong quite a number of residents live in public housing units . Therefore it is important for (%hesitation ) and the residents concerned to work together to reduce waste and recycle waste
Specifically as I said just now we have the Central Waste Disposal Recovery Pilot Scheme in three districts including Kwun Tong and Sha Tin in the Eastern District
President with the popularization of plastic materials how to deal with waste plastic to minimize the impact on the environment and ecology is a topic that the whole world must join hands to address
The Government has all along adopted a multidisciplinary approach to address the problem of waste plastic . On the one hand it has reduced waste from source through various waste reduction measures and on the other it has built up a circulating economy by upgrading the recycling support and converting waste into resources
In addition we have also stepped up publicity and education to gradually change peoples behaviour
My reply to the question raised by Mr Vincent CHENG is as follows 1 The Environmental Protection Department EPD conducts a annual statistical survey to compile a report on the monitoring of solid waste in Hong Kong
The statistics include the average air quality per month on the total amount of plastic wastage but they do not specify the statistical figures for plastic waste gas waste disposal
At present we do not have the relevant statistics for 2020 and the statistics for 2019 are still being compiled so they (cannot ) be provided for the time being
Secondly we understand that during the epidemic due to the need to prevent people from going out more people will choose to order takeaway and use disposable tableware in one go
Since March this year the Environmental Protection Department and the Committee on Environmental Movement have conducted eight months of publicity activities to appeal to members of the public through the use of bicycles and coaches bus stops MTR stations outdoor advertisements news reports and social media platforms and mobile applications . While fighting the epidemic the Government should minimize the use of disposable plastic tableware such as disposable plastic tableware that are not necessary to be used for takeaway orders
We have commenced the promotion exercise on Waste Recycling 2.0 in the middle of 2020 . At this stage we will mainly introduce various types of recyclables promote saving a bit and highlight three colours . We will also promote the enhanced community recycling network including nine green zones 22 community recycling centres and more than 100 floating recycling stations at regular intervals to receive various types of recyclables
In the second phase the publicity campaign will focus on enhancing the publics knowledge of the culture of gambling
In addition the Government also commenced a study in the scope and mechanism for the feasibility of controlling or banning the use of wet wipes in April 2019
Depending on the analysis results of the study the consultant will propose to the Government a proposal that is applicable to Hong Kong in the long run
The study is expected to be completed by the end of this year
At the same time we will continue to promote education and other different channels to encourage different stakeholders to implement the packaging of the drug
3 In the past the focus of local development was on the recycling of waste in a more efficient manner and the collection mode of the community was also more flexible
In order to tie in with public education change peoples behaviour and enhance the recycling rate of plastic waste the Government has launched the Free Central Plastic Package Pilot Scheme this year to collect plastic waste of all types of non
The waste plastic collected is of a wide variety including plastic bags plastic containers plastic utensils plastic cups fruit jars jars plates and other packaged plastic materials
In January this year the Government conducted pilot tests in the Eastern District and in Sha Tin and Kwun Tong in July and started the two years long Pilot Programme on Recycling of Plastic Waste
As at September this year there are already 166 public and private residential care homes 53 single buildings 8 villages 125 schools or public organizations etc . in the three pilot areas . A total of over 350 premises have been registered
At present the scope of service covers an area of about 42 % to 64 % of the population in the three districts and the total recycling capacity is about 200 tonnes which is on par with the target set by the Scheme
The contractor will continue to contact and invite more housing estates to participate in the scheme with buildings to increase the recycling capacity
We will take into account the experience and effectiveness of the pilot scheme and consider gradually expanding the service to cover all districts in Hong Kong
The reduction of liquor gas is a global trend . The Government will continue to adopt a multi – pronged approach to promote the liquor culture at different levels
One of the strategies of the Government is to promote waste reduction by introducing the waste charging scheme
The purpose of the legislative amendment is to increase the amount of levy to encourage members of the public and enterprises to actively implement the legislative amendments and then reduce the levy
At the same time the Government will make every effort to promote reuse and recovery support the circular economy and promote green recovery in Hong Kong
We welcome the establishment of a new Bills Committee on the Legislative Council Inland Revenue Amendment Bill 2019 to study the Bill . The Government will actively support Members in the scrutiny of the Bill and hope that the Bill will be passed as soon as possible
President we understand Dr CHENGs question and will actively follow up with the relevant Members and districts including introducing a new measure
For example recently a group of colleagues from the Green Development Programme have set up a Green Outreach Group to identify ways to reduce waste promote clean recycling and balance the environmental hazards in various districts with local residents including their trade committees local community councils and relevant organizations
Despite the fact that each district has its own situation we will actively support different communities in reducing waste and recycling
Let me make a simple point . As mentioned by the Member just now during the epidemic the (over all ) waste reduction or waste disposal volume in Hong Kong was simply based on some preliminary data
In the first half of this year Hong Kongs (over all ) waste has decreased by 5 % as compared with the first half of last year
Of course this is related to economic activities such as the (cancelation ;) of leather factories
I hope to urge Members not to forget environmental protection during the fight against the epidemic and step up waste reduction and recycling together
President I thank Mr CHUNG for his concern about waste reduction and recycling
As I said in part 3 of the main reply the Government will expand the types of recycled materials such as clean recycling materials and recycled plastic materials in the GZ Area and community recycling centres
Besides from early this year to the middle of this year the Central Government has adopted the practice of resuming land in the three districts namely the East District Kwun Tong and Sha Tin and we will also step up our efforts in promoting the promotion of these districts
If you go to the village you will see that the recycling bins there are even wider and contain more recycling materials including plastic bottles plastic bags different types of plastic packaging plastic containers and packaging . We can see the effectiveness here
I also hope that Members will continue to support more clean recycling
(how ever ) Members should understand that the recovery of materials requires attention from two sides
On the one hand it is necessary to keep the waste clean and hygienic as this will affect the storage of waste as well as the recycling of waste downstream
In general these complementary items in the international community are not suitable for discussion
First what is suitable for recycling? . It should be clean and should not affect environmental hygiene and even the environmental hygiene of workers
President the Government has the following views on part of the amendments in the fourth joint debate
As mentioned by Members the main duty of the Assistant Director of Environmental Assessment is to supervise the work of the Environmental Assessment Division including assessing the environmental impacts of various policies and schemes handling environmental procedures and applications under the Environmental Impact Assessment Ordinance and formulating environmental impact assessment and noise management policies
If head 44 is cut the estimated expenditure for paying the salaries of the two posts mentioned above will render the Environmental Protection Department EPD unable to continue with the relevant work which will seriously affect its performance in its duties of protecting the environment in Hong Kong
Therefore we do not agree with the proposal on the reduction
As regards the amendment proposed by Mr CHAN (Chit-) chuen to reduce the estimated annual operating expenditure of the Hong Kong and Zhuhai and Macao Bridge Hong Kong Works Management Office HKMWC under Head 60―Roadwork Department Head 60 the Government opposes this amendment
The functions and duties of the Hong Kong Works Management Department WRA are essential and important
The reduction of the estimated annual operating expenditure of WDO will seriously affect the normal operation of departments and the implementation of the aforesaid works projects . The community will have to pay a heavy price for the early completion of works projects
Housing is an important livelihood issue affecting all strata of society . The Government has been striving to implement various policies and measures to solve the housing problem from the root
After public consultation the Government formulated the Long Term Housing Strategy LTHS in 2014 to affirm the principle of supply leadership by increasing the supply of public and private housing to 60 and 40 and rebuilding the housing ladder to fundamentally meet the needs of the various strata of society in housing production in the long run
In respect of public housing the Government will continue to increase the supply and introduce various subsidy schemes to promote the circulation of public housing
In respect of private housing the Government has introduced a number of demand management measures to suppress the excitement of the property market and combat the short selling of properties . The measures aim at limiting the demand for foreign and local investments and giving priority to the needs of local home ownership
The Environment Bureau is mainly responsible for formulating policies and plans for energy supply strength and air quality safety and energy efficiency and energy saving for Hong Kong so as to promote marine development in Hong Kong and improve peoples living environment
The reduction of the operational expenses of Head 137 and the estimated expenditure on emoluments will seriously affect the daily operation of the Environment Bureau . For this reason we do not agree to the proposed reduction proposal
In respect of the amendments to Head 138―Planning and Landscaping Amendment No . 111 to 115 proposed by Mr Andrew WAN Mr CHAN (Chi-) chuen and Dr LAU (Siu-) lai respectively they seek to cut the estimated expenditure for the emoluments of the Secretary for Development the Under Secretary for Development and the Political Assistant of the Development Bureau
Such amendments will cause the Office of the Secretary for Development to lose the necessary resources to take forward the various key tasks undertaken by the Bureau in the year of its establishment ie . the year of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region
In the year of 2017 and 2018 BD will continue with the relevant work including 1 taking enforcement actions to clear unauthorized buildings 2 inspecting subdivided units in target buildings and rectifying unauthorized building works works . 3 clearing unauthorized buildings in the New Territories Control Area which are in a critical condition and pose a higher potential risk to building safety and 4 reviewing various relevant procedures
If Mr CHAN Cheuksen passes a motion on reducing the annual estimated expenditure for the personal emoluments of BD which may be passed the work of BD will be affected thereby affecting the quality of building safety and building development projects
If the amendment to deduct the annual estimated expenditure for the personal emoluments of LandsD is passed LandsD will find it difficult to provide various services such as land administration and land assessment to facilitate land and housing development . It will also find it difficult to handle the unsatisfactory claim of land noncompliance of management and land resumption which will seriously affect the development of the community
If Mr Nathan LAWs amendment to cut the estimated annual expenditure for Hong Kong 2030 towards development assessment is passed not only will the study on the approval process be affected but also delay the completion of Hong Kong 2030 as scheduled for 2018
Insofar as the Civil Service of the Development Bureau is concerned if some of the proposals of the Civil Service and its departments to amend the tax concessions proposed by Members are passed their operation will be seriously affected and their services provided to the public will also be directly affected resulting in the quality of living of the people and the safety of the people being affected
Therefore I call on Members to vote against the relevant amendments . The Government has given the following reply on the proposal for amending the funding arrangements for the Civil Engineering and Development Department CEDD and the Civil Engineering and Development Department CEDD
The work of CEDD is related to the provision of services to local peoples livelihood and beneficial to economic development . If the proposed amendments to the cuts are passed this time around the operation of CEDD will be seriously affected directly affecting the services provided by CEDD to the public and thus the project concerned (cannot ) be commenced or completed as scheduled
Therefore I implore Members to vote against the relevant amendments
The Government proposes to reduce part of the operational expenses and estimated emoluments of the Transport and Housing Bureau under head 158 in respect of the amendments proposed by Dr KWOK Ka kis and Mr CHAN Chuen
The reduction of the estimated expenditure of about the same amount as the estimated expenditure for the three months salary of the Secretary for Transport and Housing under subhead 000 will cause the Secretary for Transport and Housing to fail to lead the Bureau and its departments to carry out the relevant services and development work in respect of transport and housing during the aforesaid period . This will seriously affect the normal operation of the Bureau and its departments and the implementation of various plans for transport and housing development
On the other hand the Three Runway Programme is extremely important for maintaining Hong Kongs status as an international and regional aviation hub at the Hong Kong International Airport and safeguarding Hong Kongs long term and sustainable development
Last term the Legislative Council established the (sub committee ) to Follow Up to 10 Meetings on Issues Relating to the International Airport 3 Railway System including two public hearings
The Legislative Council has also set up the (sub committee ) to Follow Up the Hong Kong International Airport 3RD System in the current term
Since its commissioning in November 2016 the Small Waste Committee has held a total of four meetings to discuss the latest progress of the Three Runway Programme the construction cost and financial arrangements (man power ) planning and development as well as the progress of major environmental impact assessment measures etc
The Steering Committee will continue to fully support the work of the (sub committee ) in assisting the Legislative Council in effectively monitoring the implementation of the Three Way Project
If the funding for the relevant projects is cut the Governments monitoring role and functions in the implementation of the three runways project will inevitably be significantly undermined
Therefore we oppose the relevant amendments and earnestly call on Members to support the funding application so that the Government can monitor the work of implementing the Three Way Project
Proposed amendment to reduce the estimated expenditure on the annual emoluments of the Transport Department TDC for the whole year as proposed in head 186 . I move that the amendments be passed
The funding will be used to pay the salaries of nearly 1 705 staff members of TD . If the amendment is passed TD will not be able to pay the salaries of all staff members according to the contract and all the offices and various transport facilities of TD will not be able to ensure safe and normal operation . This will seriously affect the services provided by TD to the public and the ancillary facilities for public transport
In addition the Government may also be held legally liable for being unable to pay the salaries
The Government also opposes Mr LEUNG (Kwok-) hungs amendment proposing to reduce the estimated expenditure for the purchase of bus services under the Rehabilitation and Consolidation Scheme
Rehabilitation buses provide point to point transport services to persons with disabilities who find it inconvenient to use general public transport and fixed route services are provided by these buses to provide fixed bus stops for people with disabilities who need to go to work go to school and receive rehabilitation treatment
Early access to eHRSS services is the way to meet the transport needs of people who have made appointments to EHRSS to cope with the needs of inspection receiving treatment and participating in community activities
Over the past five years the Labour and Welfare Bureau has allocated an additional 33 buses and 156 vehicles to the bus rehabilitation service operators
The number of service passengers for bus services has increased from 75 9900 in 2012 to 89 9700 in 2016
The Labour and Welfare Bureau and the Transport Department have all along been closely monitoring the demand for bus service changes and allocating additional resources as and when necessary to strengthen and provide new bus service for rehabilitation
The funding for the 2017 – 2018 budget allows the bus operator of Fukuoka Bus to add eight new vehicles and replace the vehicles with nine vehicles with a higher number of vehicles thereby further increasing the number of vehicles to 164
The newly added bus service can provide services for 30 applicants in the Fixed Route Service Waiting List at the end of December 2016 and increase the number of phone reservation services by 25 400 per year
The reduction of the funding for the Labour and Welfare Bureau to purchase bus services will bring inconvenience to people with disabilities who find it inconvenient to use general public transport
With these remarks President I implore Members to vote against the amendments concerning the fourth joint debate
First climate change . Fighting climate change is an important issue facing the whole world
Recently the natural disasters such as heavy rains floods and floods and extreme weather in various parts of the world have reminded in a high profile the concern of more people about climate change and the Governments response has been incessant
The SAR Government attaches great importance to climate change . In 2017 we announced the Hong Kong Climate Action Plan 2030 which has described in detail the major measures to reduce adaptation and change and set out the target of reducing carbon intensity by 65 % to 70 % by 2005
Through the significant reduction of low carbon recycling activities such as fuel recovery and power generation Hong Kongs carbon emissions have reached their peak in 2014 and the carbon intensity in 2019 has dropped by about 35 % from the (base line ) in 2005
Just now Mr CHAN mentioned the progress of carbon reduction in Hong Kong . Looking back at the latest figures we can see that the target of Hong Kong Island has been achieved
In 2020 the target of reducing carbon emissions was reduced from 6.2 tonnes per capita to 4.5 tonnes per capita in the latest estimate in 2020 . Therefore the target and progress are as we have pointed out
With the implementation of various relief measures we are now on the way to achieving the carbon reduction target by 2030
At the annual meeting of the United Nations in September last year President XI Jinping announced that China would achieve carbon neutrality by 2030 and achieve carbon neutrality by 2060
This is an important undertaking made by the country in the face of climate change
In Hong Kong the Chief Executive announced in the 2020 Policy Address that the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region HKSAR would strive to achieve carbon neutrality by 2050 and step up the promotion of Hong Kongs responsibility in reducing carbon emissions
A brief summary of the measures The local greenhouse gases in Hong Kong mainly originate from several major sources namely power generation transportation and waste materials
In 2019 electricity generation accounted for about 66 % of the (over all ) emissions transport accounted for about 18 % and waste materials accounted for about 7 %
In order to reduce carbon emission the SAR Government has adopted various measures in these three areas
On power generation the Government continues to urge the two power companies to use natural gas instead of fuel to generate electricity
In 2020 the ratio of fuel to gas has been reduced to less than one fourth and natural gas has been increased to about half
In 2018 the Government and the two power companies launched the renewable energy online power grid to support community projects on renewable energy in the community . In addition electricity charging institutes were launched to install solar power generation systems for schools and welfare organizations
Together with other facilitation measures more than 180 million kWh of renewable energy can be generated each year
Buildings account for about 90 % of the total electricity consumption in Hong Kong . The energy consumption of buildings can be saved and this is conducive to coping with climate change
The Hong Kong City Energy Saving Plan 2015 to 2025 announced in 2015 sets out the target of reducing energy intensity by 40 % by 2025
This is our most aggressive target when compared with other AFC organizations
At present Hong Kongs energy intensity has decreased by more than 30 %
We understand that Mr CHAN has proposed that the Government should first formulate and announce the proposal on the target time frame and (road map ) for carbon reduction
The 2019 Policy Address sets the target of green energy for the entire Government . The target is to increase the energy efficiency of government buildings and infrastructure facilities by 6 % in 2014 to lead in energy saving and supporting renewable energy in 2025
Besides the Environment Bureau and the Development Bureau have approved the issuance of an internal notice on green government buildings which will keep abreast of the times in respect of the requirements on environmental protection for all government buildings
In respect of green transport in order to reduce carbon emissions from the transport sector we have launched the Hong Kong EVs Popularization Route this year to lead Hong Kong to achieve zero carbon emission of vehicles by 2050 . The specific measures include the suspension of new registration of diesel private cars in 2035 or earlier including hybrid vehicles
The SAR Government has taken the lead in demonstrating the need to allocate more resources to promote the electricization of government vehicles
In June this year we launched the Hong Kong Clean Air Strategy 2035 which sets out the long term goals and specific policies for continuous improvement of air quality from now to 2035 and combines the strategy of reducing carbon in various aspects to continuously enhance air quality and tie in with Hong Kongs carbon neutrality
On waste management the SAR Government announced the Hong Kong Resource Circulation (blue print ) for 2035 in February this year . Taking the vision of reducing waste through resource circulation and zero waste dumping as a whole it proposes a strategy to cope with the challenge of waste disposal management up to 2035
As for public education I agree with the two Members who said just now that it is most important for the general public to actively participate in the promotion of public education . Through various channels including the production of television and radio programmes the establishment of a (web site) dedicated to climate change and the holding of school competitions we hope to enhance public awareness of climate change and promote the way of life of people living on low carbon
President I would like to thank once again Mr CHAN Chun – ying for moving this motion and Dr Elizabeth QUAT for proposing the amendment
I am very glad to hear that more than 10 Members including Mr Martin LIAO Mr Jeffrey LAM Mr Tony TSE Mr LAU (Kwok-) fan Mr LAU (Wai-) kwok Mr Christopher CHEUNG Dr CHIANG (Lai-) wan Dr CHIANG Lian Mr CHENG (Han-) pan Mr CHAN (Hak-) kan Mr CHAN (Kin-) por Mr Holden CHOW and Mr Frankie YICK support the SAR Government to step up its efforts in tackling climate change and have expressed their views on various fronts
Just now the Under Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury gave a response on the relevant areas
Here I would like to give a general response to the discussion just now
Over the years the Government has been procrastinating in its carbon reduction efforts step by step
In fact we all know that the target set by the Hong Kong Government for carbon neutrality by 2050 is not only different from that of many major economies in Asia but even more advanced
As I said in my opening speech Hong Kong is moving steadily towards the 2030 target
(how ever ) if carbon reduction is to be further deepened in less than 30 years to achieve the target of carbon neutrality many Members will agree that this is a challenging task and there will be opportunities such as green finance
The Government attaches great importance to this and the Chief Executive has coordinated various departments and various sectors in the newly established Steering Committee on Counter (-"Change) and Carbon (-") to strengthen the solidarity and cooperation among all sectors of the community in promoting the low carbon transition in Hong Kong
Members of the public also agree that the Government should be exempted from the liability of various sectors and even the general public regardless of the Governments work
We agree with Mr CHANs proposal to step up public education and publicity to encourage the public to implement a green and low carbon lifestyle
Apart from the use of social media to disseminate relevant information to different social groups which I mentioned in my opening remarks the Government has also promoted the idea of low carbon living through different channels
For example in 2015 the Electrical and Mechanical Services Department and the Electrical and Mechanical Services Department launched a movement to promote energy saving and energy efficiency enhancement to the enterprises and the general public with a view to preparing Hong Kong for the target of reducing the (over all ) energy intensity by 40 % by 2025
In 2017 the Environment Bureau launched a number of publicity and education projects to enhance public understanding of the impacts of climate change and promote a low carbon and green life . These projects include the promotion of the Big Waste Project which has aroused public concern and discussion about climate change on various social media . The free television programme The Real Life with Zero Carbon has also been broadcast . The radio programme on Low Carbon Living has also been broadcast on the Internet
The Environment Bureau and the Observatory have also launched a dedicated (web site) on climate change to provide the public with knowledge on climate change
Moreover in 2018 the Environment Bureau launched the Low Carbon Living Computer for the general public to assess their personal carbon emissions generated in food clothing housing and transport through simplified information so as to assist them in implementing low carbon green new development
In April this year the Environment Bureau the Education Bureau and the Development Committee on Sustainable Development jointly launched a new online learning platform on carbon reduction for the long term to provide schools with teaching materials on carbon reduction and sustainable development to organize live online quiz competitions and small knowledge challenge on carbon reduction with a view to enhancing interaction among the public
Through the Outreach Programme and the Scholar Reward Scheme over 150 primary and secondary schools have organized seminars work groups performance dramas site visits community exhibitions and competitions in the past two academic years to enable students and members of the public to learn and practice the philosophy of Low Carbon Living and Sustainable Development . It is estimated that about 130 000 teachers and community members will participate in these two projects
In addition the Sustainable Development Commission has recently entered the community to organize seminars for residents and property management personnel in more than 50 housing estates over the years to promote a low carbon environment for environmental protection
In order to disseminate the message of climate change more widely the Environment and Nature Conservation Fund has earmarked 25 million to subsidize NGOs to launch environmental education and community participation projects under the theme of climate change . The sustainable development fund to be launched soon will also subsidize projects that can enhance peoples understanding and implement the concept of sustainable development to enhance the promotion of low carbon living in the public
We will continue to step up publicity and education on climate change
As for the various government departments the first step is to draw up and announce their targets and timetables and (road map ) for meeting the lead in climate change . In respect of energy saving targets in order to achieve the lead the Government has been working hard to draw up targets and timetables and (road map )
On promoting energy saving and carbon reduction the Government announced in 2015 that it would reduce the energy saving target of 5 % of the electricity consumption of government buildings from 2015 to 2020 and the target was finally achieved one year earlier
Subsequently we set out a new target on green energy so as to further increase the energy efficiency of various government departments by 6 % within the five years since 2019
The target of green energy not only covers government buildings and infrastructure facilities but also covers the content of green energy . In other words the target does not only seek to save electricity and other energy but also take into account the contribution of zero carbon energy to renewable energy projects
We will conduct a review of the energy and carbon emissions of the main building and government buildings through the cooperation between EMSD and the Environmental Protection Department and further save energy on the operation of the existing buildings by conducting a review of the buildings and checking the performance and demand of the existing buildings
In addition the Environment Bureau and the Development Bureau have also worked in collaboration with each other to launch an internal report on green government buildings . The report will keep abreast of the demand for government buildings under the Environmental Standards and Guidelines including the demand for buildings with an area of 5 000 sq m or more with a central cooling system and 10 000 sq m or more for new government buildings to at least reach the Grade One Green Building EOG
Mr KWOK has proposed amendments to increase the ratio of government vehicles to EVs
The Government has all along been paying attention to the latest development of EVs technology and encouraged various government departments to use EVs to replace their expired vehicles in response to operational needs
As at August 2021 the Government had 1 130 vehicles in its fleet of which 120 were electric vehicles equivalent to one electric vehicle per nine government vehicles
Compared to the (over all ) 3.3 % of the total number of EVs in Hong Kong the number of EVs in the Government is more than three times that of the total number of vehicles in Hong Kong which is an indicator of the effectiveness of supporting the popularization of EVs
To demonstrate the Governments determination to promote the use of EVs in Hong Kong more vigorously we will expedite the pace of using EVs by government vehicles . The latest policy this year is that apart from vehicles that (cannot ) use EVs for individual reasons such as operational needs all small and medium government private cars which are newly purchased and up for replacement must use EVs
On environmental procurement Members have proposed proposals on green procurement amendments
In order to promote environmental protection the Environment Protection Department has now formulated a set of specifications and guidelines for the Policy Bureau and government departments to draw reference from when conducting environmental protection procurement
At present the Government has covered 183 types of products and services including public works projects promotion of the use of recycled building materials
We will also organize experience sharing sessions to provide relevant practical information on the (web site) and organize seminars to encourage various trades and industries apart from the Government to set environmental procurement guidelines
We will continue to step up publicity in this regard
We hope that through joint efforts by the Government and various sectors of the community we can achieve environmental procurement increase the market demand for green products and support local recycling industries in promoting the development of a recycling economy
Third the Government has been actively promoting green projects by studying the creation of more green projects including the development of a regional air conditioning system with a traditional air conditioning system to save electricity by up to 35 %
Hong Kongs first regional cruise system is located in the Kai Tak Development Area . Together with the newly built Kai Tak Development Area system the entire Kai Tak Development Area system is expected to save more than 133 000―let me check here―133 000 tons of electricity per annum which is close to 100 000 tonnes of carbon dioxide
We will also conduct feasibility studies in new development areas such as the new development areas in Tung Chung New Town Extension and Kwu Tung North New Development Area for the construction of the regional public housing system and other new development areas so as to promote the development of green infrastructure with low energy consumption
The HKSAR Government has been actively exploring more renewable energy projects . Apart from developing solar panels and power generators in Shui Tong we will also develop renewable energy systems in suitable landfills and use the gas in landfills for power generation . We will also study installing solar power generators in landfills to promote the development of more green projects
For example the Environmental Protection Department is currently planning to launch a pilot scheme on solar power generation in the Tseung Kwan O New Territories East South landfill to lay the foundation for the development of a large solar power system in the landfill
We have also earmarked 3 billion for the provision of funds to the Government buildings venues and facilities for the setting up of small renewable energy projects and earmarked about 1.5 billion for the promotion of about 130 projects
In respect of developing waste transfer as energy efficient facilities the Government can also reduce carbon emissions from landfills and provide renewable energy
WU Lai Disposal Management Facilities T Part and Organic Rehabilitation Centres Phase 1 O Part 1 have commenced operation . We are also building and planning Organic Rehabilitation Centres Phase 2 O Part 2 and Integrated Waste Management Facilities Phase 1 I Part
These facilities can deal with waste and air waste at the same time . By converting waste into energy they can generate electricity . After the energy of the facilities has been generated by themselves the electricity can be exported to the power grid
The Environmental Protection Department will continue to strengthen cooperation with the Drainage Services Department to produce seawater energy and enhance the (over all ) fish disposal capacity in Hong Kong by using suitable sewage treatment facilities to decompose and digest fish and mud for biological gas production
Meanwhile we will promote private buildings as a green project
Just now some Members questioned whether more green buildings could be promoted . We will also step up our efforts in this regard and welcome more large enterprises and organizations to become Hong Kongs carbon neutral partners including the development of new buildings which can accommodate more EV charging facilities so as to facilitate the popularization of EVs commercial vehicles and even private cars in Hong Kong
As regards cooperation between the Mainland and Hong Kong we understand that the impact of climate change is not subject to regional restrictions as proposed by Mr KWOK when he proposed the amendment to cooperate with the Mainland in the development of green infrastructures . Therefore we have all along been working hard to promote regional cooperation in the Guangdong Hong Kong and Macao Bay Area to explore solutions to cope with climate change and environmental problems
We will continue to seize the opportunities presented by the development plan of the Greater Bay Area and the development direction of green low carbon development to strengthen our cooperation with other cities in the Greater Bay Area in developing low carbon communities develop low carbon technology and promote the exchange and cooperation in the development of low carbon products
We will also develop green finance to support investment in carbon reduction including carbon reduction projects in Hong Kong and even the Greater Bay Area
Besides Mr KWOK has proposed an amendment on developing a public transport mode of transport with low carbon emissions
Besides Mr TSE has also urged the Government to enhance the application of new energy vehicles and we will actively follow up the issue
Here I would like to share with Members the progress of the Government in the implementation of the Pilot Scheme on New Energy Recharge
Under the Pilot Scheme we plan to conduct tests on electric ferries and hybrid ferries on four of the four ferry routes and six major outlying island routes in Hong Kong
The Environment Bureau has set up an interdepartmental working group to formulate the details of the pilot scheme monitoring the progress and assessing its performance . We have also engaged a consultant from the Transport Department to assist the operator in formulating the ship design requirements for the new energy rail
We have invited an expert panel comprising local shipbuilding engineers and relevant scholars to assist in publicizing the design proposals for the new round of tender assessment
The procurement of integrated ferries by outlying island ferry routes will commence in August this year and the tests are expected to commence as early as in 2024
We plan to tender electric railways for procurement in Hong Kong within 2021 and we expect to commence the testing as soon as possible in 2023
Fourthly the Government should expeditiously implement the issuance of green retail bonds to attract investors and personal capital to support carbon neutrality . In respect of green and sustainable finance the development of green and sustainable finance will help support the financing of green carbon reduction projects and tie in with the Governments target of carbon neutrality through climate strategy
The Government will continue to work closely with relevant stakeholders including the Green and Sustainable Development Financial Intergovernmental Board led by the Hong Kong Monetary Authority HKMA and jointly promote the development of Hong Kong into an international green financial centre
President to achieve carbon neutrality need determination and policy extradition
The Steering Committee on Interdepartmental Climate Change and carbon neutrality chaired by the Chief Executive has held a number of internal meetings this year to take forward the strategy of mobilizing the entire Government to cope with climate change and implement carbon neutrality
The SAR Government will soon announce the new (blue print ) for Climate Action next month to formulate more advanced carbon reduction strategies and measures to cope with climate change
I would also like to point out that just now Mr CHAN (Han-) pan and other Members mentioned that the Government had set up an interdepartmental task force to take forward the application of new energy transport in Hong Kong with a view to generating public interest
The members of the Task Force are from different Policy Bureaux and relevant departments . They are ready to take the lead and make early preparation in terms of planning and regulation for the reception of new energy such as the application of hydrogen energy in various places in Hong Kong so as to facilitate the development of new technologies and help Hong Kong move forward
President just now each Member has raised two questions first the qualifications of the President and second the qualifications of the electors
Apart from the nationality of the United Kingdom we must examine whether Mr Andrew LEUNG still holds the right of abode in other countries or countries before determining whether he is eligible to run for the election
We have to solve the problem today . First Andrew LEUNG has withdrawn from the election . Second the Secretariat has ruled that Andrew LEUNGs eligibility to stand for election is invalid . In the absence of other candidates the Secretariat has immediately announced that Mr James TO has been elected the President of the Legislative Council
The Legislative Council Secretariat has not yet completed the procedures concerning the three persons whose oaths have not been taken by me Dr LEUNG Chung Hing and Dr YIU Chung Yim and their actions are so swift . Why do they not dare to rule that the procedures for the submission of proof of candidate eligibility by Andrew LEUNG have not been completed?
First we are allowed to take the oath again and the Secretariat will confirm that we have taken the oath
As to whether the oath is valid we should wait for the new President of the Legislative Council to make the ruling . At least this will not contravene RoP 122
I must reiterate here that the executive legislature and the judiciary are separated by three powers . The legislature has an independent constitutional status and we are representatives of public opinion . Members should resolve conflicts in the Legislative Council rather than having the court prosecuted for everything
We have the responsibility to prevent the intervention of the executive authorities and to uphold the independence of the legislature (with out ) allowing the intervention of the judiciary
Thank you President
